NDEMIC\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn to the new study on mental health and young people. Our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jen Ashton, is here with details. Doc, this study has, talk about impact on the pandemic, of the pandemic on mental health, on pediatric mental health. So, what's the big picture now?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Big picture is more confirmatory data that this, this population was vulnerable, suffered during the pandemic and continues to suffer. The most recent numbers from the CDC, when you look at pediatric mental illness, girls suffering, in some ways, more than boys. Nearly 60 percent reported feeling sad or hopeless, 30 percent considered suicide at one point. Amongst boy, almost 30 percent reported feeling sad or hopeless, and amongst LBGQT+ students, 75 percent complaining of serious mental illness issues and 25 percent of that group being suicidal. So again, when you talk about this specific population, they are in critical condition, and the country's psychiatrists have been sounding the alarm on this for some time now.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, scary to hear those numbers and something that can't be ignored, just can't ignore that.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why is this age group so vulnerable?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you have to look back on the physiology on psychology in this age group. This is a vulnerable group. Their premise for development is actually social connectiveness. So, that explains why the pandemic had such a negative impact on them. And anatomically, the prefrontal cortex, those connections are immature and subject to significant consequences based on environment. So, again, there's actual anatomy and physiology to explain why this is happening.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, if you're a parent, you're a friend, you're a loved one, you're someone that's concerned, what can you do?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First, I think you have to recognize that, we have a saying in medicine, kids are not little adults. So, in this age group, mental illness may present differently than it would amongst adults. The second thing is we have to prioritize mental health as much as we do physical health. They're on par with each other, they're on the same level. And last but not least, you have to understand that you don't have to navigate this by yourself. If you have concerns, if you have questions, ask for help. Mental health professionals, your child's pediatrician, this is their wheelhouse, and they can help.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like that, kids are not little adults.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) More care needed for them. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You bet.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's great that we can more freely talk about mental health.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's overdue, but yes, it is so important.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're getting there. We'll see you on "GMA3."\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up in our "GMA Morning Menu," as Caitlin Clark and the Iowa Hawkeyes head to the Final Four, their coach, Lisa Bluder, is going to join us live, fresh off that big rematch overnight against \uc2 LSU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also ahead, our series Before You Take the Leap, with expert advice for those nearly newlywed couples to help improve their communication.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus inside "Vanderpump Villa," a first look at reality star Lisa Vanderpump's new show. It's shot in the South of France. And Sam up there with\u160\'00\'A0Lori B.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we are, yes, we're kicking off our new series, George, it's called As Seen On Social. Lori is here to show us some products you've probably seen going viral online. This one I bought. She's the first to show you, though, this one's amazing.\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love that you have it, as well, and I'm eggstra-excited about this one, you guys. With a near perfect five-star review, this is an unlikely social media star, the Dash rapid egg cooker. It can cook eggs five different ways, hard boiled, soft boiled, poached, scrambled and even omelet style. Nothing takes longer than 16 minutes, and it costs under $20.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you guys can shop for it this morning at goodmorningamerica.com. Lori has even more of these great products coming up on "Good Morning America." It's good, right?\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's really good.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Rainy morning here in Times Square. And tomorrow on "GMA," kids and AI. It's an exclusive report. We're going to tell you what parents need to know about how to talk to their kids about AI.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240423ek420000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORM, TORNADOES, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , GAG ORDER, BOND\par \par \uc2 AB