GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN'S BREAST CANCER FIGHT, 8:18\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, HAIL, TORNADO, SNOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR CROSS-COUNTRY STORM ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS IN THE PATH OF TORNADOES, HAIL & SNOW\par \par \uc2 KEURIG, K-CUP, K-ROUNDS, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG CHANGE BREWING FOR K-CUPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEURIG INTRODUCING GREEN "K-ROUNDS"\par \par \uc2 KING, ACTOR, SON, IAN, GRIEF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REGINA KING OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON HER HEARTBREAK AFTER SON'S DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SANTA ANA WINDS\par \par \uc2 MUNN, BREAST CANCER, MASTECTOMY, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN REVEALS BREAST CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS SHARES HER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 KITCHENWARE, PURIFIER, CLEANERS, TILES, FLASHLIGHT\par \par \uc2 DUA LIPA, MURPHY, SWIFT, WEDDINGS\par \par \uc2 TRADER JOE'S, MINI TOTE, TIKTOK, TREND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRADER JOE'S MINI TOTE CRAZE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY ARE PEOPLE GOING NUTS FOR THIS MUST HAVE BAG?\par \par \uc2 FELLER, BUDGET, COOKING, RECIPES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our series about making meals on a budget. With grocery bills taking a big chunk out of families' budgets, we're showing you how to get five meal force a family of four out of $50 worth of groceries. This morning, registered dietician and cookbook author Maya Feller here to show us how she does it. Maya, thank you for coming in. Now, this is a real challenge, five meals, $50. Tell us what you bought.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 Yes, so first of all, you're absolutely right. It was really hard to get down to five meals for $50. I leaned into the center of the grocery store. We're often taught to shop the perimeter, but the center has got those shelf-stable goods for really good cost, cost saving. Also, the other thing is shop on sale and then home process. So, if there's greens on sale, then you bag them at your home and freeze them yourself. And don't be afraid of the store brands, they're fantastic, as well as those huge shopping clubs. That's where you can get your, you know, your pantry items like these. Go with your friends and family to make the most of that big shopping out, you know, going out there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DUTCH OVEN PORK CHOPS & SEASONED WHITE RICE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of good tips right there and some good meals here, as well. You start with pork chops and rice.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 Yes, absolutely. So, pork chops and rice, super delicious. Again, I've got pantry staples like white rice, apple cider vinegar. I'm using veggie bouillon instead of the bouillon that, you know, or the broth because I don't want to pay for the water. So, I'm using the cubes. You're going to see some of these items showing up in the recipes over and over again because that's a great way to really lean into those cost savings.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE FISH WITH COLLARDS, RED PEPPERS & SEASONED RICE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One of them is rice. You have it here with frozen fish.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 That's exactly right. So, I'm using frozen fish because it's less expensive. You can also use tinned fish. George, you know, really what I say to people is this is a scaffolding. Swap in and out whatever you have access to and whatever's least expensive for you at the moment. If you have greens that are on sale, whatever leafy green you have in your area, go for that. You'll notice again that onions and scallions are showing up multiple times in each recipe, boosting that flavor.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VEGGIE MAC & CHEESE WITH BROCCOLI STALK SLAW\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kids will always go for mac and cheese. What do you have here?\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, this is my favorite. We are feature a bag of frozen veggies. Do not fear the frozen veggies. They're picked at the top of the growing season, so they're just as nutritious. Again, you're going to see good pantry staples showing up here, really leaning into that cost savings. And I did something so interesting. I use the stalk of the broccoli to make a slaw. Usually that gets discarded.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh wow, okay.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 But I've upcycled it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAVORY SKILLET CORNBREAD & BAKED CHICKEN\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, and you've done something interesting here, as well. It's a skillet dish, and you used drumsticks. Of course, they are less expensive.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 Exactly. Drumsticks are less expensive and full of flavor. That's really what we want to do. We want to lean into flavor so you're going back to your table time and time again. Here, I've also got more pantry staples, things like flour. I'm encouraging folks to bake. This skillet cornbread has a little bit of zucchini, we're seeing also some, you know, scallions again and the onion, things showing back up over and over, but it's really flavorful and you're saving.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAYA'S ITAL STEW\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And finally, I know this is one of your favorite recipes.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 Yes, it absolutely is. So, this is ital stew. Usually this is, like, tons and tons of veggies. I say to people, "Whatever you have in your home, toss in there." Again, the veggie bouillon is showing up. I'm also using the onion and the scallion again. I've got dried goods from my pantry, like bay leaf and also thyme, to bring that flavor up. And then, you know, it's really warm and comforting.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was a challenge, but you really did it. Maya, thank you for coming in today.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (REGISTERED DIETICIAN)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can get the full shopping list and all these recipes on goodmorningamerica.com. And coming up, one man's inspiring weight loss journey, how he lost more than 200 pounds without drugs. He has some advice for others, and he's here live.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240403ek3e0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, TORNADOES, STORM\par \par \uc2 WINTER STORM, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER BLAST SLAMMING WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW, WIND & AVALANCHE ALERTS FROM CALIFORNIA TO COLORADO\par \par \uc2 BOEING, AMERICAN AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN AIRLINES FLIGHT MAKES EMERGENCY LANDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST INCIDENT INVOLVING BOEING JET AMID GROWING SCRUTINY\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, GALLAGHER, ROY, CHEW, CRENSHAW, TIKTOK, BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE PASSES BILL TARGETING TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOCIAL PLATFORM COULD FACE U.S. BAN UNLESS SOLD BY CHINESE PARENT COMPANY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , WILLIS, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURPRISE RULING IN GEORGIA ELECTION INTERFERENCE CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE DISMISSES SIX COUNTS AGAINST TRUMP & CO-DEFENDANTS\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, HAITI, ALBOM, DOLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO RESCUE AMERICANS STRANDED IN HAITI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARINES SENT TO PROTECT U.S. EMBASSY AMID GROWING CHAOS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILY DOLLAR & DOLLAR TREE TO CLOSE 1,000 STORES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOWNSIZING RAISES CONCERNS ABOUT FOOD INSECURITY\par \par \uc2 DOGS, STRESS, BURROWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR BRAIN ON PUPPIES!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT A NEW STUDY REVEALS ABOUT DOGS & OUR HAPPINESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY, SAND HOLE, COLLAPSE, ACCIDENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARENTS BREAK THEIR SILENCE ABOUT BEACH TRAGEDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7-YEAR-OLD KILLED AFTER SAND HOLE CAVED IN ON HER & BROTHER\par \par \uc2 HEUERMANN, ELLERUP, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECTED LONG ISLAND SERIAL KILLER'S ESTRANGED WIFE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS SHE IS GIVING HIM "THE BENEFIT OF THE DOUBT" IN NEW STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2 , AMAZON PHARMACY, MARTIN, SELTZER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIME TIME FOR WEIGHT LOSS?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON TEAMS UP WITH \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2  TO DELIVER POPULAR WEIGHT LOSS & DIABETES DRUGS\par \par \uc2 'NSYNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN'S BREAST CANCER FIGHT, 8:18\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, HAIL, TORNADO, SNOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR CROSS-COUNTRY STORM ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS IN THE PATH OF TORNADOES, HAIL & SNOW\par \par \uc2 KEURIG, K-CUP, K-ROUNDS, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG CHANGE BREWING FOR K-CUPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEURIG INTRODUCING GREEN "K-ROUNDS"\par \par \uc2 KING, ACTOR, SON, IAN, GRIEF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REGINA KING OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON HER HEARTBREAK AFTER SON'S DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SANTA ANA WINDS\par \par \uc2 MUNN, BREAST CANCER, MASTECTOMY, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN REVEALS BREAST CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS SHARES HER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 KITCHENWARE, PURIFIER, CLEANERS, TILES, FLASHLIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "GMA," live from Times Square.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240403ek3e0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN REVEALS BREAST CANCER FIGHT ACTRESS SHARES HER DIAGNOSIS\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, TORNADOES, STORM\par \par \uc2 WINTER STORM, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER BLAST SLAMMING WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW, WIND & AVALANCHE ALERTS FROM CALIFORNIA TO COLORADO\par \par \uc2 BOEING, AMERICAN AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN AIRLINES FLIGHT MAKES EMERGENCY LANDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST INCIDENT INVOLVING BOEING JET AMID GROWING SCRUTINY\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, GALLAGHER, ROY, CHEW, CRENSHAW, TIKTOK, BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE PASSES BILL TARGETING TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOCIAL PLATFORM COULD FACE U.S. BAN UNLESS SOLD BY CHINESE PARENT COMPANY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , WILLIS, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURPRISE RULING IN GEORGIA ELECTION INTERFERENCE CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE DISMISSES SIX COUNTS AGAINST TRUMP & CO-DEFENDANTS\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, HAITI, ALBOM, DOLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO RESCUE AMERICANS STRANDED IN HAITI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARINES SENT TO PROTECT U.S. EMBASSY AMID GROWING CHAOS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILY DOLLAR & DOLLAR TREE TO CLOSE 1,000 STORES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOWNSIZING RAISES CONCERNS ABOUT FOOD INSECURITY\par \par \uc2 DOGS, STRESS, BURROWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR BRAIN ON PUPPIES!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT A NEW STUDY REVEALS ABOUT DOGS & OUR HAPPINESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY, SAND HOLE, COLLAPSE, ACCIDENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARENTS BREAK THEIR SILENCE ABOUT BEACH TRAGEDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7-YEAR-OLD KILLED AFTER SAND HOLE CAVED IN ON HER & BROTHER\par \par \uc2 HEUERMANN, ELLERUP, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECTED LONG ISLAND SERIAL KILLER'S ESTRANGED WIFE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS SHE IS GIVING HIM "THE BENEFIT OF THE DOUBT" IN NEW STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2 , AMAZON PHARMACY, MARTIN, SELTZER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIME TIME FOR WEIGHT LOSS?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON TEAMS UP WITH \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2  TO DELIVER POPULAR WEIGHT LOSS & DIABETES DRUGS\par \par \uc2 'NSYNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN'S BREAST CANCER FIGHT, 8:18\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, HAIL, TORNADO, SNOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR CROSS-COUNTRY STORM ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS IN THE PATH OF TORNADOES, HAIL & SNOW\par \par \uc2 KEURIG, K-CUP, K-ROUNDS, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG CHANGE BREWING FOR K-CUPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEURIG INTRODUCING GREEN "K-ROUNDS"\par \par \uc2 KING, ACTOR, SON, IAN, GRIEF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REGINA KING OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON HER HEARTBREAK AFTER SON'S DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SANTA ANA WINDS\par \par \uc2 MUNN, BREAST CANCER, MASTECTOMY, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN REVEALS BREAST CANCER FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS SHARES HER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A message now from Olivia Munn. She reveal that she was diagnosed with an aggressive form of breast cancer and had a double mastectomy. Morgan Norwood has the story.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 She's known for her roles on "The Daily Show" and "The Newsroom."\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "THE NEWSROOM"\par \par \uc2 ACTOR ("THE NEWSROOM")\par \par \uc2 How did that happen?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA MUNN ("THE NEWSROOM")\par \par \uc2 By spending roughly 30 years being dumb as a bag of doorknobs.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But this morning, Olivia Munn is opening up about a real-life battle of her own, breast cancer. In an emotional post on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , the actress sharing this video and a series of photos, getting candid about her diagnosis and 10-month-long treatment journey, undergoing a double mastectomy, writing, "I have had four surgeries, so many days have been in bed I can't even count." Munn revealing it started in February of 2023 with a proactive genetic test for 90 different cancer genes. She writes, "I tested negative for all, including BRCA, the most well-known breast cancer gene." Yet two months later, the actress says she was diagnosed on luminal B on both breasts, a rare and aggressive type of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA SHEPHERD (OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 Now, for luminal B cancer, this is a specific type of breast cancer, and it only makes up about 10 percent of breast cancer diagnoses. And it expresses a certain protein, also known as HER-2 protein, and the HER-2 protein actually quickens the growth of cancer cells.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Munn sharing that she wouldn't have discovered the cancer for another year, during her routine mammogram, if it wasn't for her OB/GYN, who decided calculate her breast cancer risk assessment score followed by an MRI and biopsy.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN NORWOOD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Munn is now encouraging other women to talk with their doctor about the breast cancer risk assessment tool. So, here's what it is. It looks at things, including your age, age of first menstruation, family history, prior breast biopsy history. But here's the thing. It cannot tell you whether or not you will get breast cancer. Experts caution you not to panic. They add that routine mammograms and self-breast examinations, guys, are still a great tool in terms of detection.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It really is.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of great information. Thank you, Morgan. Let's go to Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very important conversation with your doctor.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240403ek3e0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:00 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, TORNADOES, STORM\par \par \uc2 WINTER STORM, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER BLAST SLAMMING WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW, WIND & AVALANCHE ALERTS FROM CALIFORNIA TO COLORADO\par \par \uc2 BOEING, AMERICAN AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN AIRLINES FLIGHT MAKES EMERGENCY LANDING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST INCIDENT INVOLVING BOEING JET AMID GROWING SCRUTINY\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, GALLAGHER, ROY, CHEW, CRENSHAW, TIKTOK, BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE PASSES BILL TARGETING TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOCIAL PLATFORM COULD FACE U.S. BAN UNLESS SOLD BY CHINESE PARENT COMPANY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , WILLIS, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURPRISE RULING IN GEORGIA ELECTION INTERFERENCE CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE DISMISSES SIX COUNTS AGAINST TRUMP & CO-DEFENDANTS\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, HAITI, ALBOM, DOLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO RESCUE AMERICANS STRANDED IN HAITI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARINES SENT TO PROTECT U.S. EMBASSY AMID GROWING CHAOS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILY DOLLAR & DOLLAR TREE TO CLOSE 1,000 STORES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOWNSIZING RAISES CONCERNS ABOUT FOOD INSECURITY\par \par \uc2 DOGS, STRESS, BURROWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR BRAIN ON PUPPIES!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT A NEW STUDY REVEALS ABOUT DOGS & OUR HAPPINESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY, SAND HOLE, COLLAPSE, ACCIDENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARENTS BREAK THEIR SILENCE ABOUT BEACH TRAGEDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7-YEAR-OLD KILLED AFTER SAND HOLE CAVED IN ON HER & BROTHER\par \par \uc2 HEUERMANN, ELLERUP, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECTED LONG ISLAND SERIAL KILLER'S ESTRANGED WIFE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS SHE IS GIVING HIM "THE BENEFIT OF THE DOUBT" IN NEW STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2 , AMAZON PHARMACY, MARTIN, SELTZER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIME TIME FOR WEIGHT LOSS?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON TEAMS UP WITH \uc2 ELI LILLY\uc2  TO DELIVER POPULAR WEIGHT LOSS & DIABETES DRUGS\par \par \uc2 'NSYNC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AHEAD ON GMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OLIVIA MUNN'S BREAST CANCER FIGHT, 8:18\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning America. It's 8:00.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR CROSS-COUNTRY STORM\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A major cross-country storm on the move. Overnight, multiple tornados touched down as the heartland braces for more severe weather. Ginger tracking it all as the storm head east.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOVE OVER K-CUPS?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Change brewing for your morning cup of coffee, why \uc2 Keurig\uc2  is saying, "Move over K-Cups" and what it means if you already have one of their machines at home.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REGINA KING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Regina King in her first TV interview since the sudden death of her beloved son, Ian. What has this time span, these last two years, been like for you?\par \par \uc2 REGINA KING ("SHIRLEY")\par \par \uc2 I'm a different person now than I was January 19. I understand that grief is love that has no place to go.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 How she's healing and how she's carrying on his legacy, as she gets ready to step into a powerful new role.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER RISK ASSESSMENT SCORE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Olivia Munn revealing her breast cancer diagnosis, a 10-month-long treatment journey, undergoing a double mastectomy and the test she says to ask your doctor about that Oliva says saved her life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOTE FRENZY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Fashion's newest it bag.\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA CONTENT PROVIDER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I got it. It's so cute.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Why TikTokkers and shoppers are obsessed with Trader Joe's $3 mini tote bag, with some now paying hundreds to buy it online.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEIGHT LOSS JOURNEY\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And one man's inspiring health journey, his desire to start a family with his wife motivating him to lose more than 200 pounds.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH CALDWELL (LOST MORE THAN 200 POUNDS)\par \par \uc2 After coming home from the doctor's office, I was terrified.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The diet he followed and how he's telling his story to motivate others\u160\'00\'A0as we say good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And we do say good morning, America. And I'm really looking forward to sharing with you that conversation with Regina King. She took some much needed time away after losing her son, Ian. They had such a strong connection, even matching tattoos that read unconditional love. And she says she knows what that means now more than ever. And she has a powerful, some powerful thoughts on grieving.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 What she told you about motherhood and timing, that resonated so much with me, and I think it will really resonate with people out there, as well. Also ahead, dietitian Maya Feller is here with easy and delicious recipes to make your family meals for just $50.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240403ek3e0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HISTORIC WOMEN'S CLINIC VISIT VP HARRIS: TRUMP "ARCHITECT" OF "HEALTH CARE CRISIS"\b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, STORM, FLIGHTS, STUBBLEFIELD, STARK\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 CLASSIFIED DOCUMENT, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, TRUMP, WILLIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE DENIES TRUMP MOTION TO DISMISS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUBWAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TERRIFYING SUBWAY SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, ELECTION, BIDEN, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC VISIT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to the race for president. And tonight, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the first vice president to ever visit a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  that provides abortions, telling voters, women's reproductive rights, abortion rights, are on the ballot this presidential election. And tonight here, what's happened, even in conservative states, when the issue of abortion has been put on the ballot after Roe went down. Here's ABC's Rachel Scott tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC WOMEN'S CLINIC VISIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VP HARRIS: TRUMP "ARCHITECT" OF "HEALTH CARE CRISIS"\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  making history as the first vice president to visit a women's health clinic where abortions are provided, a sign of just how central this issue has become to the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And walking through this clinic, that's what I saw, are people who have dedicated their lives to the profession of providing health care in a safe place that gives people dignity. And I think we should all want that for each other.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESTRICTED ACCESS TO ABORTION SINCE ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Twenty-one states have banned or restricted access to abortion since the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade. The Minnesota Planned Parenthood clinic Harris visited says they've seen a nearly 100% increase in patients coming in from other parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 How dare these elected leaders believe they are in a better position to tell women what they need? To tell women what is in their best interest? We have to be a nation that trusts women.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PASSED AMENDMENTS PROTECTING ACCESS TO ABORTION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats keenly aware voters have moved to protect abortion rights in all six states where it has appeared on the ballot since Roe vs. Wade was overturned, including conservative states like Kansas, Kentucky, and Ohio. In his State of the Union speech, President Biden noting that in the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe, the justices themselves wrote, women are not without political power.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You're about to realize just how much you were right about that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President is counting on abortion to drive voters to the polls this November.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS ON BALLOT IN NOVEMBER\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As many as 14 states could have measures related to abortion or reproductive rights on the ballot. Among them, Nevada, Pennsylvania, and Arizona. Battleground states that could decide this election.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As the nation's first female vice president, Harris has been the leading voice on reproductive rights for the Biden administration. David, she's been going state to state, hoping to rally Democratic voters around this issue. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott covering the race for president for us. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020240318ek3e00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SZA GETS BREAST IMPLANTS REMOVED GRAMMY WINNER OPENS UP ABOUT HER PERSONAL DECISION\b0\par\par\uc2 1,281 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BOEING, BARNETT, WHITAKER, MAINTENANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW CONCERNS OVER BOEING QUALITY CONTROL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPORT: COMPANY HAS FAILED DOZENS OF AUDITS\par \par \uc2 CRASH, HUFFMAN, REDSHAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY ILLINOIS SCHOOL BUS CRASH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THREE CHILDREN AMONG FIVE KILLED AFTER SEMI & BUS COLLIDE\par \par \uc2 HUR, BIDEN, CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES CAPITOL HILL TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPECIAL COUNSEL TO TESTIFY ABOUT BIDEN CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, WINDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY WINDS BATTER NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TREES DOWN, POWER OUTAGES & DAMAGED HOMES\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, BAN, TRAN, TRUONG, KRISHNAMOORTHI, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER TIKTOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESS THREATENING TO BAN SOCIAL MEDIA GIANT FROM U.S.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW NUMBERS EXPECTED TO SHOW IMPROVEMENT, BUT PRICES STILL STUBBORNLY HIGH\par \par \uc2 CARMEN, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING ERIC CARMEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "HUNGRY EYES" & "ALL BY MYSELF" SINGER PASSES AWAY AT 74\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 PRINCESS KATE, SHIP\par \par \uc2 SECURITY, PRASAD, LEXIS NEXIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COULD YOUR CAR BE SPYING ON YOU?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPORT: DATA ON DRIVING HABITS SHARED WITH INSURANCE COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, SPRING BREAK, DEALS, ROSEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DOCUMENTS, HUR, TESTIMONY, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES CAPITOL HILL TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPECIAL COUNSEL TO TESTIFY RE: BIDEN INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 STRAIN, MISSING, COLLEGE, VISIT, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH FOR MISSING STUDENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 22-YEAR-OLD DISAPPEARED DURING TRIP TO NASHVILLE\par \par \uc2 AIRBNB, PRIVACY, SURVEILLANCE, BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRBNB'S MAJOR NEW PRIVACY PUSH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY BANS INDOOR SURVEILLANCE CAMERAS\par \par \uc2 SZA, BREAST IMPLANTS, REMOVAL, CANCER, SINGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SZA GETS BREAST IMPLANTS REMOVED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRAMMY WINNER OPENS UP ABOUT HER PERSONAL DECISION\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are back now on "GMA" with our cover story. Grammy Award-winning artist SZA sharing what led her to reconsider her breast implants and get them removed. Erielle Reshef is here with the story. Good morning, Erielle.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Rebecca. SZA says that she didn't know enough about the potential risks of getting breast implants, but with a family of history of cancer, she grew increasingly concerned when she, herself, began feeling off.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's known for (inaudible) hits like "Kill Bill" and "Broken Clock." Now, four-time Grammy Award-winning musical artist SZA opening up about her very personal decision to remove her breast implants.\par \par \uc2 SZA (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 She was telling me all these things about, like, my testosterone levels and PCOS and all these things and, like, I'm, was plagued with but didn't really know what was actually happening to me.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 34-year-old sharing her story on the "SHE MD" podcast hosted by former fashion designer Mary Alice Haney, and OB/GYN, Dr. Thais Aliabadi, whose patients include SZA and other stars. SZA says her mother had breast cancer, and her doctor says the star had previously dealt with noncancerous lumps in her breast.\par \par \uc2 DR. THAIS ALIABADI (OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 You had a fibroadenoma, which is a benign, you know, breast mass that a lot of young girls have.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While not linked with breast cancer, doctors say implants can come with some other potential risks. SZA admitting she was supposed to consult Dr. Aliabadi before going under the knife.\par \par \uc2 SZA (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 You know, I was supposed to see her before I had my breast surgery, but I didn't. And I, like, snuck and got it anyways.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) SZA says her implants did not last long.\par \par \uc2 SZA (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 So, basically, I put them in. They ended up hurting me. I got way too much scar tissue. And I didn't feel good, and it was painful. So, I took them out.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, she's feeling good and heading back to the doctor soon.\par \par \uc2 SZA (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I just scheduled it thanks to Dr. A's office. I just scheduled it.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is an important point. While most data indicates women with and without implants are diagnosed for breast cancer at similar rates, it's important to note that women with breast implants do require additional images taken during mammograms to improve the sensitivity of the test, so, important to be transparent with your practitioner.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, glad that she's doing well.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it's important.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for sharing that with us, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST IMPLANT RISKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT ARE THE POTENTIAL HEALTH CONCERNS?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Robin, and we want to bring in our medical correspondent, Dr. Darien Sutton, to really break this down for us. So, lots of women turn to breast implants for aesthetic reasons, but there's also the reconstructive reasons. Some women do it after breast cancer, for example. There are risks here. Let's break those down.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, those risks are important to consider. Before, and while preparing for this, I consulted with my friends in breast surgery and reconstructive surgery, and there are some important risks that they always discuss with every patient. And here are some of them. Number one, persistent pain, as in SZA's case. Obviously, that is a red flag and a concerning risk factor, excuse me, symptom. Also scarring or contracture, the rare risk of rupture or deflation. And then what we're talking about today, the rare risk of certain types of cancer, such as squamous cell carcinoma, as well as lymphoma, that are associated with, more often than not, older form of breast implants that are no longer used but still an important risk to consider.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how about the material of the breast implant itself, silicone versus saline?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a common discussion point, especially among patients making that decision. Overall, when you look at the data, they're equally safe. Both have a silicone shell, so there's really no way to avoid silicone. For those gel-filled silicone implants, in the case of rupture, that might be more difficult to repair, and that might lead someone down one decision versus another.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what we heard from SZA is that she said she felt pain. What should people who have breast implants be thinking about?\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think what's most important to take away from here is even if you're in that position, it's not a call to remove breast implants. They are generally safe and effective measures for, regardless of what decision a woman has. But in terms of being aware, that's really important. Number one, understanding any new symptoms, skin changes, swelling, pain. That, or those are symptoms you should bring up with your doctor. Also keeping your records. Breast implants, as well as any device implanted into the body, has a device card that has the information, as far as the lot number and the serial number. You might need that for future needs. And also, call ahead before your mammograms, making sure that the imager has experience imaging breast implants. And then, if needed, you might want to consider other forms of imaging, like MRI or ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That medical device card, though, that's important for everyone with a medical device.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's so important, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Dr. Darien Sutton, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240402ek3c0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STATE OF THE UNION ADDRESS PRES BIDEN DELIVERS SOTU ADDRESS; POSSIBLY MOST PIVOTAL SPEECH OF HIS CAREER\b0\par\par\uc2 2,269 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 SOTU, BIDEN, COX, KARSAN, NEUTRA, CAMPAIGN, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for joining us. We begin tonight with President Biden and the State of the Union Address, making his case to the American people for four more years. This as voters express concerns about his age and the nation's future. Let's go right to senior congressional correspondent Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Juju, this was a fiery, at times combative State of the Union address. The President keenly aware that he is delivering this speech in a critical election year.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He did not mention \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by name, but he did take him on as well as the Republicans directly there in that chamber many times throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President going head-to-head, sparring on several key issues from immigration to social security to Medicare.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, House Speaker Mike Johnson wanted there to be a level of decorum, of civility in that chamber. Well, that did not happen. Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  kicking it off, calling the President a liar, repeatedly booing the President. Also, one key focus for President Biden tonight, the issue of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The bottom line, this felt a lot like a campaign speech in this critical election year, Juju.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott on Capitol Hill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) More on President Biden's hourlong State of the Union address. The big question, did he do enough to win over voters?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A high-stakes moment for the President...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM MCFARLAND (SERGEANT-AT-ARMS OF THE HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES)\par \par \uc2 The President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...coming on the heels of super Tuesday and now likely facing a rematch with former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this November.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's because of you that tonight we can proudly say the state of our union is strong and getting stronger.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With tensions continuing to boil over both domestically and globally, many Americans question whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is fit to lead the country for four more years. The President reassuring the nation he's up for the job.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I've been told I am too old. Whether young or old, I've always been known, I've always known what endures. I've known our North Star. The very idea of America is that we're all created equal, deserves to be treated equally throughout our lives. (sic)\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: TOO OLD TO BE AN EFFECTIVE PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The latest polling showing 47% of Americans believe the 81-year-old is too old to run, versus 21% who think Trump is too old at 77. Throughout his speech, Biden never once mentioning Trump by name, but referring to him several times.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 My predecessor and some of you here seek to bury the truth about January 6th. I will not do that. This is a moment to speak the truth and to bury the lies. Here's the simple truth. You can't love your country only when you win.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN 31%: TRUMP 43%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President speaking about the economy, something polls show Biden is underperforming on.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I inherited an economy that was on the brink. Now our economy's literally the envy of the world. Fifteen million new jobs in just three years. A record.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: IMMIGRATION/BORDER SECURITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: 26%: TRUMP 44%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden addressing immigration and border security, another issue that polling shows he trails Trump on by double digits.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 From my first day in office, I introduced a comprehensive bill to fix our immigration system. Take a look at it, it has all these and more. Secure the border, provide a pathway to citizenship for DREAMERS and so much more.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN 37%: TRUMP 28%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden also discussing reproductive rights, a key issue under his presidency and in his campaign for reelection. Biden polling at 37% favorability versus Trump's 28%.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 If you, the American people, send me a Congress that supports the right to choose, I promise you I'll restore Roe v. Wade as the law of the land again.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One of the guests in the First Lady's box, Kate Cox.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 What her family got through should've never happened as well.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Dallas mother who sued the state of Texas after she was denied an emergency abortion by the state's Supreme Court back in December. My colleague, Rachel Scott, spoke with Kate and her ob/gyn, Dr. Damla Karsan, ahead of the address.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What does it mean to you to be invited as a guest?\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (DENIED EMERGENCY ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 I'm so grateful that the State of the Union is shining a light on this very important issue.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Because Texas has a ban on abortion, the mother of two had to flee her home state in order to terminate her pregnancy in her second trimester after discovering during an early screening that her fetus had a fatal anomaly, Trisomy 18.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're told that your baby has virtually no chance of survival?\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (DENIED EMERGENCY ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. So excited for our third baby. It's the hardest news I've ever received. And it's hard to walk around pregnant with a belly. Some people ask you, you know, "When are you due? What are you having? Do you have a name?"\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her uterus was at risk of rupturing if she were to continue to carry to term. It could've risked her health and cost her the ability to have a baby in the future.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAMLA KARSAN (KATE'S OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 Kate has had two prior c-sections and so, really wasn't a candidate to, to deliver vaginally. So, she would require another c-section. Even inducing labor early still has risks when you have a scar on your uterus.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even though she was getting sicker by the day, continuing to carry a nonviable pregnancy, she still couldn't access an emergency abortion in the state of Texas?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAMLA KARSAN (KATE'S OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 Not legally.\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (DENIED EMERGENCY ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 It's hard. Because it was never a matter of if I would have to say goodbye to my baby, but when. She survived the birth, she could live maybe a week maximum. It was best case scenario. How many people have to speak up? How many women have to tell their most painful journey publicly before something changes?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you want to see changed?\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (DENIED EMERGENCY ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 I hope that in the future, families will get to have this conversation with their doctor. I hope that the law will be different to allow that, so that women and families can get the care that they need in their journeys.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Biden campaign looks at the issue of reproductive rights as a galvanizing issue. It was important to have someone like Kate Cox at the address tonight because it put a real face to the fallout of the overturning of Roe v. Wade. This is not something that's happening in the abstract.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also in the house tonight, Orna and Ronen Neutra, among the many family members of American hostages in Gaza, invited by both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA (MOTHER OF OMER NEUTRA)\par \par \uc2 The release of the hostages is a bipartisan issue and we appreciate the commitment on behalf of congress people on both sides.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Neutra's son, 22-year-old Omer, grew up on Long Island, New York, and is the grandson of holocaust survivors. He was temporarily living in Israel, serving in the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 , when taken hostage on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA (MOTHER OF OMER NEUTRA)\par \par \uc2 He always a smile on his face. He brings the, you know, people together. He loves people.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Members of both parties wearing yellow ribbons, showing bipartisan support to bring the hostages home.\par \par \uc2 RONEN NEUTRA (FATHER OF OMER NEUTRA)\par \par \uc2 All we care about right now is to see that we get our son back. And, you know, solving the issues in the Middle East can be done as, as a further step.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President stating his belief in a two party state between Israelis and Palestinians as the path forward and announcing an emergency military mission to build a port on Gaza's coastline to help get humanitarian aid into the territory.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Israel must do its part. Israel must allow more aid into Gaza and ensure humanitarian workers aren't caught in the crossfire.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 30,000 GAZANS KILLED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2 MILLION+ EXPERIENCING FOOD INSECURITY\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Since the start of the war, there have been reportedly over 30,000 Gazans killed and over two million experiencing food insecurity.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 To the leadership of - Israel, I say this. Humanitarian assistance cannot be a secondary consideration or a bargaining chip. Protecting and saving innocent lives has to be priority.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight's address coming in the wake of several primaries already showing warning signs for Biden in part over his support of the war. Nearly 20% of Democratic voters in last month's Michigan primary didn't choose Biden in protest of his handling of the Israel/\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 PALESTINIAN AMERICAN (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 The 30,000 Palestinians that have been lost, that have been killed, will no longer be back and there's nothing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can say that will make me change my mind and vote for him.\par \par \uc2 PALESTINIAN AMERICAN (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hasn't done enough to earn my vote and not done enough to stop, to stop the war, stop the massacre.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In a state like Minnesota, a state that Democrats want to win, we saw 30% of folks who cast ballots in the Democratic primary cast ballots for someone who was not President Biden. Many of those votes going to the uncommitted option and that is something that the Biden campaign is going to have to contend with come November.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden also highlighting his support for Ukraine as it fends off Russia's invasion more than two years into that conflict.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 If anybody in this room thinks Putin will stop at Ukraine, I assure you, he will not.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And emphasizing that House Republicans need to move forward in sending aid for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 My message to President Putin, who I've known for a long time, is simple. We will not walk away. We will not bow down.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Republican response came from 42-year-old Alabama Senator Katie Britt, a rising star in the GOP.\par \par \uc2 REP. KATIE BRITT (REP) (ALABAMA)\par \par \uc2 President Biden just doesn't get it. He's out of touch.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her response hitting on border security...\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (DENIED EMERGENCY ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 We know that President Biden didn't just create this border crisis. He invited it with 94 executive actions in his first 100 days. President Biden's border policies are a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...and the economy.\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (DENIED EMERGENCY ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 The American people are scraping by. While President Biden proudly proclaims that Bidenomics is working. Goodness, y'all. Bless his heart. We know better.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think Senator Britt response and her remarks really showcased what the GOP strategy is going to be, particularly around the issue of immigration.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden ending his remarks with his promise to America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Now, I'll always be president for all Americans. You're the reason we've never been more optimistic about our future than I am now. So, let's build the future together. Let's remember who we are. We are the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, of course, "GMA" will have more reaction to the President's address in the morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAITLIN CLARK\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, Caitlin Clark. One-on-one with the college basketball sensation now moving into the next phase of her electrifying career.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN CLARK (ALL-TIME NCAA SCORING LEADER)\par \par \uc2 Right over here, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's where you (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020240312ek3700002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPER TUESDAY RESULTS COMING IN FROM 16 STATES\b0\par\par\uc2 10,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SUPER TUESDAY, POLLS, TRUMP, BIDEN, HALEY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, and thanks for joining us on this Super Tuesday. Our special coverage of the Presidential primary contest tonight, we're watching 16 states tonight. It is now 10:00 PM in the east, and polls have closed in 12 of the states where Republicans are voting today. Just look at the map at this hour. Republican voters in 15 states casting their ballots tonight. 865 delegates up for grabs. 36% of the total delegates at stake in the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 STATES, 865 DELEGATES, 36% OF TOTAL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It has been a big night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , continuing his march to the nomination. Every state lit up there, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won tonight. Defeating \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in every state so far with voting still under way at this hour in Utah, Alaska, and California. On the Democratic side, tonight 15 states and American Samoa are reporting results. Not a surprise here. President Biden handily winning all 13 that have been called so far. Back to the Republican primaries at this hour, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  tonight. Let's take a look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MINNESOTA REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Based on analysis of the vote, ABC News can now project \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will win the Republican primary in Minnesota. That result just in tonight. Also just in, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won the Republican primary in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSACHUSETTS REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLORADO REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALABAMA REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARKANSAS REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRGINIA REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH CAROLINA REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OKLAHOMA REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also won in Colorado tonight. Trump wins Alabama. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beats \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in Texas. Trump wins the Arkansas Republican primary. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins the Republican primary in Virginia. He also won in North Carolina tonight. He beat \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENNESSEE REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAINE REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VERMONT REPUBLICAN PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also wins in Tennessee, the Republican primary there. Trump also wins the Republican primary in Maine. Still outstanding at this hour, a very close Republican primary in Vermont. So as you can see already, the (inaudible) headline of the night, the unmistakable signal that the general election is now essentially under way. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and President Biden on the verge of an official rematch. And if the election were held today, what does that mean? Who is ahead? Where does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  think he can win this? Where does President Biden think he can pull this off again?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And is there any chance that it could be someone other than Trump and Biden come November? We're going to dive into all the questions you have at home. But first, it is still Super Tuesday. Let's not get ahead of ourselves yet. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with a sweep so far. Let's get to Rachel Scott. She is live inside Mar-a-Lago tonight, where the former president is at this hour. And Rachel, I imagine the Trump campaign is very happy tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ecstatic, David. This is shaping up to be a huge night for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Heading into Super Tuesday, Trump's senior campaign adviser told me it was not a question of whether or not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be dominant tonight, it was a question of whether or not he could have a clean sweep, go 15 for 15 in the states that are voting today. Already ABC News projecting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won at least 10 of the states voting today. So we know the former president is here at Mar-a-Lago. He has been monitoring these results closely. We are told he does have his eye on Vermont. He is watching how close that primary is there. But by the end of the night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's goal is to nearly lock up this Republican nomination, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. We'll track Vermont right here with you as we're on the air. Rachel Scott beginning our prime time coverage here. Rachel, thank you. Of course, the question, what about \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 ? Does she stay in the race? Let's get right to ABC's Eva Pilgrim, she's in Charleston, South Carolina tonight with the Haley campaign. What are you hearing, Eva?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, that's the big question that we are asking here tonight. You might notice I'm actually standing outside. That's because \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is not having a public watch party for tonight's event. She is privately watching these results come in. And I don't have to tell you just how unusual that is. Most cases you will see those candidates having these big parties. But not having a party like this takes the pressure off forcing her to come out and say something to a crowd so she can wait and make that decision about when she wants to speak. And we are waiting to see what that is.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, we have talked with her repeatedly about what does a path forward for her look like. And she was very candid with us. She is good at math. She is an accountant. And she is not delusional about what it would take and what she needs. She knew it was an uphill climb. She said she was staying in this until she wasn't competitive anymore. When the delegate math doesn't make sense, she would then exit. So we'll have to see what she takes with these results tonight. But you can't ignore the numbers of people, a meaningful number of Republicans that are voting for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's why she has stayed in this race to this point. She has promised to stay in until Super Tuesday. There are no other events planned outside of this point. We don't know anything about her schedule beyond now. And we are waiting to see what her camp says, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Eva Pilgrim with \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's campaign tonight. Thank you. Let's get to our political director Rick Klein over at the big board where he always is on these big political nights. Rick, Eva makes a good point. We'll get into \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's supporters here in a moment. But it really does appear that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is on a march to his third Republican nomination here.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's exactly right, David. We saw a bunch of states where \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  hoped to be pretty competitive tonight. That just isn't the case at all. Remember how well she did in New Hampshire. She was looking at the states the neighbor New Hampshire, Maine, Massachusetts, and Vermont as possibilities. Maine looking like it is an absolute blowout, a 45, maybe 50 point win there. And even Massachusetts with all of the Democrats and the independents in that state, a big, wide victory. Now, Vermont, as we talked about, is maybe the one bright spot for her. At this hour she is up by - a little more than 1400 votes. That's a decent night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIMARIES/CAUCUSES\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If she is able to hold on to that, at least she walks away with one win. And maybe it's a small signal of the skepticism outside there in the Republican party. But overall, I just ran the numbers on this, David, and it's striking. We see how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is marching toward that magic number. He has 398 new delegates tonight. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  only 10. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is well on his way to getting where he needs to go. It's probably not going to happen tonight or even next week. But within a week or two, even if Haley stays in the race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO NOMINATION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is no question this has been a major night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Eva Pilgrim, though, brings up the point of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's supporters around the country. When you start to get 20% of the vote, 30%, obviously you don't win the delegates, you don't win the state, but those are significant numbers when it comes to the question that was asked in some of the exit polling about whether or not you would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come November. And certainly the Trump campaign, Rick, is aware of these numbers that she is posting.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, no question. And I've seen this same kind of trend playing out right now tonight in Virginia. This is a state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  we know is winning tonight. But he's not winning everywhere. And there's parts of the state right around the DC area in particular where \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  is actually winning by a pretty wide margin. Fairfax County a 17 point lead. In Arlington just outside of DC, we're talking about almost a 50 point lead. Those are the kind of voters that a Republican needs to keep in their fold if they're going to win in a state not just like Virginia, but in North Carolina. And those are the kind of numbers that are a warning sign, and maybe an argument for Haley to continue to make this case forward that there is a lot of people in the Republican party that are not sold on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rick Klein, that takes us right to Linsey Davis who's been diving into the exit poll data. I know a couple of these states that had primaries today actually asked the question, if you supported \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  today, will you support whoever the Republican nominee is come November? And people gave a telling result here.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're exactly right. That goes to Rick's point of these potential warning signs as far as you have these fundamental differences between the \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters and the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  voters. So let's read some tea leaves as far as what tonight means for November potentially. And by tea leaves, I mean the exit polls. So let's take a look. In North Carolina, as far as \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters go, you have 80% of them who are saying, look, even if she is not the nominee, I'm not voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTE FOR GOP PRES. NOMINEE REGARDLESS OF CANDIDATE?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S JOB PERFORMANCE AS PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So overwhelming majority. But not just there. In California, you see a similar trend with 69% of those \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters saying that they wouldn't go for Trump. More of the same here in Virginia. Again, 69%. And where this becomes potentially problematic for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, take a look. In Virginia, we see similar numbers here in North Carolina as well, that these \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters are saying that they approve by 51% of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's job performance. So perhaps if they can't vote for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , will they potentially vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's the question. Are they up for grabs? Certainly the Trump campaign and the Biden campaign both know this. They both want \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's supporters. Mary Bruce, our chief White House correspondent, no surprise \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's victories tonight to the Biden campaign. In fact, you have reported in the last 24 hours that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the President himself has said to his campaign, we need to target \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and far more aggressively here.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, they certainly see this as the kickoff to the general election. And you have seen the President changing his tactic a bit recently. He is ramping up his attacks against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is really taunting him as a loser. We've heard that repeatedly. He clearly is trying and relishing getting under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's skin here a bit. We've also seen the President changing the way he is trying to reach voters. He has been posting more on Tik-Tok, something that's new to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's been back on late night television.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the President is again outlining what is at the core of this campaign for them. His argument that democracy is really what is on the line in this election. They know they have to walk this fine line, the Biden campaign, of outlining what is the big stakes here while also trying to do a better job of selling and touting the President's accomplishments. He'll have a big chance to do that during the State of the Union Address later this week.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The State of the Union on Thursday this week. And in recent days, both of them being on the border signals that President Biden recognizes that a key issue for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the southern border is perhaps issue number one heading into this election.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the Biden campaign and the Biden administration are well aware that this is a real political liability for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  heading into November. It's why you saw them with that really rare dueling visits down to the border. And the President in a bit of a shift of a tactic there too, reaching out to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , inviting him. Saying, you know, let's try and work on this together, at the same time that both Trump and Biden are blaming each other for this crisis. And of course, the President again blaming Trump for tanking that bipartisan border deal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've got have to wonder if he is going to do the same thing Thursday night. Inviting Congress to take another look at the bipartisan legislation. I want to bring in our chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 . You hear Mary talk about the Biden campaign, saying this is the jump start of the general election. But look at these numbers. We've already been seeing these general election matchups in the polls. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " polling, Fox News, "Wall Street Journal," our 538 polling average. All of the polls putting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have Trump ahead by two points to four points there. And we look back four years ago at this point, \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was ahead by three or four points. This is a significant turnaround four years later.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2024 GENERAL ELECTION LATEST NATIONAL POLLING\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Four years ago, Super Tuesday, the 538 polling average had \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with an average of two point lead over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Also, we have the central theme really of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's campaign is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a loser. With \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  we lose. I'm the one that can beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . One of the reasons she's had a harder time making that point is because of these polls. Because the polls show that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, can and in some cases does beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But let's remember, polls this far out from a general election are notoriously unreliable.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we all remember in 2016, throughout that primary campaign, throughout the approach into the fall campaign, Hillary Clinton was going to be elected president of the United States. She was not. And I think that with Biden, you see his flaws are present before the American people every single day. He is President of the United States. Their concerns manifested with the President of the United States. You're going have a campaign where the Biden campaign is going to be very well funded and very well motivated to remind the country what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is all about.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And their tact tonight is saying, we haven't even started yet, essentially, that here we go. Let's bring in Rick Klein, because you actually went into these numbers a little further into the battlegrounds where obviously we know every four years the presidential race is decided. And where is the matchup? It appears \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is even further ahead of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLEGROUND STATE POLLING\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yean, it's not just that two point national edge that 538 polling average has for President Trump right now. It's throughout the battlegrounds. These are the seven states most likely to determine the general election. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won six out of the seven. The only one on this list he didn't carry last time was North Carolina. You can see edges between three and 10 points for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  right now in our 538 polling averages, that is significant. It doesn't necessarily mean that he wins. It doesn't necessarily mean that there isn't something that can change. But it is a big difference from what we saw from four years ago this time.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is where the 538 polling average stood on Super Tuesday four years ago exactly. You see at that time, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was leading pretty consistently in places like Michigan, Pennsylvania, even North Carolina, which he ended up losing and which is voting in the primaries tonight. So he is going to have to turn around some of the dynamics we've seen play out in the states most likely to matter the most.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Leading in four of the seven battlegrounds \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was four years ago on Super Tuesday. That's really incredible. Martha Raddatz, you've been out traveling the country as moderator, co-anchor of "This Week." One of the things you've heard from voters is that it's not just democracy as Mary Bruce points out, saving democracy, the campaign of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but that he is going to answer, have to answer to voters moving forward for the four years that he has been president now. And - have they underestimated that voters are actually asking him to answer to his own record right now too?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's exactly right. An incumbent has an advantage in many ways, and this race is different because you really kind of do have two incumbents. But voters are not in many places getting the message. Voters I talk to say, look, some of them, I think he has been a great president, but I need more from him. And they always, always go back to age. Almost every voter I've talked to, even those who are strong supporters of Biden, worry about that and say that. And age and enthusiasm. I think many people aren't really even paying attention.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They know who's going to be running against the other. They know what's going to happen in November, and that they will be the likely nominees. So I don't think they want to pay attention yet. They are looking forward and hoping the Biden supporters and the Biden campaign, that they can get those voters enthused. And that will be part of how he campaigns, what he tells them, what his message is. And they also, you know, we talk a lot about the economy. They'll say even if they support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , yeah, but groceries are more expensive. It's more expensive to go out to restaurants. Gas was more expensive. That really, really matters to them. So they're watching that. They're watching everything \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says. But they are definitely testing him about age.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Martha Raddatz here all night long as well. Thank you. Let's get to former chief of staff to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and former head of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , ABC News contributor Reince Priebus also with us. Reince, do you see any scenario where it's not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the Republican side?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, I don't. And keep in mind, everybody, - it's important to remember that the way delegates are awarded in a party, in the Republican party, is that most of these delegates are locked in on at least the first vote at the national convention. So it really is difficult. You remember back in 2016, people were saying we're going to free the delegates. We're going have an open convention. I knew the whole time that's just - the way the rules are written, - it's impossible pretty much to do that. So I think this is where we're at. It's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 /\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I think the general election starts now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of people at home are saying I cannot believe this, we're doing this all over again. But in the meantime, what does \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  do? Does she endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? How much longer does she stay in this?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well - as you noted earlier, I think it's pretty remarkable and noteworthy that she is not doing anything tonight. She is not giving a speech. Look, we've got every network, every cable show on the television going, talking about Super Tuesday. It's a really great time to get 15 minutes of free air time. She's not taking it. That should tell you everything you need to know. I mean, I would suspect within 48 hours, my guess is that she is going to be dropping out of this race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. We'll find out if you're the answer. 48 hours. The clock is ticking. Reince Priebus with us here as well. Reince, thanks. Former head of the DNC, ABC News contributor Donna Brazile also with us. Donna, you've been in contact with the Biden campaign, of course. They want to run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they have said this. But you see the polling there. You saw the battlegrounds that Rick just showed us. You see the overall general election matchup. A lot of voters out there who support President Biden. Democrats out there in this country, when they see numbers like that, now that this looks like an unofficial rematch, it makes them really nervous.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, I don't think so. I think it really makes us eager to go out there and really fight for this country and the country's future. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  understands what's at stake. He understands how to bring jobs home to the American people. He knows how to deliver. And maybe what he will do this week at the State of the Union is tell the American people how he can do even more. So I tell my friends, don't fret, don't whine. Just get out there and vote.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Donna, does he have to make this about more than just saving democracy, more than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? You heard Mary Bruce reporting in the last 24 hours, President Biden has said to his own campaign team, we need to go aggressively after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But does the President need to be out there making his own case on the southern border, on the economy, recognizing that Americans are saying, as Martha points out, that they're not feeling it?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, I think president Biden can do both. He can say that I can run this country and run this campaign because I may be old, but my ideas are young. I am focused on the future. Here is what I tell my grandchildren and what I want you to tell your grandchildren. We're going to be a stronger, more prosperous, safer country with a Democratic president who is running to make sure that we can all win. He is not running to stay out of jail. He's not running to demonize anybody. He is running for the future of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Donna Brazile, always great to have you as well. Stick with us for the prime time hour. We should point out that in California at this hour, another big story unfolding, the big Senate primary, who will - fill the seat of the late \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 . In California, it's actually not too separate primaries. It's the two top vote getters who will then face off, which could be a Democrat and a Republican or it could be two Democrats facing each other. So let's get right to Matt Gutman. He is live in California covering a Senate race that's going to be watched by the country. Hey, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, David. That's why it's called jungle primary, because it can be pretty tough. And it's the top two candidates who then proceed to the general election. Right now, the favorite is Adam Schiff. He is a congressman who presided over the impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And he's been running in what he says is a fashion that he prefers, which is either running scared or running unopposed. He is kind of doing both at this moment. He is running scared in the sense that he is taking his primary opponent here, who is \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , another congresswoman from California, very, very seriously. She is a maverick, a rising star in the party.\par \par \uc2 MATT GUTMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But he has attacked her by actually trying to elevate a Republican in the race. Steve Garvey, Major League Baseball star. He has run many millions of dollars of attack ads on Garvey, thereby elevating his profile and his image to the extent that Garvey in some polls is actually leading the race. So it could either be Schiff or Garvey likely as number one and two. And that would basically mean that in the general election, he would be running unopposed, because in California, Democrats outnumber Republican registered voters by a factor of two to one. Now, \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 's supporters have said hey, that's cynical. It's brazen. Adam Schiff supporters have a different term for it. They call it politics, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Matt Gutman, tracking this well into the night, thank you. When we come back, we still have a lot to get to here tonight on this Super Tuesday. Results still coming in. Of course, the top issues, and if this is a Trump/Biden rematch again, what both candidates need to address, from immigration, the southern border, to the economy, abortion rights, after the overturning of Roe. And now the future of IVF in this country. And of course the question many people are asking in their own living rooms, is there any chance that these tickets could change? Could someone jump in at the last minute? Even \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , the former first lady weighing in late today on whether she would run for president. Like I said, we have a lot to get to. Don't go away.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VERMONT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage of Super Tuesday. You're looking at the results as they come in right now. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won 11 out of 12 states in the Republican primary so far. As you can see, Vermont also very close at this hour, the closest race. Perhaps \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's one chance tonight to pull off a victory. But so far \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it's been a sweep of this night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . A major night. In fact, the former president speaking moments ago at Mar-a-Lago. Let's take a listen.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 ...Super Tuesday for a reason. This is a big one. And they tell me, the pundits and otherwise, that there has never been one like this. There has never been anything so conclusive. This was an amazing, an amazing night and an amazing day. It's been an incredible period of time in our country's history. It's been sad in so many ways. But I think it's going to be inspiring because we're going to do something that frankly nobody has been able to do for a long time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at Mar-a-Lago moments ago. Let's get reaction from Rachel Scott who is covering the Trump campaign. She is at Mar-a-Lago right now. Rachel, a very happy campaign tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, the former president still on the stage right now taking this victory lap, David. And one person he did not mention at all so far in these remarks is \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . The former president clearly trying to pivot here to this general election, likely rematch with President Biden. He called him out by name. He is taking him on not only on immigration and the economy, two issues that according to our latest polling, ABC News/IPSOS polling that shows more Americans trust former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on those issues than they do President Biden.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now the former president here also calling for unity, calling for the Republican party to rally behind him. Again, he did not mention \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  by name. But he did make a call to his supporters to rally behind him, even as he is still undergoing all of these legal troubles, David. We know that's central to his campaign message.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Supporters cheering on the former president there in the room behind you. Rachel Scott down at Mar-a-Lago. Rachel, thanks. We have word just in from President Biden himself.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And David, we've been talking about how eager the Biden campaign is to crystallize the stark differences between Biden and Trump. In the statement tonight, the President said that "tonight's results leave the American people with a clear choice," saying "they can choose to either keep moving forward or let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  drag us backwards." The President goes on to outline some of his accomplishments, saying all of that progress is at risk. He says that "Trump is driven by grievance and grift, focused on his own revenge and retribution, not the American people." Saying he will say or do anything to put himself in power.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Game on. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Off to the races.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, Mary. You know, we heard from voters today, immigration among their top issues. And for some of them, it is the top issue heading into November. Both President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , both at the southern border in recent days. Mireya Villarreal live tonight at the border for us. Mireya, this could be a defining issue come this November.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, that is an understatement. And look no further than what we are seeing right now in Virginia. Perfect example, a state that is nowhere near the border, and yet immigration is at the top of their priority list. We saw the same thing happen in Iowa and New Hampshire as well back in January. Here is the thing that concerns people most is we have not seen immigration reform since the Reagan administration. And the concern from voters right now we're hearing from is that it doesn't feel like to them that politicians even want to fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In fact, in the field we call this immigration issue a political pi\u241\'00\'F1ata. Basically, both sides of the aisle are hitting this issue as hard as they possibly can. The result is votes come out, right. If you're on the Republican side, you use words like migrant crime or invasion. And that really gets people angry and frustrated. And they go to the polls. And then on the other side of it, you know, on the Democratic side of it, you hear words like dignity and humanity, tugs at your heartstrings, again, making you go to the polls. But in the middle we have voters, people that live right here in these border communities that are very concerned that what happens after November, what happens after someone gets elected. Will immigration still be at the top of the list?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Real question, Mireya Villarreal at the southern border tonight, thank you. As we've seen in the polling, immigration, the southern border has become a major issue for Democrats and Republicans in this country who recognize that Congress and whoever the President is, has to work together to get something done. The other key issue that we've seen just in recent days, abortion rights front and center again. The future of IVF after what we saw in recent days in Alabama, of course the state's highest court determining that frozen embryos are people. Clinics and hospitals there halting for now. Let's get right to Elizabeth Schulze who has been covering this story for us. Elizabeth?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And David, the Biden campaign is directly tying that IVF ruling here in Alabama to the end of Roe v. Wade. Vice President Harris blames \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for picking three \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  justices who would overturn Roe and lay the groundwork for states like Alabama to impose new restrictions on reproductive rights. Alabama is one of 16 states that has banned nearly all abortions since the end of Roe. And voters here tell us they didn't expect that IVF would become a target so quickly when Roe ended. They say that it highlights the importance of down ballot races, of judicial picks, as well as the presidential race. Republican lawmakers here as well as on the national level, David, including Trump, are really trying to walk a fine line. They want to show that they support IVF which has broad public support, while also at the same time pushing for more restrictions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Elizabeth, thank you so much for your reporting. Mary, you have sourcing inside the campaign. They very much say this will be on the ballot this November and it will literally be on the ballot in some states.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, David. We have seen abortion right advocates encouraged by a string of wins in other states, now trying to add measures to protect abortion in at least nine states, including several key battlegrounds like Arizona, Nevada, and Florida. We have seen in previous races how this really is motivating voters to come to the polls. The Biden campaign hoping it will continue to motivate voters. Also, of course, abortion is going to be an issue to watch closely in key states that have tough restrictions like Georgia, possibly North Carolina, areas where the Biden campaign may be hoping that a backlash to this issue will also drive voters as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES WHERE ABORTION COULD BE ON THE BALLOT IN NOVEMBER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they hope the issue will galvanize people in the battlegrounds, Martha. But it doesn't even need to be the battleground. We've seen in some very conservative states like Kansas what has happened when abortion rights is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And - they rejected any abortion restrictions, several states did. Kentucky, Kansas, Ohio. They rejected that. So this is a big issue. And I think obviously it's one that the Biden campaign wants to talk about a lot. Because they do look back at the midterms. And it had a real, it really made a difference in those midterms, according to the exit polls and pollings there. But voters I talked to as well, and women, it is such a complicated and personal issue. There was a Trump voter who I spoke to in Pennsylvania recently, and she said look, I'm a Catholic. She actually sounded very much like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She said I'm a Catholic, but I think women have a right to choose. So that is complicated and especially when they talk about limits.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Linsey, what is the exit data showing us from voters so far this primary season on this issue?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL LAW BANNING MOST OR ALL ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, well, particularly in Virginia, let's take a look. You have 54% saying that they would oppose banning most or all abortions. What is interesting, though, when we're looking at California, North Carolina, Virginia, quite often, this is their fourth place issue as far as what drove them to the polls. In many cases, we're seeing 9 or 8% who said this was top of mind for them. Of course, this is really the flip flop of something that you're seeing as far as Democrats. This is a high priority. Republicans are saying this is fourth or fifth place.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very interesting. And we've seen the Vice President also on the campaign trails. Clearly she has been assigned this by the Biden campaign to be out front and center on this issue. A lot more to get to when we come back here. The other major question tonight, will \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  face any of these federal criminal cases before election day? And how does that change the equation? What did voters say today if he does? And could we see one of these trials actually right in the weeks before the election? That was \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 's argument in just the last couple of days. We've got our team standing by live at the Supreme Court right after the break.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Your Voice, Your Vote, Super Tuesday 2024 continues. Here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to our live coverage of Super Tuesday here on ABC News. Let's take you right to the map on the floor. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually winning a sweep of the night so far. He has won 11 of the 12 states that have closed, where the polls have closed. ABC News calling 11 of those states for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Vermont very close at this hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 RACES IN PLAY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And of course 15 states total voting on this Republican primary. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is having a very good night. On the eve of this Super Tuesday, the Supreme Court in a unanimous decision ruled that states cannot act on their own to remove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the ballot, saying only Congress has that power. And we know this came after Colorado's highest court decided to take \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  off the ballot because of the - what happened on January 6th. The Supreme Court said again that this is not up to the states.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now the Supreme Court, even though the justices said we should turn down the temperature across this country, it's going to be very hard when they hear arguments about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's claim for presidential immunity in the coming weeks in the prosecution of the federal election interference case. I want to bring in Devin Dwyer who covers the court. He is live at the court tonight, and Devin, we were talking. You'd have to go back to Bush v. Gore to see the last time the Supreme Court played such a pivotal role in a presidential election year.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, David. More than 20 years ago. This is the most direct intervention in a political presidential election by this court. And its decisions, as you already said, they are already having an impact on the contours of the campaign. That unanimous decision just yesterday striking down a major legal attack on Trump's campaign that was brought by both conservatives and liberals. But now a much bigger showdown is shaping up next month here when the Supreme Court will decide whether Trump can get total immunity and shut down that federal election interference trial against him from going forward.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The former president says he is immune. The justices will decide whether that holds. If he is immune, David, that would shut down that prosecution in its tracks. If he does not have immunity, a trial could commence as soon as late summer or early fall, pushing right up against the election. A - verdict in that trial, and the extraordinary scene as you said at the top of a candidate sitting in a criminal trial, David.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT HEARS PRESIDENTIAL IMMUNITY CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARGUMENTS APRIL 22\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DECISION EXPECTED MAY-JUNE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Devin Dwyer, live at the court. Appreciate it. I want to bring in our legal analyst Dan Abrams. So Dan, what is the court going to do? I know you always get this question, but I'll pass it off to you. You know, \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  saying we need to take down the temperature. It's going to be very hard as the Supreme Court takes up this issue. Kate Shaw also with us tonight. But Dan, to you first on what you suspect the court could do here.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, the question on immunity, it's very unlikely that the court is going to rule that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or any president has complete immunity. The question is going to be the clock. When do they decide this? Do they decide at the very end of the term? Let's even assume that they do. Okay. Then how long does it take, the trial that's been on hold in Washington, DC, to then move forward? Now the Department of Justice has taken the position, at least the special counsel has, that this 60 day rule, which is really a guideline within the Department of Justice, that says that the Department of Justice shouldn't be getting involved in political issues, investigations, indictments in the 60s days before an election.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Their position is, well, that's not what we're doing here. When the court would set a trial date isn't the issue of the 60 days. That would be an issue of an indictment or an investigation or a subpoena, those sorts of issues. So from their perspective, they think that the case could even begin within the 60 day period right before the election. I still find that all to be very hard to believe that that will happen.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And not to put you on the spot, you suspect the court will say he does not have complete immunity and then this is going to be up to Judge Chutkan to decide if and when this case begins?\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Correct. I think - that anyone who is watching the court would be stunned if they accepted the argument that he has complete and total immunity. Remember, there are two questions, right? Number one is, does he have this, does the former president have complete immunity? And number two, was he acting in his role as president when this happened? And so the fact that there are two questions could lead to some question marks. But, again, the way that the court could really punt this if they wanted to would be to send it back to the trial court to resolve certain questions.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And again, then you think about the clock, right. Typically, in these kinds of cases, we don't think about the clock first. In this kind of case, you have to be thinking about the clock at all times. How long is that going to take? How much of a delay will that be? Does that still leave time to have the case go before the election? And look, in theory, the issue shouldn't be when is the election? But as a practical matter, you know that the special counsel, \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  is thinking about that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dan Abrams, thanks to you. Kate Shaw, you and I have been talking, you have to look back to history to see perhaps how quickly in terms of the court itself, they could act here. If they're going to take up arguments on this in April in just weeks' time, how soon after that could we actually get a decision from the Supreme Court and where this would go from there?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think, David, history tells us the Supreme Court can move very quickly when it wants to. We've talked before about 1974 and the United States versus Nixon case. But actually, just looking at the decision yesterday, restoring Trump to the ballot in Colorado and every other state, I think that's actually pretty good evidence that the court can move quickly when it wants to. That was about three and a half weeks between the argument in that case and the decision that we got yesterday. So when the court feels the need, it can move quickly. It clearly wanted to get its decision out before Super Tuesday so there was a definitive ruling about Trump's eligibility to be on the ballot in all of the states where people are voting today.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if the court feels a similar sense of urgency, just to give an answer to this immunity question one way or another, to allow a trial to potentially proceed, then it can certainly decide this immunity question, which I think is in many respects simpler than the disqualification question in yesterday's opinion, in a matter of weeks too. So it could decide the case in May. And then I think you are probably outside of this 60 day clock that Dan was just talking about. You could well see a trial late summer, very early fall, at least with a bit of a buffer before actual election day.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, a few seconds left. You think we could get a decision from the court, Kate, sometime in May, early June?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. I think even mid-May is a possibility. You get into June and things get trickier with the trial calendar. But May, I think everything is possible.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And not to put you on the spot too, but do you agree with Dan Abrams that it's likely the court will not grant the President total immunity here?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, Dan and I disagree a lot, but we totally agree here. I think that the arguments that Trump is advancing here I think are difficult to square with our Constitutional tradition, which just says really, no person is above the law. That's essentially what Trump is asking the court to grant him, essentially, you know, a complete immunity from ordinary criminal process. And I don't see a majority court. I'm not even sure about even more than one or two justices adopting that argument.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A moment of unanimity with our legal team here too as well. Kate Shaw, Dan Abrams, our thanks to you. Real quickly before we go to the break, because this, again, not to get ahead of ourselves, but if this were to go to trial, if there was a conviction, some voters have indicated that that would change the way they vote.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has said all along I'm staying in this for those 40% of the voters who don't want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And they have some strong feelings about if he were to get convicted. Look at Virginia coming in hot. 87% of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters say that Trump is not fit to be president if he is convicted. Just ticking through here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS TRUMP FIT TO BE PRESIDENT IF CONVICTED?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) North Carolina more of the same. 79% say he is not fit. Take a look at California with a similar number. 71% - say he is not fit if convicted. And if we just look at the aggregate of six states that we know so far, if you throw in Iowa, New Hampshire, South Carolina, 79% say if convicted, again, these are \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters, they don't think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is fit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is why a huge strategy of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the campaign, is to push this timeline as best they can to get it...\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Running out the clock.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...pushed out beyond the election. Linsey, our thanks to you. We've got a lot more ahead here. When we come back here, a lot of attention, Martha mentioned it earlier, on age in this race. Really, for both candidates. And the question being asked across this country, is there any time at this point for anyone else to step into the race, even perhaps a last minute around convention time? People have talked about the former first lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . Well, she weighed in on this whole idea today, whether or not she could run for president. We'll have that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back, ABC News live coverage of Super Tuesday. This is the primary season so far. Take a look at the wall right now. You can see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 739 estimated delegates so far this primary season. Vastly helped by tonight. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  with 53 estimated delegates, about 10 so far tonight. If you look at the very top of that graphic that you see, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  needs 1,215 delegates to clinch the nomination, which takes us right back over to Rick Klein at the big board. When does he clinch it, given the pace of the math so far?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO NOMINATION\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, there's still a lot of delegates in play tonight. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is positioned well to get most of them. Even if he loses Vermont, which seems likely right now, he is going to win California almost certainly. He'll probably be at around a thousand to 1100 plus delegates by the end of the night, which puts him in prime position to clinch this nomination. Maybe as soon as next week. If you look at what's coming up, fast forward to March 12th, these are the delegates in play in Mississippi, Washington state and Georgia, the state that broke him - in the general election and of course led to criminal charges ultimately, could be one of the states that puts him over the top as soon as next week. If not next week, almost certainly the week after, even if Haley stays in the race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF THE RACE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So not tonight, but very likely next week or the week after.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's exactly right. He is on the precipice.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. What a remarkable turnaround, \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , for a former president who left office, having lost to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The events of January 6th, there was a lot of attention not only in this country, but around the world. And he's had a sweep tonight and really an incredible primary season so far.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As one of Trump's top advisers said to me a while back, this will be the greatest comeback since the Roman leader Cincinnatus. That's a long time ago. I mean this - this is unbelievable when you think that he left the White House in January of - 2021 in disgrace. He was a pariah to most of the leaders in his party. He was then impeached. He then started to reassert himself slowly and inserted himself in the 2022 elections. Republicans lost all over the map in 2022, vastly underperforming. Those losses were blamed on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He announced his campaign, David, on November 15th of 2022 at Mar-a-Lago. I was there. Very few other Republican leaders were there. There was very, he - had no real endorsements. The first polls in late 2022 early last year actually showed \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  beating him. And he has made this comeback. And it's remarkable because it is a comeback that has happened, coinciding with four criminal indictments, 91 counts. All that has happened, Republicans have come back and chosen him decisively. It's a remarkable story.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Remarkable also because it's happening at a time when we've seen the national polling that voters really don't want either one of these men. They don't want this rematch in this country.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, we're headed to a rematch that the country doesn't want. And frankly, you heard \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  say this a lot in recent weeks, but it's true. 70% of the country says they don't want this rematch. And we're barreling towards a Biden/Trump rematch. You know, it's where we are. But I have to say, Trump is, this is a very big night for him. He is not clinching tonight, but he is effectively the presumptive nominee following what has happened here tonight. But he has dominated Republican primaries. He dominated them in 2022 as well, his candidates. He has lost every general election campaign since that one that he won in 2016. So now we'll see. He has dominated the Republicans. Where is he going to lead the Republicans and the country to in November?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's the question for him. The question for President Biden is how does he address the age issue, which has become a significant issue for him. And also this question we were getting at with Jon a moment ago, that the country doesn't want really this rematch at all, either one of these candidates. It's why the conversation leads to \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  and other people. It appears the former first lady had had enough of that talk.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, she is shutting that one down. We have seen this before, the speculation, the rumors that perhaps \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  will get in. Her team says, not going to happen. She fully supports the Biden/Harris ticket. She'll be happy to be a surrogate out there on the trail. But it does speak to this real discontent. Look, the fact of the matter is a lot of Democrats wish \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wasn't running again. But he is. And the campaign is really arguing that right now is the time to accept that reality and move forward. But they are certainly exasperated and very frustrated by this continued focus on the President's age.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They understand it. They're trying to tackle it. They have a messaging issue with it. They know they need to do more. And I think when they look at the big picture, yes, you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the oldest president in American history. He would be 82 on election day. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not that much younger. But I think the big picture they argue is when they stack, and they hope what will happen, when they stack \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's accomplishments against a twice impeached defeated former president facing 91 felony counts who has been found guilty of sexual assault and financial fraud, that ultimately that if it comes down to that, if they can remind voters of his record and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's record and what he stands for, that voters will back \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  once again.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's the strategy. The reality, though, what do voters say about age?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  voters, people say all kinds of things about them. But one thing that stands true is they are loyal. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 77 years old, just four years younger than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But take a look at these Trump voters in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP PHYSICALLY & MENTALLY FIT TO BE PRESIDENT?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 96% say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is physically, he's mentally fit to be president. On the flip side, when we look at just a few days ago there was a "New York Times"/CNN poll where you have Biden voters who are a little more questionable. 61% believe that Biden may be too old to be effective as a president. So really a total opposite there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN TOO OLD TO BE EFFECTIVE PRESIDENT?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Martha Raddatz, you hear this from President Biden supporters across the country who went out in these primaries to support him. Even though he is the incumbent, didn't need it. But went out to show their support, recognizing that age is an issue.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It clearly is an issue. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , look, he just had a physical, right? The White House released that. He does have a stiff gait. You talk to people in the White House all the time. I talk to people in the White House who say, in meetings, he is coherent, he is fine. But you get these moments with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And they dominate news cycles. They do. They especially dominate them in conservative media and people look at that. And in some ways, when they see those moments, it validates their own suspicions. Like he is old. I just saw that moment.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was thinking it was kind of like Dan Quayle, the Vice President who people thought maybe he is not so smart. And then he misspelled potato, which was such a little, tiny thing. But when you get something that validates your own suspicions, when they say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  forgets things, when they see him walking, then that validates that. And that's the problem. And the problem he can do, none of us can do anything about.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Dan Quayle is wondering tonight, really, you had to bring up potato?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've got more to our Super Tuesday coverage coming up here in a moment. And one key question, in all these polls that show them neck and neck right now, President Biden, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who are the 7 to 8% undecided and will they decide this election? We'll ask that of the roundtable here in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back live with our Super Tuesday coverage, right to the numbers. You can see it's been a very strong night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is now within a week or two of clinching the math he needs for the nomination. Rick Klein, very tight race between President Biden, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  already. Two to four points in the national polls between them. This might all come down to the 7, 8% of undecideds in this country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2024 GENERAL ELECTION\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's basically a tied race with general elections starting now. And those 7 or 8 percentage points that you're talking about, they're what people talk about as the double haters. They dislike both candidates. They're on the sidelines for now. Of course, the effort will be to get them back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All about the independents and the moderates. I'm David Muir. Thanks for watching. "\uc2 GMA\uc2 " first thing in the morning and I'll see you for "World News Tonight" tomorrow. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020240315ek3500002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CVS AND WALGREENS TO START DISPENSING ABORTION PILL THE PHARMACY CHAINS WILL PROVIDE ACCESS IN SELECT STATES FIRST\b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT: FIRE AND ICE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLOCKBUSTER BLIZZARD\par \par \uc2 BLIZZARD, CALIFORNIA, SNOW, WARNINGS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, WILDFIRE, SMOKEHOUSE CREEK\par \par \uc2 WINTER, WEATHER, SNOW, RAIN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ELECTION, WILLIS, HEARING\par \par \uc2 CVS, WALGREENS, ABORTION PILL, ACCESS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the decision by two major pharmacy chains to provide access to the abortion pill in states where it's legal. ABC's Em Nguyen is in Washington with that story. Em, good morning.\par \par \uc2 EM NGUYEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. The abortion pill, Mifepristone will soon be available at the two largest pharmacy chains in the US. \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  says it expects to start filling prescriptions within a week, providing potentially easier access to the drug at a small number of its locations where it's legal, including New York, Pennsylvania, and California. CVS says it will start dispensing in the weeks ahead in Massachusetts and Rhode Island, and then expand to other stores and states where the drug is legally permitted. Both pharmacy chains say they've received certification to begin dispensing the abortion drug under the new rules the fda released last year.\par \par \uc2 EM NGUYEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But those rules are now part of an upcoming case before the Supreme Court where an anti-abortion rights group argues the drug is dangerous. It is a claim widely refuted by the fda and medical communities, which say more than two decades on the market have proven otherwise. Now, Justices will hear that case later this month.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LEGISLATING IVF PROTECTIONS. ALABAMA LAWMAKERS PASS BILL TO SAFEGUARD FERTILITY SERVICES\par \par \uc2 EM NGUYEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Meanwhile in Alabama, lawmakers rush to approve legal protections to In Vitro Fertilization services this week in the wake of the state's Supreme Court's controversial ruling that frozen embryos should be considered children. State legislators in the Republican-led House and Senate expect that measure to be signed into law next week. Gio.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Em, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240318ek3200006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 I'LL SEE HER AGAIN BATTLE OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,117 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I'LL SEE HER AGAIN\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA, PREGNANCY, ABNORMALITIES, TIEN, ABORTION, VANESSA, JENNY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I'LL SEE HER AGAIN\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for joining us. Tonight we begin with the battle over abortion rights, which once again could drive some voters to the polls this election year, but there's another side to the story, the lives caught up in the middle, forced to make painful decisions and travel hundreds of miles away from home. ABC's Rachel Scott takes us inside one couple's deeply personal journey that they never expected to make.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 So this was going to be her nursery. We've gotten some toys and some onesies. Even friends have given us a bunch of hand me downs. I'm very anxious this whole pregnancy, so I didn't like to come in here, really to be honest. I was scared for most of this pregnancy. This is the special bear that the high-risk doctor gave us with her heartbeat in it. You like messing with that, don't you?\par \par \uc2 YOUNG CHILD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I do. It annoys you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I like it, but it's also very upsetting too.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sometimes there aren't words to describe a grief this deep. A young mom supposed to be celebrating the coming birth of a new life, instead mourning it. Forced to travel hundreds of miles from home in order to save her own life.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Blood on your hands.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Blood on your hands.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Since the fall of Roe versus Wade, whole regions of the country have plunged into uncertainty, surrounding laws restricting abortion access, patients face harassment, potential lawsuits. Doctors face fines, suspensions, even prison time. In many places, abortion advocates say there are exceptions to the bans. But what does it take to qualify?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 Do we have to wait until they are septic and have uncontrollable infection, have an ICU admission, need multiple blood transfusion, at the brink of organ failure before we intervene? That's not how we practice medicine.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) What does it mean to be on the brink? And what is the cost of the lives in the balance?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I shouldn't have to be on my death bed for you to consider my life as important as my baby's life.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At 22 weeks along, it's painfully obvious that Alexandra is pregnant. What you can't see is the heartbreak behind her smile. This is a last-minute maternity shoot for the 31-year-old Texas mom. In just a few hours, she and her husband Blake will leave for Washington, DC to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I guess that's one thing that I'm grateful for is that I'm able to have this time with her and like know like there's an end near, but I can enjoy her last days. It's hard, but I'm thankful for it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's a tragic end to a much wanted pregnancy, one Alexandra wanted to badly, she took on a second job to afford IVF. A baby girl named Aurora Grace. And while she is confident in her decision, she did ask us to change their names to avoid harassment for speaking publicly about this very private journey.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 This is her little head and her nose. This is like her mouth area, but her hand was in the way. I went to a high-risk doctor and they did an anatomy scan, I was 19 I was almost 20 weeks, 19 weeks and a few days. The first thing he said is, you know, I'm seeing a lot of abnormalities here, and she is not compatible with life.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In all there were four different abnormalities. Altogether, there was no chance of survival, especially with one being Potter Syndrome, where critical organs don't develop.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Kidneys. The kidneys are not functioning, which caused no fluid. You have to have fluid for a baby's lungs to develop. So in essence, she wouldn't be able to breathe when she gets out. With all of them together, it just wasn't right.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I think my health is what I'm scared about the most, just because no matter what she's going to be gone, but I still have a family that I need to live for, and my son needs me. And I just want to stay healthy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In addition to the fetal diagnosis, Alexandra had other complications. A few weeks ago, she started bleeding. She hasn't stopped. She also has something called placenta previa, which can cause severe bleeding closer to delivery.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What did the doctors say about the risks to your own health?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 He was like something needs to be done now. You know, they were very scared about hemorrhaging or, you know, any complications. I called my husband on the way home and was like meet me at the house. I talked to him about it, and he's like, no matter what we're saving you first. He's like we love this baby, but if she has no quality of life, there's no reason for us to drag this on longer than it has to be.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When did you know that something was wrong?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I always had like mother's intuition, like there was something wrong.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you're getting the news that no expecting mother wants to hear from the doctor, and you're not getting any answers as to what more you can do and what your options are, did you understand why in that moment?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I did afterwards. And that was very frustrating for me, because the whole word, "Abortion," was not in my vocabulary, because I don't, I personally don't believe in it. But it's never been something that I've ever really thought about in-depth. You know, like, I'm not that political.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What about now?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 It's not just yes or no. It's not black and white. It's, there's definitely gray areas that should definitely be considered and not some government telling you that yes or no.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For someone living in Texas and all across the southern part of the country, abortion is largely banned. And for someone in their second trimester, finding treatment gets even harder.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're a nurse. You help people every day. Did you get a sense that your doctors were afraid?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, definitely. It wasn't that they didn't want to help me, they were just nervous about telling me where to get the help.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Shelly Tien knows what's that's like. On this day Dr. Tien is in Kansas at the Trust Women Clinic.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 Hi, Dr. Tian, nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Good to see you too.\par \par \uc2 NURSE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You did good, go right through here, my dear.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As a board certified OB/GYN who specializes in maternal/fetal medicine, Dr. Tien is an expert in high-risk pregnancies. She's also an abortion provider.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why do you think it's essential to provide abortion care?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 Safe abortion care is women's health care. It's necessary health care. And suddenly we are in a position where we're scared to talk about it. Physicians and health care systems are wondering is she sick enough to meet that exception? Can I act now?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Tien goes to Kansas for a few days every month. She also works part of the year attending to high-risk pregnancies in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 Do you need me?\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 What can I do for you?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But her home base is in Jacksonville, Florida, which means Tien spends an extraordinary amount of time in airports and on planes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 You know, on a typical clinic day, we will wrap up usually by 5:00 PM, but that can also vary. I do always have a backup.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Florida's Republican-controlled government passed a 15-week abortion ban that could soon become a six-week ban. Dr. Tien is one of the plaintiffs challenging those restrictions in court.\par \par \uc2 CLINIC STAFF MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So today in regards to staff, we've got everybody here? We've got fearless Tien, 27 consults. A lot of consults today. Five, four surgicals.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 Can I come in? Okay. You're all set.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 When I talk with patients, you know, I tell them pregnancy is very much a stress test for the body. It's a stress test for the heart, for the lungs, for the kidneys, for the pancreas. So patients that already have preexisting medical concerns, that pregnancy can really worsen those health conditions.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA (SINGLE MOM)\par \par \uc2 My name is Vanessa. I'm a single mom from Pensacola, Florida. I have an autistic six-year-old son and I am here for a medical abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Vanessa asked us not to show her face. She nearly died after giving birth to her son when she was just 19 years old.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA (SINGLE MOM)\par \par \uc2 My blood pressure would not go down for like about a week straight. I had a stroke just the day after I had my son. It was a very scary situation. I didn't know that I was actually having a stroke.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Vanessa's blood pressure is still an issue, making pregnancy dangerous. She was in the hospital just days ago.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA (SINGLE MOM)\par \par \uc2 It just kind of feels like you have no other choice up there but to have a baby once you get pregnant.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On the same day, we also met a woman we will call Jenny.\par \par \uc2 JENNY (HIGH RISK PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I have stage 4 kidney disease, and I have very high blood pressure, and also type 2 diabetic. And my age, I'm 42 years as well. So I'm a very high risk pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Like Vanessa, Jenny was also recently hospitalized. She says this doesn't feel much of a choice to her either.\par \par \uc2 JENNY (HIGH RISK PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 It's life or death. Like I'm on a kidney transplant list now. But if I continue with the pregnancy, I'll get kicked off the kidney transplant list. I'm already sick. So if I were to continue with the pregnancy, I would put my life at risk, and then in the end the baby may not survive.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All of Jenny's doctors recommended termination.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 She did great. So her procedure went very smoothly. She's getting cleaned up and she's going to recovery.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OBGYN & MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 The exceptions to the abortion ban in many of these states are confusing, vague, and truly just woefully inadequate for how complex and nuanced pregnancy complications can be.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If Florida gets to impose a six-week abortion ban, both of these women would have to get incredibly sick to qualify for an exception. They would have to be on the brink of death. Back in Texas, that's something Alexandra refuses to let happen to her. Up next, her heart wrenching journey, half way across the country to save her own life.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020240221ek2j0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 I'LL SEE HER AGAIN BATTLE OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,120 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I'LL SEE HER AGAIN\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA, PREGNANCY, ABNORMALITIES, TIEN, ABORTION, VANESSA, JENNY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I'LL SEE HER AGAIN\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA, BLAKE, PREGNANCY, ABORTION, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I'LL SEE HER AGAIN\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Well, our flight was pretty early in the morning, for people that live two hours away, it means you have to get up at 3:30, 4:00, head to DFW get through security.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This trip to Washington, DC is one Alexandra and her husband Blake never thought they'd have to make.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 The flight was fairly easy. It was a two and a half, three-hour flight. So it's a good half day, more than half day travel. It lessened some anxiety just getting oriented to our room that we'll be in for the next few days before having to start everything tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Do you have your shoes on?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) How concerning were the complications to your health, knowing that you were at risk?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I was already bleeding. I woke up bleeding. I was bleeding more than normal. So if I, I would say if I was at home and this was a normal pregnancy, I would have thought she's gone. That's how much blood was coming out.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE (HUSBAND OF ALEXANDRA)\par \par \uc2 Let's go.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The DC clinic they chose specializes in abortions later in pregnancy. And because Alexandra is so far along, the appointment will happen over two days.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 So we've been here about an hour. We've paid for the remainder of our appointment, our procedure, and given us some options for funeral services afterwards. They said I had been contracting as well, it looked like I had been contracting. And she was like you have a very high risk of bleeding and hemorrhaging, and we don't want that.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I think that's it.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE (HUSBAND OF ALEXANDRA)\par \par \uc2 So it's about 9:20, sitting in the waiting room.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I've gotten my IV started. Feeling a little bit nervous, but she's still kicking and we're just cherishing that.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE (HUSBAND OF ALEXANDRA)\par \par \uc2 It's about 11:30 I think. She's very tired right now. She's sleeping, as you can tell.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I surprised myself, you know. I thought I would be bawling my eyes out and just feeling horrible today, and I just feel at ease. I know she's not coming home with me, but I have pictures, and I have everything that I need. This is her hat that she had on while we were with her. It was actually too big for her, actually. And then we got her little handprints and footprints. They were perfect. I love it.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE (HUSBAND OF ALEXANDRA)\par \par \uc2 I guess it all hit me later after the fact.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The clinic allowed the couple to spend that precious day there, to see and hold their daughter's body. For Alexandra, it was relief. For Blake, it was a heartbreaking hello and goodbye.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE (HUSBAND OF ALEXANDRA)\par \par \uc2 I guess I was more sad just because it was like this is the first time I'm seeing her, but also the last time.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I couldn't have done it without him. And there were times where he's comforted me for so long, you know, these past few weeks. And I think it was time for me to comfort him today. I don't think I could have been able to have closure if I did not have this day with her. I'll always have her in my heart. I'll see her again.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A month after the procedure, we caught up with Alexandra down in Texas.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 This one's name is Jelly Roll.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jelly Roll.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 And that's Morgan Wallen.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Talk to me about that first week being back home.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Throughout the whole process I clung to my son, just because I was like, you know, life is fragile. I felt like I just clung to him more. You know, my milk came in. So I had a lot of time to get that. That was a big distraction, trying to pump every two hours. I wish we could have done it closer to home. I think I probably wouldn't have been so stressed out.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will you try again?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Oh, definitely. We're definitely not going give up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You said you never thought about the word, "Abortion," before. You were against abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why come forward and share your story?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 When I found out about my diagnosis, I was so scared to tell people what my options were. I had told somebody, and he is very hard-core Republican. He was like, I've never thought about that. You've changed my mind on it. And that is what got me. I was like, if I can get this man to change his mind on it, there's got to be more people that I can make a change for, people that want pregnant, that want their babies and have to do it for something that is so unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA (HAS NON COMPATIBLE PREGNANCY)\par \par \uc2 I'm getting stronger where I can talk about it more. Everybody has said I would have done the same thing. I would have done the same thing. You've done, you did the right thing. And that has given me a lot of, you know, hope that my story did change people's minds, and that they may possibly, you know, do something different.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I'LL SEE HER AGAIN\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our thanks to Rachel Scott for that report. Alexandra and Blake plan to try another round of IVF some time this year. Our best wishes are with them. And that's "Nightline" for tonight. Thanks for staying with us. Good night, America.\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020240221ek2j0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BLACK WOMEN & MENTAL HEALTH THE CHANGING DYNAMIC IN COMMUNITIES OF COLOR\b0\par\par\uc2 1,296 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, FLOODING, LANDSLIDES, RESCUES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, FLOODING, WAVES, MUD\par \par \uc2 SENATE, BIPARTISAN, BORDER, UKRAINE, HOUSE, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ATTACK, RETALIATION, TARGETS, SULLIVAN, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 BOEING, 737S, SUPPLIER, NONCONFORMANCE\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, DEATHS, MISSING, HOMES, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAMMYS, CHAPMAN, SWIFT, CYRUS, DION, JAY-Z\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CRUMBLEY, TRIAL, MANSLAUGHTER, JURY, SMITH, MCDONALD, MARTIN\par \par \uc2 MOVING WALKWAY, AIRPORT, INJURIES, GOW, LAWSUIT, ALEINIKOFF\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, AI, RUFUS, HERZOG, SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL, 49ERS, CHIEFS, VEGAS, USHER, JAPAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, FLOODING, MUDSLIDES\par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, MUDSLIDES, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, DEAL\par \par \uc2 SPACE, TRAVEL, KONONENKO\par \par \uc2 CHOKING, SORRENTINO\par \par \uc2 LUCE, HEART VALVE DISEASE, MILLER, ADAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE MOM'S WARNING ABOUT HER HEART CONDITION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HER MESSAGE AFTER MITRAL VALVE PROLAPSE NEARLY KILLED HER\par \par \uc2 CHOPPED SALAD SANDWICH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE CHOPPED ITALIAN SALAD SANDWICH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRYING OUT THE LATEST VIRAL FOOD TREND\par \par \uc2 GRAMMY AWARDS, PITT, GARTEN, GOLDEN RETRIEVER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BRUNSON, "ABBOTT ELEMENTARY"\par \par \uc2 MENTAL HEALTH, WILLIAMS, FINZI-ADAMS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now as we celebrate Black History Month on "GMA." This morning, we're looking at mental health among black women in particular and how the culture of struggling in silence is now changing with the new generation. Deborah Roberts sat down with two women who are paving the way. Good morning, Deb.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLACK WOMEN & MENTAL HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE CHANGING DYNAMIC IN COMMUNITIES OF COLOR\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Linsey. And yes, paving the way, it is so important. Lately, we've heard a lot of discussion, especially from millennials about prioritizing our mental health. But in communities of color, the conversation can be a little tricky, particularly amongst older women who prioritize strength and perseverance. Yet a mom and daughter from Maryland are trying to bridge that divide.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BOONE (DAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 I would tell her about, you know, other things going on with myself physically. But when it came to mental health, it just wasn't a discussion we had in our family.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA BOONE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 From a small child, my emotions, you suppress them, you suck it up, you keep it moving.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lauren and Glenda Boone expressing a long-held belief among black women, keep your struggles and your inner most feelings to yourself.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The strong black woman is the image that we admire. Did you ever think about paying attention to your mental wellness?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE MOTHER & DAUGHTER HOPING TO INSPIRE CONVERSATIONS AMONG WOMEN OF COLOR\par \par \uc2 GLENDA BOONE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Never. I never thought about taking care of my mental health because my generation was taught when you talk about mental, you automatically thought mental illness. And so, it was very difficult for me to even consider taking care of my mental health.\par \par \uc2 DR ZOEANN FINZI-ADAMS (LICENSED PSYCHOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 That's what we call the superwoman schema. I can do it all, push through, persevere. If I'm distressed, it's my fault and my responsibility. So, going to a therapist means, I'm not doing my job ultimately.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But that thinking slowly changing in communities of color.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN THEE STALLION (RAPPER)\par \par \uc2 Black women, we have always been like, having to put on a mask like everything's okay.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thanks partly to well-known figures who are bravely opening up publicly.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN THEE STALLION (RAPPER)\par \par \uc2 We are usually everybody's therapist.\par \par \uc2 HOST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARAJI P HENSON (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Including former Destiny's Child singer Michelle Williams, who speaks openly about her history of depression, and says mental health struggles can look different in women of color.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHELLE WILLIAMS TALKS MENTAL HEALTH IN THE BLACK COMMUNITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SINGER ON HOW THE STRUGGLES CAN LOOK DIFFERENT IN WOMEN OF COLOR\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WILLIAMS (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 Irritability is a missed symptom of depression, because we think depression is just sadness. Once I started getting into therapy more consistently, it started giving me language to everything that I internalized, but I just didn't know what to say.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, there's strength in vulnerability?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WILLIAMS (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 There is strength in vulnerability. I can't tell you how strong you are when you can say, I need help versus thinking your strength is acting out because the quality of life is so much better when you're not triggered all the time.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And do you think younger women might be helping their mothers to think about health? Do you think that it's changing the dynamic?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WILLIAMS (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 It absolutely is.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA BOONE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 When I did start the therapy, I have to be completely honest with you, I went kicking and screaming.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lauren and Glenda living proof of that changing dynamic.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BOONE (DAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 And this is our second episode of "Healing Together."\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Even taking their journey online in a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  series, hoping to inspire other inter-generational conversations among women of color.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA BOONE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 It has helped me to understand me and the makeup of me.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BOONE (DAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm, mm-hmm. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLENDA BOONE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 My daughters let me know, you know, you were present, but you weren't present. She, right off the bat was, like, you never came to my softball games. So, my defense for my generation I'm like, are you kidding me? But when I got into therapy and started taking away the layers, that was just a symbol of providing for her, but you weren't there for things that were important to her.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when you started to dig deeper, what did you start to discover about yourself?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BOONE (DAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, girl.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How much time do we have?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BOONE (DAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Right? Keep talking about the hard things. That's the only way we're going to get better is that we're facing it, we're processing it.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what's your message to people who are closer to your age who have never even thought about therapy and counseling, but who maybe want to try it?\par \par \uc2 GLENDA BOONE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Don't be afraid. It will change your life if you're willing to do the work. But if you're willing to do is work, it will be the best thing you have ever done for you.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, Glenda's advice is so important because women of color are underdiagnosed when it comes to mental health issue often because symptoms like sleep disturbance, irritability, as you heard, and self-criticism aren't seen as signs of depression, but can be. So, this issue is as important as your mammogram or your pap smear.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as they say, it can change lives. And, guys, my daughter and I know because we've done some of this work and we've dug deep. And it's not easy, but we've come so much closer together because of therapy and counseling.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, thank you for sharing that. I think it's amazing. I love their strength and vulnerability. It's a really simple sentence. It means so much.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So much.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so important to have the conversations to normalize the idea of therapy and talking about it. Deb, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240222ek250000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SARAH FERGUSON'S NEW HEALTH BATTLE DUCHESS REVEALS SKIN CANCER DIAGNOSIS AFTER BEATING BREAST CANCER\b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC: ICE STORM STRIKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARM UP ON THE WAY\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, ICE, SLEET, SNOW, PIPES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAINFALL, SNOW, FLOODING, COMMUTE\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, ICE, SNOW, WARM UP\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS, PRIMARY, TRUMP, HALEY\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS, PRIMARY, TRUMP, HALEY, MENTAL FITNESS\par \par \uc2 MILITARY, INJURIES, BRAIN, PENTAGON, MILITANTS, ATTACKS\par \par \uc2 GAZA, MILESTONE, ISRAEL, DEATHS, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , TUNNELS\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2 , BOEING, 737 MAX, DOOR PLUG, INSPECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAINFALL FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 PETERSON, MURDER, SPITULSKI, VAN, BLOOD, EVIDENCE, GUDGELL\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON, CANCER, BREAST, MELANOMA, BUCHBINDER\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Kayna, really fascinating stuff, that all these years later there still seems to be a question. Kayna, thank you. Now to Duchess Sarah Ferguson, revealing her diagnosis of skin cancer. Maggie Rulli has the very latest. And, Maggie, of course, this news comes right after she beat breast cancer just months ago.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SARAH FERGUSON'S NEW HEALTH BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DUCHESS REVEALS SKIN CANCER DIAGNOSIS AFTER BEATING BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI ( ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Linsey, hey, good morning. Well the Duchess posted just moments ago on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  saying getting another cancer diagnosis has been shocking. She's now urging everyone to be diligent.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI ( ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, another royal health scare. Sarah Ferguson, Duchess of York, announcing she has malignant melanoma, an aggressive form of skin cancer. The diagnosis coming just months after she shared she had beaten breast cancer. The 64-year-old posting on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  this morning, "Naturally another cancer diagnosis has been a shock. But I'm in good spirits and grateful for the many messages of love and support."\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI ( ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "It was thanks to the great vigilance of my dermatologist that the melanoma was detected when it was." The cancerous mole was discovered when she was undergoing her reconstructive surgery for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH BUCHBINDER (SENIOR PHYSICIAN DANA FARBER CANCER INSTITUTE)\par \par \uc2 Some genetic predispositions, and sometimes with breast cancer there can be melanoma along with it. But often it's totally unrelated as well. So it could be that there's a genetic link.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI ( ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Duchess' breast cancer was caught in the early stages. She underwent her mastectomy last June, talking about that diagnosis on her podcast, "Tea Talks."\par \par \uc2 DUCHESS SARAH FERGUSON ("TEA TALKS")\par \par \uc2 It's vital that we need to wake everybody up to go screen. Not just breast cancer I'm talking, prostate, and all the checks.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI ( ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And, again, she's using her diagnosis to raise awareness writing, "I believe my experience underlines the importance of checking the size, shape, color and texture and emergence of new moles that can be a sign of melanoma, and urge anyone who is reading this to be diligent."\par \par \uc2 DR. ELIZABETH BUCHBINDER (SENIOR PHYSICIAN DANA FARBER CANCER INSTITUTE)\par \par \uc2 There is so much more hope, in terms of there are much better treatments, much better therapies, much better tests, but it's still a very, very serious cancer.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE RULLI ( ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, guys, to avoid the risk of skin cancer, doctors urge people to avoid the sun and tanning beds whenever possible, to wear sun screen. And if you do have a suspicious skin growth, to get it checked out immediately. As for the Duchess, she's back home in Windsor with her family. Guys.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROAD TO THE SUPER BOWL\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are wishing her all the very, very best. Maggie, thank you. Coming up next, some dedicated fans have been waiting three decades for their team to make it this far. Lara will have more on the road to the Super Bowl. Come on back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240201ek1m00035\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN STRUGGLES TO SHAKE CONCERNS OVER AGE, MENTAL ACUITY SOME REPUBLICANS HIGHLIGHT VP HARRIS IN CASE AGAINST BIDEN\b0\par\par\uc2 2,429 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2024 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 NEW HAMPSHIRE, TRUMP, HALEY, WHALE, PIAZZA, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 538, POLLING, GOP, PRIMARY, VOTERS, TRUMP, HALEY, DESANTIS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 BURGUM, 2024, TRUMP, BIDEN, 2020, HALEY, PELOSI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BURGUM ENDORSED TRUMP ON EVE OF IOWA CAUCUSES\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HARRIS, 538, TRUMP, FULKS, IPSOS, PHILLIPS, VOTERS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Biden team is hitting the campaign trail as the President faces low approval ratings and questions from voters on whether he's up for another term. So, this week our chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce took those questions to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS/YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN STRUGGLES TO SHAKE CONCERNS OVER AGE, MENTAL ACUITY\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Hello, Raleigh!\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At 81 years old already, and hoping for another four years, voter concern about President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's age and fitness for office is growing. We asked Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  about that when we spent the day with her in the first Democratic primary state of South Carolina. She brushed it off and defended her running mate's mental sharpness.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I spent a lot of time with President Biden, be it in the Oval Office, in the Situation Room, and other places. He is extraordinarily smart. He has the ability to see around the corner in terms of what might be the challenges we face as a nation or globally.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But it doesn't seem that that's getting out and resonating with Americans, with a lot of your supporters.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean, listen, you've, you're here with me in South Carolina. You saw every room we went in, the numbers of people who are there, applauding quite loudly. They're there because they believe in what we're doing and they want to see us continue to do this work.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME REPUBLICANS HIGHLIGHT VP HARRIS IN CASE AGAINST BIDEN\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But some Republicans are trying to make the not-so-subtle case that Biden may not make it through the next four years, and that their real opponent in 2024 is the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 We cannot afford a President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We won't survive it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HARRIS INCREASING APPEARANCES ON CAMPAIGN TRAIL\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  barrels towards the GOP nomination, Harris has been ramping up her presence on the campaign trail, by our count visiting at least 18 states in just the last six months. She's taking a leading role on issues like voting rights and abortion.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 In this year of our Lord 2024, the government should not be telling women what to do with their bodies.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 538: AVERAGE APPROVAL RATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HYPOTHETICAL MATCHUP AGAINST TRUMP\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both Biden and Harris face negative approval ratings, and though battleground state polls show Harris actually outperforming Biden in hypothetical matchups against Trump, both come up short to the former president. Harris, however, remains confident they will win this race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LACKLUSTER BIDEN POLL NUMBERS WORRY SOME DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 If it is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , we've beat him before and we'll beat him again.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But that confidence has even some top Democrats concerned, urging the campaign to be more aggressive.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why not go out and attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , go after his legal challenges? What are you guys waiting for?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Well, let me just tell you something. I am of the school that you either run without an opponent or you run scared. I have learned that to be a fact, and that is the way that I feel about any election. So absolutely not. You can't take anything for granted. And we have a duty, a responsibility to earn this re-elect.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY BIDEN SUPPORTERS EXPRESS CONCERN ABOUT 2024 STRATEGY\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Polling shows Biden and Harris are struggling to sell their accomplishments to the American people. Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , whose support in South Carolina resuscitated Biden's 2020 campaign, says he's especially concerned about their standing with Black voters.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How concerned are you that this key constituency may sit this one out?\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You got to earn the votes. And the votes are gonna be earned based on one, or in a re-elect, have you actually responded to the needs of the community? We have a responsibility to communicate. We've done really good work. Our challenge will be to let people know who brung it to them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN HAS MENTAL SHARPNESS TO BE PRESIDENT...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Mary for that. We're joined now by Biden principal deputy campaign manager, \uc2 Quentin Fulks\uc2 . Welcome to "This Week," sir. I know you saw the gaffe by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and you heard Governor Burgum's response. But age and mental acuity are a big concern with president Biden among voters. Our recent poll with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  found 69 percent of those polled do not think the President has the mental sharpness to be president. That is up from 43 percent in May of 2020. I know these are polls, not votes, but how do you get past this issue?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, thank you for having me on this morning, Martha. It's great to be with you. Look, when it comes to the President's age, we have a simple formula for that, and that's results. You know, age equals wisdom equals results and experience. And President Biden, because of his age, has come to the table and brought people together from both sides of the aisle to deliver results for the American people. Whether it be the historic bipartisan infrastructure bill, whether it has been bringing people to the table for job creation, making sure that inflation is down, the President has been delivering results.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 And our best answer to this is to continue to communicate about the things that people care about, Americans care about. This election is not going to be about age. This election is about freedom and democracy and the fact that Democrats under President Biden's leadership believe that people deserve more freedom, not less, and Republicans want to roll that back and rip it away.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whether you believe the polling or not, and surely you have polling of your own about age, that is what your opponents are hammering about President Biden. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  talks about it constantly.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 Well, look, I, I think that our - our opponents are hammering that because they have no platform or nothing else to think about. I'm sure it's much easier for them to talk about age than it is to talk about the fact that they want to rip away a woman's right to choose or take away reproductive freedom from a third of women across this country that are living under a national abortion ban because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I'm sure he'd much rather talk about anything except for that. I'm sure it's, you know, not good for them to talk about an economy that's booming with job creation up, consumer sentiment up, inflation going down. It's much easier for them to talk about age than it is to talk about those things.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 And so, they're gonna do anything and say anything to avoid talking about what's really at stake. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has come out of the gate saying that he pledges to be a dictator on day one, bragging about the fact that he ripped away a woman's right to choose and took away her reproductive freedom. This election is about freedom and democracy and our campaign's gonna stay focused on taking that case directly to American voters. While Republicans continue to try to distract from the fact that they have no platform, their vision for America is one that makes it less safe, and less personal freedom from individuals all across this country. And of course, so of course, they're going to do everything they can to avoid talking about those issues.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well, Mr. Fulks, one of the things you have been talking about in the Biden campaign, you have said we could expect him to restore abortion rights guaranteed by Roe, ban assault weapons, cap the price of insulin for everyone, and forgive billions more in student loan debt. But he's been trying to do that this term. It has not happened. How can you possibly think it will in a second term?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN RUNNING ON SIMILAR CAMPAIGN PROMISES IN 2020\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, look, I would say that the President has forgiven $132 billion in student loan debt for millions of Americans and he has capped the cost of insulin. Look, when the President said that he wants to finish the job, these are the things that he, he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just for Medicare recipients.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 He's established an office, he's established an office for gun violence prevention, and we have to do more to ban assault weapons while Republicans under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continue to say that we need thoughts and prayers when kids are being gunned down in schools. And so, this is a continuation of work that the President has already been doing, and we're gonna continue to push to do more of that, and communicate that to voters.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just a reminder, you'd need 60 Senate votes for abortion access and an assault ban - assault weapons ban. But let's move on. Democrat \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  in Minnesota, the Minnesota congressman who is running a primary challenge to President Biden, said for the first time, he would consider running on the No Labels ticket. Are you concerned about that, that it would take away votes from President Biden?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 Look, Martha, at the end of the day, there is only gonna be two parties that have an ability to get to 270 electoral votes, and that's gonna be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as the Democratic nominee and whoever emerges from the extreme Republican primary that's going on right now. So, you know, look, we're gonna stay focused on the issues and make this about freedom and democracy. The Americans that have the most at stake understand that, and they're not gonna be fooled by anything else.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 There are only two options of who is gonna be the President of the United States and that's either \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or whoever the Republicans put forth. And our campaign's gonna continue to make sure that we're delivering a message to draw that contrast about what this election is all about, and that's restoring democracy and protecting freedoms for millions of Americans across this country.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Recent polls show the campaign is underperforming with Black voters compared to 2020 exit polls. South Carolina Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  has said he is very concerned about this. Vice President Harris, I know you just saw that, told Mary the campaign has responded to the Black community's needs. But if that's the case, why has support for President Biden dipped?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 Look, I think what a lot of people are expressing is urgency. They sense a sense of urgency because of the threat that Republicans pose to, you know, America right now, and that's precisely why the President and the Vice President are running for reelection, to make sure that that doesn't happen. When it comes to African-American voters, I want to be very clear about this, that no administration has done as much for the African-American community as President Biden and Vice President Harris. We're talking about Black wealth being up 60 percent, the racial wealth gap being the lowest as it's ever been in recorded history.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLLS: BIDEN CAMPAIGN UNDERPERFORMING WITH BLACK VOTERS\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 We have to continue to take this message directly to African-American voters, and our campaign has done that. We've come out of the gate with very large buys targeted at African-American voters organizing in their communities to send a clear signal that one, we don't take them for granted. Two, we recognize that we need to earn their support in this campaign and communicate with them all the ways in which this administration has, all the work that this administration has done to make their lives better. And that has to also be in juxtaposition to the contrast of what, you know, the Republicans are putting forward.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 There is going to be a choice in this election. And we have the choice about all the things that I just laid out that President Biden and Vice President Harris have done for the African-American community, and what's on the Republican side, which is ripping away health care, pledging to repeal the ACA that would drive African-American uninsured rates through the roof.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Mr. Fulks?\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS (BIDEN PRINCIPAL DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER)\par \par \uc2 And so, that's the choice that African-American voters are gonna have.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're, we're just about out of time, but I thank you very much for that. We'll see you again. We'll have much more on the 2024 race with the Powerhouse Roundtable. But up next, the top White House national security official responds to concerns about escalating conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020240125ek1l00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REPUBLICANS' FINAL IOWA FACE-OFF HALEY & DESANTIS DEBATE AS CHRISTIE DROPS OUT DAYS BEFORE FIRST VOTES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,104 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE, DESANTIS, 2024 ELECTION, HALEY, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now, though, we're going to begin with the race for the White House. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  head-to-head in Iowa last night in the final debate before the first votes of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICAN'S FINAL IOWA FACE-OFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY & DESANTIS DEBATE AS CHRISTIE DROPS OUT DAYS BEFORE FIRST VOTES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It came hours after \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  announced he's dropping out of the race, hours before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  set to appear in court again. Rachel Scott starts us off in Des Moines. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning to you. That major campaign shake-up coming before the candidates even took the debate stage. Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  dropping out of the race, raising the stakes for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  with just four days to go until the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP-FL) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 ...put our own people first.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE / FMR GOV SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 You're so desperate. You're just so desperate.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  going head-to-head, taking the stage at the Iowa debate just hours after \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  suddenly dropped out of the race.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE / FMR GOV SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 If leadership is about getting things done, how did you blow through $150 million in your campaign and you were down in the polls?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP-FL) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 So, here's a, I think that's very...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE / FMR GOV SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 You are not a manager. I think I hit a nerve.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP-FL) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 This is a great window into leadership because she's focused on a lot of political, process stuff, things that no voter cares about.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Haley and DeSantis are fighting to prove they are the best alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , now battling for second place with the Iowa caucuses just four days away.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP-FL) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 If Trump is the nominee, it's going to be about January 6th, legal issues, criminal trials.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE / FMR GOV SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 His way is not my way. I don't have vengeance. I don't have vendettas.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Haley calling January 6th a terrible day and ripping into Trump's argument in court that presidents should have total immunity from prosecution.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE / FMR GOV SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 That's ridiculous. That's absolutely ridiculous. I mean, we need to use some common sense here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY & DESANTIS FACE OFF IN FINAL IOWA DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICAN HOPEFULS SCRAMBLE TO GAIN GROUND ON TRUMP\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the candidates spent far more time going after each other.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP-FL) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 She's got this problem with ballistic podiatry, shooting herself in the foot, every other day saying things that now she doesn't even take questions from people.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The rivals clashing on education, Social Security, foreign policy, and abortion.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE / FMR GOV SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 These fellas don't know how to talk about abortion. I have said over and over again that Democrats put fear in women on abortion, and Republicans have used judgment. This is too personal of an issue to put fear or judgment.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP-FL) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I think she's been confused on the issue. I think she's trying to speak to different groups with different things.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Not on the stage, the front-runner, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who was doing a separate town hall just a few miles away, slamming DeSantis' six-week abortion ban in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FOUR DAYS TO IOWA CAUCUSES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP HOLDS SEPARATE TOWN HALL AS HALEY & DESANTIS DEBATE\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (REP) (2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 If you talk five or six weeks, a lot of women don't know if they're pregnant in five or six weeks. We're not the radicals. We are not the radicals, but we're living in a time when there has to be a little bit of a concession one way or the other. And I think, you know, I want to get, I want to get it right.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With voting set to begin, the stakes are high as the field only narrows. Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  officially ending his 2024 bid, but issuing a stark warning about Trump.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (REP) (FORMER GOVERNOR NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 I am going to make sure that, in no way, do I enable \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to ever be President of the United States again. And that's more important than my own personal ambition.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  DROPS OUT OF 2024 RACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER NJ GOVERNOR ISSUES STARK WARNING ABOUT TRUMP\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But moments before he took the stage, Christie caught on a hot mic attacking Haley.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (REP) (FORMER GOVERNOR NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 She's going to get smoked. And you and I both know it. She's not up for this.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then saying this about DeSantis. The audio cutting out mid-sentence.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (REP) (FORMER GOVERNOR NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 I talked to, DeSantis called me, petrified that I would...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And leaving this message about Trump for his rivals on his way out.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (REP) (FORMER GOVERNOR NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 Anyone who is unwilling to say that he is unfit to be president of the United States is unfit themselves to be president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sources confirm that DeSantis did speak to Christie. DeSantis wishing Christie well and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  commending him on a hard-fought campaign. We are told, at this point, Christie does not plan to endorse a candidate in this race, but polls have shown that his supporters could turn toward \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in the New Hampshire primary. And I'm told that is something DeSantis advisers were worried about months ago, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it'll be interesting to see if that happens. Rachel, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240125ek1b00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NICOLE EGGERT REVEALS BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS HOW "BAYWATCH" STAR MISSED THE EARLY WARNING SIGNS\b0\par\par\uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXTREME WEATHER ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, WIND, RIVERS, FLIGHTS, FATALITIES\par \par \uc2 TORNADOES, DAMAGE, RESIDENTS, WINDS, INJURIES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, FLOODING, RIVERS, SNOW\par \par \uc2 CAUCUSES, DESANTIS, HALEY, DEBATE, VOTERS, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, COURT, HEARING, IMMUNITY, PAN, SAUER, PEARCE\par \par \uc2 ALASKA FLIGHT, BOEING, CALHOUN, PLUG, MAX 9, \uc2 UN\uc2 , MCCAUL\par \par \uc2 ECUADOR, VIOLENCE, TV, ATTACK, EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 BOTTLED WATER, PARTICLES, PLASTICS, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE SNOW TO COME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOWFALL FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 JUDGE, ATTACK, LASSO, HOLTHUS, COURTROOM, INJURIES\par \par \uc2 SALAS, DANIEL, MURDERED, JUDGES, PROTECTION\par \par \uc2 AI, \uc2 WALMART\uc2 , \uc2 MICROSOFT\uc2 , SHOPPING, MCMILLON, NADELLA\par \par \uc2 RED CROSS, BLOOD DONATIONS, \uc2 NFL\uc2 , SUPER BOWL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOWFALL FORECAST\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN, CANCER, ABSENT, HOSPITALIZED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENSE SECRETARY TREATED FOR PROSTATE CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUSTIN HOSPITALIZED AFTER SURGERY COMPLICATIONS\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA, TEENAGERS, SECURITY, CONTENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTECTING TEENS ON SOCIAL MEDIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: META PUTS IN NEW CONTROLS FOR \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2  & INSTAGRAM\par \par \uc2 NEW ORLEANS PELICANS, \uc2 GOLDEN STATE WARRIORS\uc2 , \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PELICANS & WARRIORS TIP OFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW ORLEANS LOOKS TO IMPROVE PLAYOFF CHANCES\par \par \uc2 RED CROSS, BLOOD, DONATION, SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT CALL FOR BLOOD DONATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 AMERICAN RED CROSS\uc2  ANNOUNCES AN EMERGENCY SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 EGGERT, BREAST CANCER, SYMPTOMS, DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NICOLE EGGERT REVEALS BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW "BAYWATCH" STAR MISSED THE EARLY WARNING SIGNS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We're going to turn to "Baywatch" and "Charles in Charge" star Nicole Eggert. She revealed that she's been diagnosed with breast cancer and how she missed the early warning signs. Eva Pilgrim here with the story. Good morning, Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. Nicole Eggert sharing her recent diagnosis of breast cancer, being incredibly candid, saying, "This journey's been rough." The actress vowing to fight, saying her biggest fear is not being here for her children.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nicole Eggert, known for her roles in "Charles in Charge"...\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "CHARLES IN CHARGE"\par \par \uc2 NICOLE EGGERT (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 You know, the usual dinner, a movie and a dozen roses will be fine.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...and "Baywatch"...\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "BAYWATCH"\par \par \uc2 NICOLE EGGERT (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Well, you better be careful. He's right there in Tower 15.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...is opening up about her battle with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE EGGERT (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 So, I have a little bit of a bomb to drop.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The actress revealing her journey to diagnosis on her podcast, "Perfectly Twisted."\par \par \uc2 NICOLE EGGERT (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I started having a lot of pain on my left side. And it was really hurting, so I did an exam, and I found something.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After a mammogram and multiple biopsies, the actress tells "People" magazine she was eventually diagnosed in December with invasive cribriform carcinoma, a rare type of invasive cancer that accounts for just 0.3 percent to six percent of all breast cancer diagnoses.\par \par \uc2 DR. HATEM SOLIMAN (\uc2 MOFFITT CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 This breast cancer isn't one that is likely to be one that is extremely aggressive or grows very rapidly. It is associated with a high chance of cure and less of a chance of it occurring outside of their breast, with appropriate treatment.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eggert saying, "I have panics where I'm like, just get this out of me," telling people the next step is surgery. "You sit there, and it's in you, and you're like, every second that passes and it's inside of me, it's growing." The 51-year-old says her first symptoms were a rapid, 25-pound weight gain over three months and terrible pain in her breast, which she initially attributed to menopause.\par \par \uc2 DR. HATEM SOLIMAN (\uc2 MOFFITT CANCER CENTER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 If there's a persistent change in the breast, as she was explaining that she had swelling and pain in the breast that was not going away, that should prompt a woman to go seek out the medical attention of her doctors in order to get a workup as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The single mom of two says she's fighting to be there for her daughters, 25-year-old Dylan, and especially her youngest, 12-year-old Keegan.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE EGGERT (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 There's no being depressed. There's no laying around. There's like, it's like enjoy right now today, keep positive.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Nicole says after she found that lump, it took her a month to get an appointment for a mammogram, a good reminder for us all not to wait if something doesn't feel right and to take that appointment, even when it doesn't happen right away, guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A month, that's way too long. Okay, thank you, Eva.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240124ek1a0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT TO HEAR TRUMP APPEAL JUSTICES TO WEIGH TRUMP ELIGIBILITY FOR COLORADO BALLOT\b0\par\par\uc2 538 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2024\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 , ELECTION, SUPREME COURT, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening on this Friday. We do begin with tonight with several breaking stories.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT TO HEAR TRUMP APPEAL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This major winter storm set to slam the east. Also, the massive fire, passenger planes landing through smoke at Newark Airport. But first, tonight, the breaking news from the Supreme Court, an historic announcement. The justices will hear former President Trump's appeal after Colorado Supreme Court decided he should be off the ballot because of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Supreme Court late today saying they will not only hear the case, they will do so relatively quickly when it comes to court's calendar. The justices agreeing to hear arguments on February 8th, just one month from now, suggesting they could decide on this before Super Tuesday, before voters in Colorado join so many other states in voting during this primary.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  leading us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICES TO WEIGH TRUMP ELIGIBILITY FOR COLORADO BALLOT\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, an historic announcement from the Supreme Court with sweeping implications for the 2024 election. The justices revealing they will take up the case of whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can be banned from the ballot in Colorado for engaging in insurrection against the United States January 6th. The court putting the case on an extraordinarily fast track, hearing arguments on February 8th, just over a month from now. A ruling expected shortly thereafter, perhaps in time for Super Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Colorado Supreme Court banned Trump from the primary ballot there citing the 14th amendment, which prohibits any officer of the United States who swore an oath to the Constitution and engaged in insurrection from holding office. Whatever the High Court decides could have an impact far beyond Colorado. There have been similar efforts to keep Trump off the ballot in more than 30 states.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  who's been reporting on this every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the Supreme Court will hear \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's appeal. Of course, this is significant. But also, Jon, there's also news from the court tonight involving abortion. And at issue here is whether a state can make it a crime to actually perform or assist in an abortion in an emergency situation. And what has the court signaled tonight?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, the Supreme Court has temporarily reinstated a near total ban on abortions in the state of Idaho even in the case of medical emergency. Parts of the ban had been blocked by a federal judge because it includes no exceptions for the life of the mother. The Supreme Court says it will hear an appeal on this in April with another potentially landmark abortion ruling coming from the Supreme Court by the end of June. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  on both breaking headlines tonight. Jon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020240109ek1500002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MENOPAUSE RETREATS NEW WELLNESS TREND SUPPORTING WOMEN DURING CHANGE\b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPENING NOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WICKED WEATHER ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, FLOODING, STORM, BIRD STRIKE, FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WICKED WEATHER ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORRENTIAL RAINS TRIGGER FLOODING ALONG EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRAVEL, FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW YEAR'S TRAVEL RUSH UNDERWAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS FLOCK TO AIRPORTS, ROADS FOR HOLIDAY EXODUS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, JOHNSTON, ADAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOWDOWN OVER THE BORDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAYORS DEMAND FEDERAL HELP AMID MIGRANT ARRIVALS\par \par \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , WAR, ISRAEL, HEZBOLLAH, DRONE ATTACKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING FEARS OF A WIDER WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL'S NEW WARNING AMID CONFLICT WITH HEZBOLLAH ON NORTHERN BORDER\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, BENSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP WINS COURT VICTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHIGAN SUPREME COURT: TRUMP CAN STAY ON BALLOT\par \par \uc2 RESPIRATORY ILLNESS, RSV\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ON RSV RISK FOR CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESEARCHERS, KIDS MORE LIKELY TO BE HOSPITALIZED FOR RSV THAN FLU, COVID\par \par \uc2 REUM, RESCUE, GARCIA, EDWARDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON MIRACLE RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAN SPEAKS OUT AFTER BEING TRAPPED IN TRUCK FOR NEARLY A WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 DEMOLITION, GRAY, FERENTINO\par \par \uc2 FRANKE, HILDEBRANDT, GUILTY PLEA, TERRY\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL, PETTERSON, CHAPPELEAR, WORLD CRUISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 3.5-YEAR CRUISE TO SET SAIL?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INSIDE TREND OF LONG-VOYAGE CRUISING\par \par \uc2 SMOTHERS, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, HOLIDAY, STORM, RAIN, FOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WICKED WEATHER ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRACKING THE PATH OF CROSS-COUNTRY STORM\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRAVEL, HOLIDAY, RAIN, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY TRAVEL HAVOC?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CROSS-COUNTRY STORM ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 DIET, WEIGHT LOSS, STUDY, HARVARD, LOW-CARB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY ABOUT LOW CARB DIETS & WEIGHT LOSS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY THE QUALITY OF INGREDIENTS MATTERS SO MUCH\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS, "JEOPARDY!", CLUE, GAME SHOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEMBER OF THE GMA TEAM A CLUE ON "JEOPARDY!"\par \par \uc2 MELLENCAMP, MELANOMA, CANCER, "REAL HOUSEWIVES"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEDDI MELLENCAMP OPENS UP ABOUT MELANOMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER REAL HOUSEWIVES STAR TALKS ABOUT CANCER\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, RETREAT, WELLNESS, HEALTH, TREND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE RETREATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW WELLNESS TREND SUPPORTING WOMEN DURING CHANGE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now a new trend in the wellness world, menopause retreats, where women gather with health professionals to learn more about the physical and emotional changes they are going through. But not all doctors are sold on this idea. Reena Roy is here with details. Good morning to you, Reena.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Eva, good morning to you. Well, menopause is something that affects millions of women around the world each year. But even though it is so widely experienced, experts say there is a major lack of education around it. This growing trend is hoping to change that, so women can enter this new phase of life better prepared.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "SEX AND THE CITY"\par \par \uc2 ACTOR ("SEX AND THE CITY")\par \par \uc2 I think I'm finally in menopause.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR ("SEX AND THE CITY")\par \par \uc2 Welcome to the club. Prepare to be sweaty.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) From hot flashes to weight gain, mood swings or maybe no symptoms at all, menopause is different for everyone.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAUREN STREICHER (OBGYN)\par \par \uc2 Unlike when girls first get their period, and their mother sits them down to say, "Okay, honey, this is what to expect," no one does the what to expect when your estrogen levels take a plunge. So, women are blindsided when it happens to them.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Enter menopause retreats, a wellness trend recently growing in popularity, like this one at Canyon Ranch in Massachusetts, hosted by Indi Garay. (PH) It looks and feels like a spa getaway, but the main focus, learning how to navigate midlife and menopause.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING SUPPORT WHILE ON MENOPAUSE RETREATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN PRIORITIZING THEIR PHYSICAL AND EMOTIONAL WELLBEING\par \par \uc2 ATTENDEE (CANYON RANCH)\par \par \uc2 I think we go over that bridge later.\par \par \uc2 KATE CHAPMAN (RETREAT ATTENDEE)\par \par \uc2 One of the highlights was being with a group of like-minded women, who were all there because they wanted to prioritize their health and well-being and have the attention for three days of this world-class doctor, who treated us all like her own patients.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Robin Noble, a certified menopause provider and OB/GYN leading the retreat with group sessions and private consultations about hormonal changes and how exactly they impact things like mood and sleep, plus bone, heart and sexual health.\par \par \uc2 DR ROBIN NOBLE (RETREAT HOST)\par \par \uc2 Things are really different for different people. This is the way, at some level, to kind of bring women together, allow them to talk about their individual symptoms, answer some questions, have a deeper understanding of what the rest of their life and what their health risks might look like.\par \par \uc2 KATE CHAPMAN (RETREAT ATTENDEE)\par \par \uc2 It was just really nice to have an opportunity to hear other people's experiences, and you felt more connected to the experience yourself.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But some doctors warn of getaways that promise miracle cures for menopause symptoms and encourage women to do their research.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME DOCTORS QUESTION MENOPAUSE RETREATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARY OF MISINFORMATION BEING SPREAD, ENCOURAGE RESEARCH\par \par \uc2 DR. LAUREN STREICHER (OBGYN)\par \par \uc2 Like everything else out there on social media, there's a lot of, number one, misinformation and, number two, a lot of people that are making a lot of money. The most important thing is to see who's running them and who's speaking.\par \par \uc2 REENA ROY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And most of these retreats do come with a hefty price tag, but there are a lot of other resources out there. Experts recommend the \uc2 North American Menopause Society\uc2 , where you can not only find useful information but also menopause practitioners who can help answer your questions. Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thanks, Reena.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240109ejcs0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN BIDEN: I WANT ISRAEL TO FOCUS 'ON HOW TO SAVE CIVILIAN LIVES'\b0\par\par\uc2 2,052 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 HALEY, SUNUNU, ENDORSEMENT, TRUMP, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 , ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , BIDEN, NETANYAHU, IRAN, HOUTHIS, UKRAINE, BORDER\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Do you want Israel to scale back its assault on Gaza? And by the end of the year, do you want them to tone it down, move to a lower intensify phase?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I want them to be focused on how to save civilian lives, not stop going after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but be more careful.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISRAEL/\uc2 HAMAS\uc2  WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: I WANT ISRAEL TO FOCUS 'ON HOW TO SAVE CIVILIAN LIVES'\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was President Biden this week responding to how Israel is carrying out the war in Gaza. For the latest, let's bring in Democratic Maryland Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , a member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. So, Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2 , you heard President Biden's message quite clear there. Jake Sullivan was obviously in Israel delivering a similar message. Now we've learned overnight that Defense Secretary Austin is on his way to the region. Is the message getting through?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I don't think it's getting through clearly enough because we continue to see unacceptably high levels of civilian casualties. And when it comes to the humanitarian crisis, we still have a near total siege. Now, as of today, there may be some progress, Israel finally opening the Kerem Shalom Crossing, which is an important crossing. It shouldn't take this long. Look, President Biden has been absolutely right to stand with Israel in the objective of ending the military threat from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , no more October 7ths.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 But he's also right that how Israel conducts this operation, how the Netanyahu government conducts this operation is important. And that is why it's really essential that these issues be addressed. And they're not being addressed sufficiently enough to my mind.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: ISRAEL LOSING SUPPORT DUE TO 'INDISCRIMINATE BOMBING'\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We also heard him this week in a, in a closed event, but, but say that Israel was doing indiscriminate bombing, indiscriminate bombing, and, and this was risking them losing support. That, that's not a phrase that I've heard others in the administration repeat or him repeat. Is that what is, Israel has been doing? 'Cause that, I mean, indiscriminate bombing, depending on how you define it, is potentially a war crime.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, Jon, look, here's the bottom line, we do have unacceptably high levels of civilian casualties. We see very loose rules of engagement, way looser than anything the United States would exercise. We would not drop a 2,000-pound bomb on a refugee camp to target a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commander. Just yesterday, snipers fired into a church compound killing a mom and her daughter without warning. So, it is very important because the United States is not a bystander to this. We are a big supplier, of course, of military assistance...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 ...to Israel, so we are with them entirely in the objective of ending the military threat. But again, we need to make sure that our values are reflected in this so long as we are providing all of this equipment.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NETANYAHU: 'PROUD TO HAVE PREVENTED' PALESTINIAN STATE\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, when you're facing - an enemy that works within the civilian population, hides, has bases beneath hospitals, it's very difficult. But I want to ask you about something Netanyahu said overnight in a press conference. He said, "I am proud to have prevented the establishment of a Palestinian state because today everybody knows that that Palestinian state could've, and what that Palestinian state could've been after we saw a little Palestinian state in Gaza." So, there you have the Prime Minister of Israel saying, "I am proud to have prevented the establishment of a Palestinian state."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He also, by the way, continues to say he is opposed to the idea of the Palestinian authority taking control of Gaza once this military operation is over. What, what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, this is a, a, a direct, you know, response to, to President Biden calling for a two-state solution. Ultimately, it's the only political settlement that's viable. And here's the thing about Prime Minister Netanyahu, he has continued to weaken the Palestinian authority. This is the organization that recognized Israel's right to exist decades and decades ago. Instead of trying to find peace or at least preventing the conditions on the ground from changing with additional settlements to allow a two-state solution, he, he has shut the door on that effort. Meanwhile, as you've seen, there have been lots of reports that Prime Minister Netanyahu actually, you know, supported Qatar's efforts to finance \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDE BETWEEN WHITE HOUSE, NETANYAHU APPEARS TO DEEPEN\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 ...in, in...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this is a fundamental disagreement about something that isn't trivial. It's what happens after this is over.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 This is...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The United States is saying, the path has to be towards a - Palestinian state, and the Palestinians have to run Gaza when this is over, and Netanyahu is saying the opposite.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Right, and this is where it's really important that President Biden assert leadership in this moment, in this very dark hour. If there's gonna be any light at the end of the tunnel, you're going to have to have a political settlement, not with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Obviously, they're not any partner for peace. We need to end the military threat from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But you need to have Palestinians who have long accepted Israel's right to exist, to be able to govern and represent the people within, within the Palestinian area.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And let me ask you another thing. As again, Defense Secretary Austin is on his way to Israel, we see that overnight US and British forces shot down more than a dozen drones over the Red Sea, drones that were, you know, sent by the Houthis, which are supported by the Iranians. How worried are you that this is about to escalate, and, and actually, I mean, should it escalate in terms of the United States actually attacking these Houthi positions in Yemen?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US SHOOTS DOWN MORE DRONES FROM IRAN-BACKED HOUTHIS\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I worry a lot about escalation both in the Red Sea area with, with the Houthis, and as you say, we shot down their drones. We also have to keep an eye on the northern front with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . This is why...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 ...why President Biden sent two aircraft carriers, groups to the region to prevent that kind of escalation, to send a message to Iran, to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and others. So, look, I think that the administration has been smart in terms of how it's responded to those attacks, but, but clearly the risk of an American being killed in the process, it is high, and that would obviously create a, a, a big response from the United States.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There have been reports and certainly there have also been calls for the United States to take that - that, that proactive action and not just shoot the drones as they're on their way towards potentially our warships, but to go after the Houthis in Yemen. Again, this is an Iranian-backed group that is consistently sending, you know, attacking US positions in the region. I mean, should, should the United States take them out?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, there are also reports that that's exactly what Iran would like to see happen. There are also reports that that's what Putin would like to see happen, have the...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because it creates a bigger conflict.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 ...have the United States get bogged down in the Red Sea, and that means that we get, we get distracted. But to answer your question, I clearly, I mean, if, if US forces are, are killed, you're gonna get a big, big response from the United States.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Before you go, I want to also ask about these negotiations that are going on over the weekend on, on a deal to fund, you know, fund for, funds for Israel, funds for Ukraine, including changes in border policy. Do you have a sense of where these negotiations are?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESSIONAL CLASHES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE CONTINUES NEGOTIATIONS ON FOREIGN AID, BORDER POLICY\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, they've been very tight-lipped, understandably, about where they are, but they say they're making progress. I hope that's the case.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and what do you think of this idea of, of having significant changes to the border tied to funding for Ukraine and Israel? I mean, among the changes that Republicans have been demanding are changes to our asylum laws, making it harder for people to declare asylum, restricting that. And even, you know, Republicans want a return to Remain in Mexico, that, that the policy of the Trump, which is ask for asylum before you come to the United States and come after or if it's been granted.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, I think it's essential that we provide military assistance to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROGRESSIVES BLAST 'EXTREME' REPUBLICAN BORDER POLICY STANCE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 This is a pivotal moment in American leadership and history. And we need to make sure that we help our Ukrainian friends against Putin's aggression not just to protect their freedom, but because it would send a terrible signal around the world to our allies who would no longer trust us, and to our adversaries who would be emboldened if we're not doing that. In terms of border security, I have to look at the details, and the big question, Jon, is who's at the table on the Republican side? I don't mean the individual. But are they really working with the President to try to get border security? Because the President has proposed historical increases in resources for border security.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPEAKER JOHNSON: BORDER CHANGES ARE GOP'S 'HILL TO DIE ON'\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. All right. And they're asking...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 That's...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they're asking for policy changes now more than resources. Senator...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 So, we have to look at it, you know?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (DEM-MD) (FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new reporting by ABC's Diane Sawyer and Rachel Scott on the challenges facing many women navigating state abortion restrictions. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Rachel Scott is here to discuss her extraordinary reporting on the human cost for many women navigating state abortion restrictions. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020231229ejch00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NIKKI HALEY/GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU NEW HAMPSHIRE GOV. SUNUNU ENDORSES HALEY FOR PRESIDENT\b0\par\par\uc2 3,954 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 HALEY, SUNUNU, ENDORSEMENT, TRUMP, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This week, one candidate hoping to break that historical trend, Nikki Haley, secured the endorsement of New Hampshire's popular Governor, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 . It's just the latest sign of momentum for the former South Carolina governor who also served as \uc2 UN\uc2  ambassador in the Trump administration. Big dollars have also started flowing Haley's way as she seeks to become the leading alternative to Trump. I traveled to New Hampshire to sit down with Haley and Sununu as they team up to try to catch the frontrunner. As you'll hear, Haley is trying to walk a fine line, taking on Trump, but not alienating his supporters, at times attacking the former president, at other times, praising him, sometimes in the same sentence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE YOUR VOTE 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW HAMPSHIRE GOV. SUNUNU ENDORSES HALEY FOR PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor Haley, this was the endorsement that everybody not named Trump was trying to get. So what does it mean?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's huge. You know, first to have the endorsement of the largest Conservative freedom-loving grassroots organization in the country with \uc2 Americans for Prosperity\uc2 , and then go get the endorsement of the live free or die governor, I mean, it doesn't get any better than that. I mean, it's rock solid. And, you know, we're focused on, okay, how do we make it a live free or die country? How do we put the people back in charge? And Chris is such a great partner. We've had a great time. We've won so many rooms and, you know, we're just getting started.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How are you gonna help her win New Hampshire? Because, I mean, obviously right now, Trump still has a huge lead. He's had a huge lead for a long time.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Well, look, no one, no one in New Hampshire's gonna vote for Nikki Haley just 'cause the Governor says so, right? You earn it. I think we've been successful knowing how to earn it, how to engage with constituencies, what's important to, that not just what the voters want to hear, but how they want to ask the questions, and most importantly, how are they gonna earn the trust with, with folks? We can all agree on a lot of policies. We wanna know that as individuals we come first. And that's exactly what Nikki's bringing to the table.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 5 WEEKS UNTIL 1ST-IN-THE-NATION NEW HAMPSHIRE PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What are the, are the stakes here? How important is this primary, not just New Hampshire, the Republican primary?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I think this election is important for Republicans and Democrats. I mean, you see Democrats are just as worried. You've got 75 percent of America that say they don't want a Trump/Biden rematch. And so, there's a lot at stake for our country. And I think for Republicans, they're looking at the fact that, look, we've got to get this back on track, and they like the idea of a new generational leader. You know, I agree with a lot of Trump's policies. I think he was the right president at the right time. But looking at the situation now, our country's in disarray, the world is on fire, and chaos follows him. And we can't have a country in chaos for four more years or we won't survive it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY ON TRUMP: 'THE WORLD IS ON FIRE & CHAOS FOLLOWS HIM'\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I hear you say that a lot, chaos follows him, but is it chaos follows him or does he create the chaos?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 What...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, that sounds so passive, he, chaos follows him.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, I, rightly or wrongly, you call it whatever you want to call it, but when you feel it, it's chaos. When I tell these rooms that, they all nod their head, they get it. It's the chaos, and Americans are tired. They want government to work for them again, and they want to win. And you look at these general election polls and Biden and Trump are head to head. It's gonna to be another nail-biter. I defeat Biden by 17 points. That's a total mandate.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you said, Governor, nobody's gonna vote for Nikki Haley because you said to vote for her.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Maybe some people, but not a lot. But your track record on endorsements in the midterms, the number of candidates you've endorsed lost to much more Trump-centered candidates. I'm old enough to remember when your father endorsed \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in 2000, (inaudible) McCain, and winning by 18 points.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, how's, why is this gonna be different?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 We have 40 days. We're gonna be out on the trail and we're gonna be helping make that grassroots support, helping build folks up. But right now there's so many folks undecided. Everything's been delayed. A lot of the decisions won't be made really until the last couple of weeks. And in New Hampshire, it's all about results. So, if you're results-driven, you want that efficiency in government, here's the path not just to get the Republican Party unified again, but the entire country. Give them a strong, next generation of Conservative leadership that everybody can get behind.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And what I appreciate about Chris' endorsement, it's not a one and done. This, for the next 40 days, we're partners because we honestly believe we have a country to save and we're determined to do it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let, let me ask you, speaking of Trump, he has claimed absolute immunity in his defense to the election interference case. Is that your view? Do you believe a president has absolute immunity for anything that happens while they're president?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY DEFERS TO COURTS ON TRUMP'S IMMUNITY CLAIMS\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm gonna let the courts figure that out. I mean, the last thing you're gonna see me do is weigh in or learn the details about any of his court cases because I can't follow 91 charges and I'm not going to.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That makes, that makes total sense. But let me just ask you on the principle. Forget his case, do you think that a president of the United States, that if you get elected president, you would have absolute immunity for anything you did while you were president?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think the court issues are, do you have immunity when you're president, when you're not president? At what point does that line fall? I'm gonna let judges decide that. I don't know where the line falls. President Trump's gonna have to defend himself no matter what. If he's found guilty, he's found guilty. If he's found innocent, he's found innocent. It would be wasted energy for me to sit there and focus on court cases and not focus on how to win that room that we just left.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 'Cause, 'cause that sounds a lot like saying you're above the law, that a president's above the law, that you can do whatever you want.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, he can answer for himself. I am not in a court case. I'm happy I don't have to answer for that. So...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 ...let him answer it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICO, TEXT OF QUOTE\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to ask you about something you said not long after January 6th. I'm gonna read the quote. You said, "We need to acknowledge he let us down," meaning Trump. "He went down a path he shouldn't have, and we shouldn't have followed him, and we shouldn't have listened to him, and we can't let that ever happen again." Do you still feel that way?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY REFLECTS ON JAN. 6 CAPITOL RIOTS, TRUMP'S ROLE\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Of course. I don't ever want that to happen again. I think January 6th was a terrible day, and I think that the tone at the top matters. On so many levels, the tone at the top matters. Look, anti-Trumpers think I don't hate Trump enough. Pro-Trumpers think I don't love him enough. I call it like I see it. If I agree with you on something, I'm gonna say it. If I disagree with you on something, I'm gonna say it. At the end of the day, people want the truth. They want to know what I think. I tell them what it is, and we let the chips fall where they may. That's what I felt about that situation on January 6th. I hope we never have an event like that again.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you said, "We shouldn't have followed him and we shouldn't have listened to him. We can't ever let that happen again."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think the part, the problem was, and it, what everybody, so many of his friends and family and everybody saw is, you had good people who went there to support him. You had good people that were there at the rallies, and then you had people who broke the law, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY: TRUMP WAS 'THE RIGHT PRESIDENT AT THE RIGHT TIME'\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 There's a difference. Don't group everybody together. There's a difference. But when President Trump had the opportunity to stop it, when he had the opportunity to say it, the bully pulpit matters, people listen, he didn't. And I, and I hate that for the people that were there supporting him. I hate that for those of us that were watching it. But what I do know is he was the right president at the right time. But the situation we're on, in now, nobody wants that chaos again. That's what we're trying to get past. My approach is different. No drama, no vendettas, no whining.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) At, at one of your town halls this week, there was a voter that stood up and said...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You really need to turn it up on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that he attempted a coup on national TV. You need to call it what it is. You need to go after him hard.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's interesting because I just heard you say that he was the right president for the right time. What, what, what do you say to voters like that, that say that you really need to draw that line?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Because I answered...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You have drawn it at times and other times you say he was the right president for the right time...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 You're...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...he's fit for office.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're one of those, too. Y'all want me to either love him or hate him all the time.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No. I'm just asking you to respond to a New Hampshire voter. I mean...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 So, I did.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And I responded to him in every way. And what I said to them was, anti-Trumpers want me to hate him. Pro-Trumpers want me to love him. But this is where I stand, there are things I agreed with the President on. I had a good working relationship with him. There are things I don't agree. I don't agree with the fact that, yes, we had a good economy while he was there, but he put us $8 trillion in debt that our kids are never gonna forgive us for. I don't agree with how he handles national security. He focused on trade with China, but he did nothing about the fentanyl flow.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 He did nothing about the fact that fentanyl has killed so many of our Americans. I don't think you should praise \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . I don't think you should criticize Netanyahu when Israel's down on our knees. I don't think you should congratulate the Chinese Communist party on their 70th anniversary. I think that when it comes to national security, we don't praise thugs. We let them know where we stand, and we let them know there'll be hell to pay if they do anything against us. That's who I am.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, he's running on retribution. He wants to go out, and he talks about annihilating his enemies and using the criminal justice system to do so. What, what do you, what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 You guys are exhausting. You're exhausting in your obsession with him. The thing is the normal people aren't obsessed with Trump like you guys are. The normal people care about the fact that they can't afford things. They feel like their freedoms are being taken away. They think government's too big. I know y'all want to talk about every single word he says and every single tweet he does. That's exactly why we need a new generational leader, because people don't wanna hear about every word a person says or every tweet. They want to know how you fought for them that day, and they want to know how their life is going to be different. And life would be a whole lot different if the media would stop this obsession with Trump.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, I was just asking about his central campaign theme which is, I want, you know, I am your retribution, and he's winning in the polls. That's why I'm asking. It's not an obsession.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Well, it, it's like, you know, one thing...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm asking about the leading candidate that you're running against.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. He does everything he can to not talk about issues.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 He almost acts like he wasn't there, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 He doesn't want to talk about building the wall and securing the southern border 'cause he didn't do it. He doesn't wanna talk about fiscal responsibility because he made a hard promise that he would do it in that debate. "I'm gonna be the most fiscally responsible president this country's ever seen," he said. He...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Balance the budget.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, didn't even try to balance the budget. And you know, the thing that, for someone in New Hampshire where, you know, it's not about big government, we love the idea that he was gonna drain the swamp. That was an amazing opportunity, didn't even try. I mean, literally didn't even try. So, if he talks about those issues, he has to kind of own those failures, so he's always gonna talk about retribution and just kind of try to spur something up.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And if you wanna talk about Trump, if you really wanna talk about Trump, why don't you go ask him if he's gonna get on a debate stage in Iowa where Iowa's voting? Why don't you go ask him if he's gonna get on a debate stage in New Hampshire...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, no, that's a good one.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 ...where Granite Staters are voting? That's what you should be asking as the media, not asking about what he happened to say today.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HALEY WEIGHS IN ON TEXAS SUPREME COURT ABORTION DENIAL\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a good question, and which we have asked. But let me move on. We had the Texas Supreme Court abortion case, really kind of a tragic case. Kate Cox, the Supreme Court ruled that she could not have an abortion even though her doctor said that her health was in danger, it might jeopardize her ability to have children in the future, that the baby, that the fetus was almost certainly not going to survive. Did you, do you disagree with that decision by this Texas Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think it, it is the right thing that unelected justices no longer decide this, and it's in the hands of the people. I appreciate that Texas went more on the pro-life side, but as we go through this, listen, my heart broke for her because I had trouble having my children. These, the states are now gonna have to look at these because what we don't want to see is a woman with a rare condition having to carry a baby until term...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But let me ask you really directly, do you think the Supreme Court in Texas made the wrong decision? \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  has come out and said it was the wrong decision and he's criticized you for not giving a direct answer. Can you give a direct answer now? Was that the wrong decision?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Supreme Court said what, that the law that the state put was, was the one that they had to follow, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Supreme Court said that she could not have the abortion. That was the rule.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Right. So, that's when a state corrects itself and says, how do we make sure that doesn't happen again? We tweak things all the time as governors.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, it was the, it was the right decision by the Court?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, the Court had to follow the law. The law said that she couldn't have the abortion. Now it's up to the legislature in Texas to say, how do we make sure there are no more Kates that go through that? That's what you look at. As a governor, you don't just say, this is golden. As a governor, when something happens that churns your stomach, that says that's not what this was intended to be, you go back and say, okay, what do we do to make sure that, that, that we are saving as many babies as possible, but also supporting as many moms as possible? It's not as cut and dry as everybody wants, but states will self-correct to this. That's what they do.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 But, yeah, and, and I think to Nikki's point, it's in the law, right? The Court...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The problem was the law. The problem was not the court's decision. Is that (inaudible) ?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 The Court's, the Court's only, only decision is, is, is the law, are they following the law?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 To interpret the law.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 So, I think what Nikki is saying is exactly right is...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, a lower court ruled one way, the Supreme Court ruled the other way.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 And what Nikki is saying is that the, if there's a problem there, it's in the law, and that's up to the citizens and the, and the lawmakers and the, and the legislature. That's how states do it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you very much. I've got one more question for you. I, I heard the Governor say, when you win New Hampshire...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you going to win New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 My goal is to be strong in Iowa, strong in New Hampshire, strong in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But do you, you, you need to win somewhere, right? I mean, what...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're saying that, what I'm saying is, why don't we try and do the best we can in every state...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 ...and let the people decide which way this goes? I think I'm gonna be strong in Iowa. I think I'm gonna be strong in - in New Hampshire. I think we're gonna be strong in South Carolina, and I think we're gonna take it. And we're not settling for anything else.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you can be more direct about New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUNUNU: NEW HAMPSHIRE 'AN ABSOLUTE WIN' FOR HALEY\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, it's an absolute win. No, it's, it's a win and a reset button. If everyone that could vote in the primary comes out and votes, not only she's gonna win in a landslide, and, and that's not an exaggeration. So, you're gonna see a record...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's setting the bar for you here. The expectation...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 No, it's, it's not an expectation.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 He knows his state better than I do.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 No, it's not an expectation, it's people getting excited.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP) (PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 It's exciting. We can feel it on the ground. We're gonna do this.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Nikki Haley and \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  for that interview. When we come back, the IDF mistakenly kills three Israeli hostages as they face pressure over the handling of the assault on Gaza. We'll talk to Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  about the war and the battle over funding for Israel and Ukraine. We are back in two minutes.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020231229ejch00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW REPORTING HIGHLIGHTS IMPACT OF RESTRICTIVE ABORTION LAWS TEXAS ABORTION LAWS IN SPOTLIGHT AFTER ROE'S REVERSAL\b0\par\par\uc2 1,612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 HALEY, SUNUNU, ENDORSEMENT, TRUMP, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , BIDEN, NETANYAHU, IRAN, HOUTHIS, UKRAINE, BORDER\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, BAN, \uc2 COX\uc2 , NELSON, THOPPIL\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM DECEMBER 6\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (ASKED TX COURTS FOR ABORTION EXCEPTION)\par \par \uc2 We're, we're grieving, you know, the loss of, of a child. There's no outcome here, you know, that results in us taking home a healthy baby girl. So, it's, it's hard. It's overwhelming. And I could see in this case, surely I would qualify for, for that exemption. So, my doctor told me that I did not have that option in the state of Texas. I was, I was shocked.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was Kate Cox, the woman at the center of the Texas abortion access case, speaking with senior congressional correspondent Rachel Scott last week. The Texas Supreme Court blocked her from terminating her high-risk pregnancy, leading her to leave the state to get an abortion. I'll speak with Rachel in a moment about her reporting with our Diane Sawyer on the impact of abortion restrictions on women across the country. It's part of a new special, "Impact by Nightline: On the Brink," now streaming on Hulu. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM "IMPACT BY NIGHTLINE"\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You are looking at a family making a desperate race against time, leaving their home for another state to get care for a dire pregnancy complication. Just like so many families in 21 states caught in a web of confusing state laws and medical restrictions, they are searching the Internet and calling, hoping the find medical care in states where doctors can make decisions with their patients, not under the threat of government intervention.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Well, and instead of being able to grieve the news that we've just received, we have to plan travel to a foreign place we don't want to be in in the first place, spending money we don't want to spend...\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 That we don't (inaudible) have.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 ...or may not have to go do something to care of ourselves.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We're like medical refugees in the United States.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And some lawmakers are narrowing the possibilities, some counties in Texas and Idaho making it illegal to use their highways to cross the border to end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I just felt hunted. The entire time I felt like I was gonna get pulled out of TSA, and then asked, you know, what, what was I doing? Why, why had I gone to Colorado? I...\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's so terrifying.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I was terrified.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is this one country with two different health care systems playing out?\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 More than multiple. Multiple health care (inaudible)...\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) And it's one where our lives and our health are debatable, where they're up for committee review.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And in others where you can actually get access to care.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAP, ABORTIONS MONTHLY INCREASE\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the states bordering Texas alone, look at the increase in patients coming to them for treatment. A lot of clinics now have long wait lists.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The woman in the front row is Blair Nelson from Austin, Texas, and she paid a huge price for the delay it took her to find care. She discovered her son had a rare, deadly condition very early on at just 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Every single one of my son's organs were growing outside of his body, including his heart, everything. But the heart was still beating outside of his body, and I couldn't even get the care.\par \par \uc2 DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And the heartbeat, it's such nonsense. Like, we would know right here that if one of us had no brain with a heartbeat, you're not living.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It would take Blair a few months to find a clinic in another state that would fit her in. It was during those days that were ticking by, doctors suspect she was unknowingly developing a potentially life-threatening complication called placenta accreta. At the clinic having the procedure, she started bleeding.\par \par \uc2 BLAIR NELSON (DOCUMENTARY PARTICIPANT)\par \par \uc2 I just remember laying there and feeling a ton of liquid leave my body, and I was hemorrhaging. I was taken out of the back door and my husband had to rush me to the hospital because I was at risk of needing a blood transfusion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is Blair's OB/GYN, Dr. John Thoppil. He says, if she had been able to get care back home in Texas, this would not have happened.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JOHN THOPPIL (OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 That month delay allowed for this abnormal sort of process where you have this placenta invading, you know, into her, into her uterus. She went through something that I don't think she would have had to if she didn't have this one-month delay in care.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctors performed an emergency procedure to save her uterus, but now future pregnancies are extremely risky. Thoppil asks, is this really what Texas lawmakers want?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Listening to this nightmare that she had to experience, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JOHN THOPPIL (OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 The policies of Texas may have kept her from ever having a kid again. The irony of all these laws that have been put in place, you know, to, to supposedly protect the sanctity of life, we might have stopped the ability to create life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION IN AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORTING HIGHLIGHTS IMPACT OF RESTRICTIVE ABORTION LAWS\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Rachel joins me now. Rachel, incredible, heartbreaking reporting. You'd been working on this story with Diane Sawyer for months.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Long before the Kate Cox story came to light, but what, what comes across here is that is not an unusual situation.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Kate Cox is not an anomaly in this situation. We had 18 women across ten different states all in one room. They were mostly complete strangers. They had never met anybody that went through something similar. And for every woman that was there, there was another woman who wrote to us who told us that they went through something similar, but they either couldn't come, the trauma was too much for them to want to speak out about their experience. But these doctors were sounding the alarm. They told us this is happening to women all across the country in states with complete abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the story we just heard there of Blair is also in Texas...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...like Kate Cox. Why is it that Texas seems to be where so much of this has happened?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAWS IN SPOTLIGHT AFTER ROE'S REVERSAL\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And you may remember long before Roe versus Wade was overturned, you had Texas impose the strictest abortion ban in the country. It's one of two states where doctors can face up to life in prison. They could lose their medical license. They could face extraordinary fines. And so, what it has created is this chilling effect. And I asked Dr. John Thoppil if he felt like he could have a direct and honest conversation with his patient. He told me, no. He told me that many doctors fear even discussing terminations even if it means that it could possibly save their patients' fertility or protect their own life.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let, let me move to the political context...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...because obviously, as deeply personal each one of these cases are, there's also a larger political context.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is your sense of how front a center the abortion issue is gonna be in 2024?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is going to be critical, and we are already seeing it play out there on the campaign trail in presidential politics, but also, this now growing push to get abortion on the ballot in critical states. I'm gonna have my eye on Florida, as well as Arizona, to see if it ends up on the ballot there. You have many advocates that are looking at what happened in Kansas. They're looking at what happened in Ohio. And they're saying that, even in some of these Conservative states, you have voters that want to at least protect the right to an abortion or at least give women, like the ones that you just heard of, a choice.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS GALVANIZING VOTERS IN POST-ROE WORLD\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Rachel Scott, again, really powerful, excellent reporting. Thank you for sharing it with us here on "This Week."\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks, Jon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Powerhouse Roundtable is up next. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020231229ejch00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BATTLE OVER ABORTION ACCESS WOMEN, DOCTORS GRAPPLE WITH STATE LAWS POST-ROE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,124 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, EDWARDS, MADDREY\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERFUL CROSS-COUNTRY STORM\par \par \uc2 JETBLUE, COLLISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUNWAY CLOSE CALL INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , KIRBY, NETANYAHU, BIDEN, AMI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. URGES ISRAEL TO SCALE BACK WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, HALEY, LAKEY, CHRISTIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here in the US, and to the very personal issue of abortion. The case making national headlines. The young pregnant wife, her health in danger, and the Texas state lawmakers and that state's Supreme Court making the decision for her, blocking her abortion in Texas. Now, presidential candidates are being asked about this. Tonight, Diane Sawyer and Rachel Scott with women who were pregnant and faced health risks, too, and told they could not have an abortion as well. So, who should decide this? Here's Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the young pregnant wife from Texas. Her doctors saying her health was in danger, that her baby had little chance of survival. But she was blocked by the Texas Supreme Court from getting an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN, DOCTORS GRAPPLE WITH STATE LAWS POST-ROE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This deeply personal struggle now forcing candidates for president to address how they feel about Kate Cox's ordeal. Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (REP-NJ) (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE/FORMER GOVERNOR)\par \par \uc2 It deems to me this should have been a pretty easy decision and the Texas Supreme Court got it wrong.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley suggesting a female president might be better at this saying, "I don't think the fellas have known how to talk about it," telling our own \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  today, it's up to Texas to change the law.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (REP-SC) (2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE/FORMER GOVERNOR)\par \par \uc2 Now, it's up to the legislature in Texas to say, how do we make sure there are no more Kates that go through that?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Diane Sawyer and I sat down with 18 women from ten different states who faced life-threatening emergencies and severe complications with non-viable pregnancies, but were denied access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is this a debate over abortion or is it a debate over health care?\par \par \uc2 MOTHERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Health care. Health care.\par \par \uc2 MOTHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's a debate over human rights.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We also spoke with doctors like Amelia Huntsberger (PH) in Idaho who told Diane, the law's exception to save the life of the mother is vague and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Is 25% chance of death, is that, is that enough? Does she have to have a 75% chance of death? Who gets to decide? Do you want the government making your medical decisions?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) These are some of the state politicians around the country who supported the abortion restrictions currently in place. There are over 2,000 of them. They are overwhelmingly male. In Idaho, abortion is banned outright except in cases of rape and incest and to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes or no, the health of the woman is irrelevant? Yes or no.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR TODD LAKEY (REP) (IDAHO)\par \par \uc2 I would say it weighs less, yes, than the life of the child.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But to Dr. Huntsberger, it's all too much.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It feels like state-sanctioned suffering.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This summer, Dr. Huntsberger and her husband, who is also a doctor, decided they were no longer able to take care of their patients under these conditions and they moved out of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I had the privilege of serving a community that I love for more than ten years. It's been really hard, and I had don't have any regrets about the decision that we've made.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Doctors that we spoke to told us that the more state lawmakers and the courts make decisions about their patients' health, the harder it is to care for them. In fact, in states with complete abortion bans, there has been a significant drop off of OB-GYN doctors who want to practice there, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This has started a real conversation all over again. Rachel Scott with us tonight. And we should point out that Rachel is joining Diane Sawyer for this in-depth report, what so many women are now facing. The report on "Impact x Nightline" called "On the Brink."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "IMPACT BY NIGHTLINE", "ON THE BRINK"\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Diane and Rachel uncovering the harrowing stories of pregnant women who have medical emergencies in states with laws restricting abortions, and the doctors caught in the middle. It's streaming right now on Hulu. Rachel, thank you again tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE AND MOLDOVA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EUROPEAN UNION TO OPEN MEMBERSHIP TALKS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To the other news this evening, and to Ukraine. Tonight, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  agreeing to open negotiations for Ukraine to become a member.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This all comes as Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was sitting down for his annual televised question and answer session, making clear he thinks that western support for Ukraine is waning with aid being held up by Republicans in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2 . Putin saying, he's open to peace talks, but on Russia's terms, saying he's not willing to compromise, saying, quote, "Peace will come when we achieve our goals."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARKET RALLY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOW SURGES TO NEW RECORD\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Here at home tonight, and to the economy. Stocks reaching a record high for a second day in a row now after news interest rates may be coming down.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOW JONES, 37,248.35, UP, 158.11, 0.43%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Dow surging to another all-time high after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  signaled, they will lower interest rates three times in the coming year, meaning lower mortgage rates, credit card costs, and auto loan rates, among others.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here tonight, the doctor and wife from a reality show pleading not guilty, accused of using the identities of other cast members. Tonight, authorities on what they were allegedly doing. Also, this disturbing scene on the basketball court, the \uc2 NBA\uc2  star suspended indefinitely tonight. And the scene that halted commuter traffic in New Jersey straight into New York City. The bull on the loose running down the train tracks.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020231218ejce00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "IMPACT BY NIGHTLINE: ON THE BRINK" PART 2: HIDDEN MEDICAL CRISIS OF PREGNANT WOMEN IN ABORTION-RESTRICTED STATES\b0\par\par\uc2 899 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IMPEACHMENT VOTE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, HEALTHCARE, COOK, ANAYA, HUNTSBERGER, VERMA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CRISIS, HEALTHCARE, TIEN, SULLIVAN, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. We're here with ABC's senior congressional correspondent, Rachel Scott. This work that you and Diane and your team produced, this is an examination of the abortion debate I've not seen on network television before. In your two years working on this, was there anything in particular that surprised you?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The hidden reality of just how far-reaching the consequences are really surprised me on this one, Byron. You know, we talked to nearly 100 doctors. Some of us told us (sic) that the more these complete bans go into effect in their states, the harder it is for them to care for their patients. So, the reality is, is they're now leaving the state to practice elsewhere. And so, it really leaves this question, what is the complete and total impact this is gonna have on the healthcare system as a whole?\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, one of the doctors in your full report used the phrase, "unintended consequences of these new abortion laws." Politically speaking, are there any measurable pushbacks occurring now anywhere in the country?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There are some state lawmakers, ones who have supported these bans who have heard some of the stories from women like you just heard and they have said, "We are getting it wrong," that we need to make change, we need to amend these laws specifically to address the health of the woman. Well, they get a lot of push back from their Republican colleagues and these state capitols. And so, yes, things are starting to change, but it's certainly come with a lot of push back from other Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is one of those, those shows, that I, I think where, no matter where you sit on the debate, in families, right, where there's division, where people can sit down and watch this sober report, that was, that was just so powerful. And painful. Is there hope of any kind in this story to be found?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think the hope comes from awareness. And it's, particularly when you heard from the men in that piece where they just said, "I didn't know. I didn't know that this could happen. I had my own viewpoints and then, I learned that these could be the extreme consequences that could happen as a result of these laws and their minds changed. And so, I think the one thing here is the awareness, which is why stories like these are just so important. Because these are the hidden realities of living in a post-Roe America, Byron.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, there are a number of - cases in the court system now. One case in particular, Kate Cox, that we've reported on the air before, what, what more can you tell us about that case in Texas?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This was the first pregnant woman to sue any state in a state where abortion is completely banned. Her uterus was at risk of rupturing. She had been to the emergency room four times in the last month. She had a nonviable fetus with a very severe condition and so, she was asking the state a simple question. "Can I please get an emergency abortion?" Well, went all the way up to the Texas Supreme Court and they ruled against her. One thing here, though, that is very notable is that advocates believe that the floodgates will now open and we may start to see more and more cases just like that one.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have a sense of how exhausting this kind of work can be, when you and the team you work with pour your hearts into it. Is there something that in particular that will stick with Rachel Scott?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a good question. That's a Byron question. The stories of the women in that room. Hearing the emotion. It took so much courage for them to get there. There were women there that had never even been on a plane before that wanted to come in order for Americans, for people in this country, to know the reality of what they have lived, what they have endured, what they have suffered with. And so, the consequences, whether intended or not, are real for these families. And they will be living with this trauma for a very long time and their one hope, their one hope is that another family does not have to go through what they went through.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those of us who went to journalism school, we went through it for, for nights like this. Thank you so much to, to you, to Diane, to your remarkable team. Powerful reporting. We encourage you all to watch.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON THE BRINK: IMPACT BY NIGHTLINE HULU\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You can stream the full episode of "Impact by Nightline: On the Brink," now on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020231218ejce00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "IMPACT BY NIGHTLINE: ON THE BRINK" THE HIDDEN MEDICAL CRISIS OF PREGNANT WOMEN IN ABORTION-RESTRICTED STATES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IMPEACHMENT VOTE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, HEALTHCARE, COOK, ANAYA, HUNTSBERGER, VERMA\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us. Tonight, we're diving deep into a hidden medical crisis, pregnant women with dire complications in states with abortion restrictions. Our Diane Sawyer and Rachel Scott teamed up to report on women with high-risk pregnancies facing emergencies in the 21 states that have enforced their own laws since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Rachel joins us now with a preview of the special on Hulu. Rachel?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Byron, it really is a hidden medical crisis. We spoke to more than 100 doctors across 21 states. They say the laws are confusing and uninformed about high-risk pregnancies and that women are paying the price and that it is dire. So, the woman that you're about to hear from have come out of the the shadows to tell us their harrowing stories so that everyone in America can know the truth, asking us all, is this really what we want for pregnant women in this country?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We're there as they walk into a room in Texas. From Idaho, Florida, Alabama, South Carolina, Wisconsin, Arizona, Arkansas, Louisiana, Tennessee. They are strangers who have gathered together because they share a common bond.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BANS\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Suffering in the crossfire and confusion of new healthcare laws, laws in 21 states in this country with varying bans and restrictions. These women say they know there are thousands of others out there like them.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm wondering how many of you almost didn't come? How many of you felt you had to come?\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. For sure.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 I don't think people realize how common this is.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is this a debate over abortion? Or is it a debate over healthcare?\par \par \uc2 WOMEN (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Healthcare.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 It's a debate over human rights.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 That's what people need to understand is that these were wanted babies.\par \par \uc2 WOMEN (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Let's start with the story of this woman in the front row, Kristen Anaia. Like so many in this room, for months and months she'd been trying to get pregnant. Here's the moment she got the phone call of her dreams.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 Hello?\par \par \uc2 DR. MANSFIELD (OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 Hi, Kristen, it's Dr. Mansfield. Are you guys together?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN'S HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Hey, man.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 We are.\par \par \uc2 DR. MANSFIELD (OB/GYN)\par \par \uc2 Because we have really good news for you. Congratulations, you're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Four months of complete happiness before the day of complete heartbreak. The membranes protecting her fetus broke and her water, the amniotic fluid, lost. Doctors say this happened so early the fetus cannot be saved. With her fetus dying inside her, Kristen is now at risk. The clock begins ticking on infection and the terror of sepsis. In states where there are no bans, doctors would race to induce labor to protect her health, her fertility, her life.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 The doctors needed to start inducing me immediately, however, they were unable to because of Texas abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 And so the exact words were, "We have to prove to the ethics board and the medical board at this hospital that your life is in danger before we can intervene."\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She is shaking violently from fever, vomiting nonstop.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 She's literally dying inside of me as I lay on the bed going septic and in what world does that make any sense to prevent healthcare for me?\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 What about my life?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you were waiting, what, how many hours? Twenty, 22 hours?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 Over 24, almost 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, the boards approve her treatment. Her doctors induce labor. The baby passes. But Kristen is so sick from infection, she has to remain in the hospital for the next five days, getting IV antibiotics and blood transfusions. In the course of our reporting, we talked to nearly 100 doctors in those 21 states. The doctors told us about wading through the ever-changing contradictory state laws. To be clear, every state law does include an exemption to save the life of the mother. But doctors say, what about the mother's health? Her fertility?\par \par \uc2 DR. NISHA VERMA (OB-GYN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Because these exceptions are so confusing and it's unclear how sick someone has to be, we actually have to wait.\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN (OB-GYN MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE AZ FL KS)\par \par \uc2 Do we have to wait until they are septic and have uncontrollable infection? At the brink of organ failure before we intervene? That, that, that's not how we practice medicine.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMELIA HUNTSBERGER (OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 Is 25% chance of death, is that, is that enough? Does she have to have a 75% chance of death? Who gets to decide?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENALTIES FOR DOCTORS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOSS OF LICENSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FINES UP TO $100,000\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFE IN PRISON\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And take note of what the doctors are up against. If the state disagrees with their decision, the punishment could be extreme. Loss of medical license. In some states, fines up to $100,000. And even life in prison.\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTOPHER SULLIVAN (OB-GYN MATERNAL FETAL MEDICINE AZ)\par \par \uc2 You can't make rules up for the clinical scenarios that we deal with. It's too technical, there's, there's too many variables.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And here's what happened in Florida to Anya Cook and her husband, Derek. They were finally pregnant after years of infertility and miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 That's her little heart. Oh my God, a heart.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Anya was 15 weeks pregnant when the couple went out to dinner to celebrate their dream come true.\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 As we were walking out of the restaurant, I felt a gush of water being thrown at me. Never in my wildest dreams did I even think that that was my amniotic fluid that was leaking out of me. So, then we, we went to the hospital, which was about five minutes away.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Florida, abortion is banned after 15 weeks. So, where does this leave Anya, who was just a few days past the limit?\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 He told me in that very moment that, "You can get an infection, possibly sepsis and die."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DISCHARGE DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The doctor is discharging her from the emergency room. She has symptoms of pelvic pain and bleeding, but the hospital said her life was not in danger yet. So, she did not qualify for an emergency exception. She's sent home with antibiotics and an ominous feeling.\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 I had a hair appointment that next day and it popped in my mind and I said, you know what? I have to go to my hair appointment because, I hope my mom doesn't hear this part, because I didn't want my mom to have to worry about getting my locks done or having a mortuary take care of it. Because I knew I would die within the next two to three days. So, we went. I went to my hair salon.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And while you're there?\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 I sat up and something just told me I needed to go into the bathroom. And in that moment, Diane, I've never given birth. I didn't know exactly what was gonna happen. I've seen videos. But this was not that. I literally put my hands on my thighs and I just let out a really big sigh. And that's when I heard her. I heard my daughter hit the toilet. Oh, okay. (makes noise)\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Derek comes in, calls an ambulance. She's clearly hemorrhaging.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How much blood did you lose in the end?\par \par \uc2 DEREK COOK (ANYA'S HUSBAND)\par \par \uc2 That's half the blood in her body.\par \par \uc2 ANYA COOK (LOST FETUS)\par \par \uc2 Half the blood in my body.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Half the blood?\par \par \uc2 DEREK COOK (ANYA'S HUSBAND)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the stories we heard, it's not only women who have been impacted. Many of their husbands also felt an urgent need to be here.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 A surreal experience. The whole thing has been surreal, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 DEREK COOK (ANYA'S HUSBAND)\par \par \uc2 And for me, it's emotional because a lot of men step back. They don't think this is their issue.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can things change without the support of men?\par \par \uc2 MEN (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 DEREK COOK (ANYA'S HUSBAND)\par \par \uc2 We need to be, be, be together as a team.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We were.\par \par \uc2 DEREK COOK (ANYA'S HUSBAND)\par \par \uc2 Imagine if this continue (sic) to keep going and we don't step up as men, or if our women don't fight the way they, they are fighting right now. Imagine if everybody just swept this up under the carpet. It's gonna be a lot of casualties. A lot of women gonna (sic) die.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 There are so many women that this happens to that don't have the resources, that don't have the support, that are stigmatized and forced into silence.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 A lot of us feel like the protectors of the family. And then, now we have outside sources telling us how to be fathers.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's not only a girl's dream to have a child. It's also our dream.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know that this is such a polarizing issue.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 But why? Why? It shouldn't be. Some of us were already for abortion, some of us were against it. In my personal opinion, I was 100% against it. I did not believe even in rape. A 12-year-old could've got raped (sic), didn't believe it. Nope. Nope.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What made you change your mind?\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 My daughter. I had to go through such a traumatic trauma (sic) experience to change my view.\par \par \uc2 HUSBAND (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I am extremely grateful for those who have decided, "I had beliefs previously, I have new information now, I'm gonna choose to change my beliefs based on that new information." That, to me, is hopeful and I am hopeful that these men will stand as examples for other men to do the same.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just wow. I mean, first off, Rachel, to you, to Diane, to your team, I say this as a man, as a father, as a person of faith, thank you. Their stories, so powerful. Those women, so strong. So powerful.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah and it really is an incredible team behind this. Each of those women and their partners that came forward to tell us their stories had an extreme amount of courage. And they were mostly complete strangers. Now, they leave that room forever bonded by the trauma that they went through, Byron.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have plenty of questions, plenty to unpack and we'll do that when we come back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020231218ejce00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT HIGH STAKES BATTLES IN ARIZONA, TEXAS & KENTUCKY\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLDWIDE CALLS FOR CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 UN\uc2 , ISRAEL, GAZA, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , BIDEN, HUMANITARIAN, CIVILIANS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HOSTAGES, GAZA, UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, REPUBLICANS\par \par \uc2 MIDEAST, TENSION, WARSHIP, DRONE, MISSILES, HOUTHIS\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, STRIKES, RED SEA, ISRAEL, GAZA, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, IMPEACHMENT, BIDEN, MEASURE, REPUBLICANS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RIGHTS, COX, COURTS, DUNE, HEALTHCARE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We do expect that. All right, Selina, thank you. Now to the abortion battles playing out across the country. The new fallout after the Texas Supreme Court ruled against a woman who sought an emergency abortion there. As a new case now plays out in Kentucky. Rachel Scott is here with more. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH STAKES BATTLES IN ARIZONA, TEXAS & KENTUCKY\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Robin, good morning. From Arizona to Texas to Kentucky, several states now grappling with the question, what abortion restrictions should be in place and whether they do enough to protect the health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the legal fight over access to abortion rippling across the country. From Arizona where the state supreme court is hearing arguments on whether to enact a 19th Century law that would ban abortion entirely.\par \par \uc2 STATE OFFICIAL (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion in health care.\par \par \uc2 STATE OFFICIAL (MALE)\par \par \uc2 For over 100 years Arizona has fully protected life from the moment of conception.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To Texas where 31-year-old Kate Cox took her pregnancy complications to court, suing the state, asking for an emergency termination after finding out her fetus had a severe condition and little chance of survival.\par \par \uc2 KATE COX (SOUGHT AN ABORTION IN TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 There's no outcome here, you know, that results in us taking home a healthy baby girl. So it's hard. It's overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS NEW FALLOUT FROM TEXAS SUPREME COURT RULING\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas has a narrow exception to save the life of the mother. Cox was certain she would qualify. Her doctor said her uterus was at risk of rupturing and warned her future fertility could be in danger. But the Texas Supreme Court ruled against her stating some difficulties during pregnancy, even serious ones, "Do not pose the heightened risks to the mother, the exception encompasses." Her lawyer said she had no choice but to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY DUNE (ATTORNEY FOR KATE COX)\par \par \uc2 Patients can't be running to court every time they need urgent health care. It's really a human rights violation.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Within hours of Kate Cox filing that lawsuit, another emerged in Kentucky. A pregnant woman suing her home state for the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That woman in Kentucky not revealing her identity, but her lawyers say that she has learned that her pregnancy is no longer viable. They are still pushing to continue that case, expecting that more women will be in the same scenario, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Rachel, we know the President has weighed in on all this.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this is a blistering statement. President Biden saying legal and medical chaos is playing out in state likes Texas, Kentucky and Arizona, insisting that no woman should be forces to go to court or flee their home state to receive health care, and he's blasting the Republicans who supported these laws, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel, thank you. And you're gonna be back in our next hour with Diane Sawyer to share a preview of your report on the impact of abortion bans on thousands of families across the country. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcd0005r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIDDEN MEDICAL CRISIS IN STATES WITH ABORTION LAWS WOMEN WITH HIGH-RISK PREGNANCIES FACING EMERGENCY\b0\par\par\uc2 1,901 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLDWIDE CALLS FOR CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 UN\uc2 , ISRAEL, GAZA, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , BIDEN, HUMANITARIAN, CIVILIANS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HOSTAGES, GAZA, UKRAINE, ZELENSKYY, REPUBLICANS\par \par \uc2 MIDEAST, TENSION, WARSHIP, DRONE, MISSILES, HOUTHIS\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, STRIKES, RED SEA, ISRAEL, GAZA, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, IMPEACHMENT, BIDEN, MEASURE, REPUBLICANS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RIGHTS, COX, COURTS, DUNE, HEALTHCARE\par \par \uc2 FED, INTEREST RATES, STEADY, ECONOMY, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SHIPPING, DEADLINES, USPS, FEDEX, UPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIND CHILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "\uc2 NATIONAL LAMPOON\uc2 'S CHRISTMAS VACATION"\par \par \uc2 AIRLINES, INTERNATIONAL, STOWAWAY, PASSPORT, TICKET, FEINSTEIN\par \par \uc2 ADELSON, MARKEL, MURDER, JAILHOUSE, CALLS, SAUNDERS\par \par \uc2 BRAUGHER, "HOMICIDE", "BROOKLYN NINE-NINE", LEGACY, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 CHRISTMAS, RESIDENTIAL, LIGHTS, GAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARCTIC RECORD HEAT\par \par \uc2 GAZA, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , ISRAEL, CEASEFIRE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING CALLS FOR CEASE-FIRE IN GAZA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN: ISRAEL "STARTING TO LOSE" SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 PRIVACY, MEDICAL, RECORDS, PHARMACY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHARMACY CHAINS GIVING WARRANTLESS RECORDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEDICAL PRIVACY AT RISK?\par \par \uc2 TESLA, RECALL, AUTOPILOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE TESLA RECALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWO MILLION CARS AFFECTED OVER AUTOPILOT CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 IPHONE, APPLE, STOLEN, SECURITY, TOOL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: APPLE'S NEW SECURITY FEATURE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY ROLLING OUT TOOL TO PROTECT STOLEN PHONES\par \par \uc2 PREGNANCY, ABORTION, LAWS, NEW, ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIDDEN MEDICAL CRISIS IN STATES WITH ABORTION LAWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN WITH HIGH-RISK PREGNANCIES FACING EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Back now with our "GMA" cover story, pregnant women with dire complications in states with abortion restrictions. We're seeing it play out in Texas and Kentucky right now. And Diane Sawyer and Rachel Scott, they teamed up for an ABC News special report on a hidden medical crisis, pregnant women with high-risk pregnancies facing emergencies in 21 states that enforce their own law since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade. They both join us now and so, this is so necessary. And it's, it's an angle to the story that people have not approached until now.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I didn't know the depth of this, and I want to give credit to Rachel, first of all. For more than a year now, she's heard the cry of alarm from the doctors and from the patients about what is happening in this hidden crisis. And so, I asked her if I could team up with her, we could go out and try to find a number of women to come together and tell their stories, and they are harrowing.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The more time that we spent reporting on this, the more women that we found. And their stories are so stunning, so searing, it really begs essential question. Is this really what we want for pregnant women in this country?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We're there as they walk into a room in Texas from Idaho, Florida, Alabama, South Carolina, Wisconsin, Arizona, Arkansas, Louisiana, Tennessee. They are strangers who have gathered together because they share a common bond, suffering in the crossfire and confusion of new healthcare laws, laws in 21 states in this country with varying bans and restrictions. These women say they know there are thousands of others out there like them. It takes courage to go on camera.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm wondering how many of you almost didn't come. How many of you felt you had to come?\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. For sure.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 Without a doubt.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't think people realize how common this is.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is it the debate over abortion, or is it a debate over healthcare?\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Healthcare.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Human rights.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 That's what people need to understand is it's, these were wanted babies.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Let's start with the story of this woman in the front row, Kristen Inaya. Like so many in this room, for months and months, she had been trying to get pregnant. Here's the moment she got the phone call of her dreams.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (EXPERIENCED PREGNANCY EMERGENCY)\par \par \uc2 Hello?\par \par \uc2 DR. MANSFIELD (HEALTHCARE PROVIDER)\par \par \uc2 Hi, Kristen, it's Dr. Mansfield. (PH) Are you guys together?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (EXPERIENCED PREGNANCY EMERGENCY)\par \par \uc2 We are.\par \par \uc2 DR. MANSFIELD (HEALTHCARE PROVIDER)\par \par \uc2 Because we have really good news. Congratulations, you're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Four months of complete happiness before the day of complete heartbreak. All of a sudden, Kristen felt something wrong. The membrane protecting her fetus broke, and her water, the amniotic fluid, lost. Doctors say this happened so early, the fetus cannot be saved. With her fetus dying inside her, Kristen is now at risk. The clock begins ticking on infection and the terror of sepsis, infection that can rage through your bloodstream and cause organ failure. In states where there are no bans, doctors would race to induce labor to protect her health, her fertility, her life.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (EXPERIENCED PREGNANCY EMERGENCY)\par \par \uc2 Within hours, I was showing all signs of sepsis. The doctors needed to start inducing me immediately. However, they were unable to because of Texas abortion laws. And so, the exact words were, "We have to prove to the ethics board and the medical board of this hospital that your life is in danger before we can intervene."\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She is shaking violently from fever, vomiting nonstop.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (EXPERIENCED PREGNANCY EMERGENCY)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our doctor, nurses were crying at our bedside, my bedside, wishing they could do something with blank stares on their faces because they literally couldn't do anything until the bloodwork showed that I was dying.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hour by hour the doctors monitor the increase of the infection in her blood. She knows there's no chance for the baby.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (EXPERIENCED PREGNANCY EMERGENCY)\par \par \uc2 She's literally dying inside of me as I lay on the bed going septic. And in what world does that make any sense to prevent healthcare for me? What about my life? We didn't ask for that. We didn't want that.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you were waiting, what, how many hours?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN ANAYA (EXPERIENCED PREGNANCY EMERGENCY)\par \par \uc2 Almost 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Finally, the boards approve her treatment. Her doctors induce labor. The baby passes, but Kristen is so sick from infection, she has to remain in the hospital for the next five days, getting IV antibiotics and blood transfusions. A room full of women with similar stories of trying to get medical care, but state laws have tied their doctors' hands. The stories will break your heart.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Risk for blood clots, infections.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Every single one of my son's organs were growing outside of his body.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Torture.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 That is torture. That's monstrous.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Missing the skull.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Women and doctors sounding the alarm that something has to change.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMELIA HUNTSBERGER (OB-GYN)\par \par \uc2 Is 25 percent chance of death, is that, is that enough? Does she have to have a 75 percent chance of death? Who gets to decide?\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You're not fatal enough, or you're not sick enough. It's like, in what world are we living in right now?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Many of their husbands also felt the urgent need to be here, calling on men across the country to join in protecting pregnant women fighting to live.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can things change without the support of men?\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 PANEL MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Imagine if this continued to keep going and we don't step up as men? There's going to be a lot of casualties. A lot of women are going to die.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, good to see the husbands stepping up and wanting to be a part of this necessary discussion. And Diane, I believe you said that you spoke with like 100 doctors facing fines.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, not just fines, $100,000 fines, loss of their licenses and prison time, up to 99 years. And again, the doctors are in despair that they can't help patients and do what they know is the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And speaking with so many families and seeing just their emotions, how many families would you say that you have spoken with?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We reached out to 50 women, and we got all of those women in one room together. But I think it's important to note that for every woman that was seated there, there was one woman who told us that they couldn't come, that they were nervous about speaking out, but they privately shared that similar things happened to them, as well, that their own live, their health was at risk. And they felt like their doctors couldn't have this honest conversation with them. Some doctors even told us that they were even nervous to say the word abortion. So, it's created this chilling effect.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you're also going to see these women racing to get healthcare. And what it takes to get out of their states, and what they're going through even when they have a medical emergency, the miles they drive and the terror, the fresh hell to get the medical care in another state.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's very much in the headlines right now, the woman in Texas. I know you've been covering this woman, who had to leave the state.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The first pregnant woman to sue the state of Texas, to sue any state in this post-Roe environment that we're currently living in, it's really a test case for this type of healthcare, whether or not women can get access to it. Her uterus was at risk of rupturing. She had been to the emergency room four times in the last month. But still, the Texas Supreme Court decided to rule against her, saying that she did not meet the exceptions in that state to get an emergency abortion.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now a woman in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now, another woman in Kentucky. And so, advocates that we speak to say we're going to start seeing more and more cases like this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Diane, we are about to reach 2024, 2024.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this issue...\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this issue, and the women say we're here for the other women. We're here because we believe if Americans know this is happening that they'll ask for change.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And hearing, hearing from them, hearing from them.\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you said when we were watching the piece, they are so brave.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They really are. Thank you both so very, very much. And "Impact by Nightline: On the Brink," streams only on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . And that starts tomorrow. Look at you both. Thank you. Bless you both.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcd00066\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOOD MORNING AMERICA\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLDWIDE CALLS FOR CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning America. Worldwide calls for a cease fire and a warning from President Biden. Biden toughens his stance, warning Prime Minister Netanyahu that Israel is starting to lose the world's support because of its indiscriminate bombing, as Israeli forces start flooding \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnels below Gaza with sea water. Top national security official, John Kirby, joins us live. With more tension in the region, a US warship shoots down another drone.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REALITY OF ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The legal battles rippling through the nation, after a Texas woman was denied access to an emergency abortion. And this morning, inside the new reality across the country.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Is this a debate over abortion or is it a debate over healthcare?\par \par \uc2 WOMEN'S HEALTH CARE ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Healthcare.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Diane Sawyer and Rachel Scott with a powerful look at the hidden medical crisis facing women with high risk pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 WOMEN'S HEALTH CARE ADVOCATE (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Our doctor, our nurses were crying at our bedside, at my bedside wishing they could do something with blank stares on their faces, because they literally couldn't do anything until the blood work showed that I was dying.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Breaking news. Nations striking a historic deal. The major move to reduce fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAW PROFESSOR MURDER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 New details in the murder for hire of a law professor. The jail house phone calls between the former brother-in-law convicted in his killing and his ex-mother-in-law, now facing charges and accused of trying to flee the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILE HIGH MYSTERY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mile high mystery. As airlines gear up for record holiday travel, how one man flew from Europe to the US without a passport, Visa or airline ticket.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: APPLE'S NEW SECURITY FEATURE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  takes a bite out of iPhone theft. The new security feature that prevents criminals from breaking into your phone.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REMEMBERING ANDRE BRAUGHER\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Remembering Andre Braugher. The Emmy Award winning actor passing at age 61.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE BRAUGHER (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 And I know that every one of you gave me everything you had, and I will never forget it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 How his friends and co-stars are responding this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHT" CHRISTMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC: JONAS BROTHERS' BIG ANNOUNCEMENT\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Light Christmas. One family's record setting dazzling home display giving the Griswolds a run for their money. Plus, don't miss the Jonas Brothers with an exclusive announcement right here on "\uc2 GMA\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning America. Nice to see you on this Wednesday morning. We have a lot to get to this morning. With 12 days until Christmas, and today marks the first shipping deadline to get your presents under the tree in time.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A lot of work to be done today. Also ahead, the Fed is set to announce its next move in the fight against inflation. We're going to tell you what it could mean for mortgage rates, your credit card bills and more.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcd0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BITTER COLD BLAST MILLIONS WAKING UP TO WINTER-LIKE WIND CHILLS\b0\par\par\uc2 571 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, COX\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, SMITH\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, FUNDING, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY IN WASHINGTON TO APPEAL FOR \uc2 U.S. AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN LEADER SET TO MEET WITH PRESIDENT BIDEN & HOUSE SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 ABULHIYA, MILLER, WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , YADLIN, HOSTAGES, CUNIO\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, DEFAMATION, MOSS, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUDY GIULIANI SUED FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY TO DETERMINE DAMAGES FOR FALSELY ACCUSED GEORGIA ELECTION WORKERS\par \par \uc2 TEXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 AIR FORCE MEMBERS DISCIPLINED IN PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTIGATORS: MEMBERS OF SUSPECT'S UNIT FAILED TO TAKE "PROPER ACTION"\par \par \uc2 OHTANI, CONTRACT, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON OHTANI'S HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: NEWLY-MINTED DODGERS STAR TO DEFER $68 MILLION OF ANNUAL SALARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "BARBIE"\par \par \uc2 MEDINA, ROMERO, STABBING, ARREST, DUBROC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH SCHOOL CHEERLEADER MURDER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SEARCH FOR MOTIVE AFTER ARREST OF SUSPECT IN 16-YEAR-OLD'S STABBING\par \par \uc2 CORCORAN, MORTGAGE, REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REAL ESTATE REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARBARA CORCORAN ON THE STATE OF THE HOUSING MARKET\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS, NOMINATIONS, "BARBENHEIMER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BARBENHEIMER" BLITZ!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOX OFFICE PHENOMENONS RULE THE GOLDEN GLOBE NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, COX, LAW, EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, COLD, WINTER, CHILLS, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS WAKING UP TO WINTER-LIKE WIND CHILLS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are going to turn now to the big chill that is settling into parts of the country as the South cleans up after that tornado outbreak. Let's go to Sam, who's in for Ginger.\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, guys. So, we've got some cold air kind of scampering in after the big tornado mess. And remember, this was a EF-3 tornado, 150-mile-per-hour wind. But here's new information, 43 miles on the ground. Compare normal, maybe three to 15 miles on an average tornado on the ground. Lasted for an hour. Compare to normal, you think about tornados popping up and down, maybe five, 10 minutes on the ground. So an hour, you can see how just devastating that is. As that moisture moved east, it did have some very heavy\u160\'00\'A0rain\u160\'00\'A0but also a little bit of snow in Western Virginia, more than 6.5 inches of snow.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIND CHILL\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But that's it for a while because this next pocket of chilly air that has us with frost alerts, and even the wind chills, is coming in dry. So, you're looking at that, all the way down to Tallahassee and Jacksonville, with a wind chill in the 20s and 30s.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILLY TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we stay chilly here because there are several reinforcing pockets of cold air, buy they also come in dry. So, when you look at this, Washington, DC, 31 by Friday morning, New York City in the 30s. But look at Raleigh, in the 20s. And you think that's a little colder than normal, and it is because Raleigh would normally be waking up right around 35 degrees. So, settle in to the chill, but don't expect a whole lot of snow with this one. Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, grab a blanket. Thank you very much, Sam.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcc0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CARING FOR CANCER THRIVERS MEET SOME HEROES OF MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING\b0\par\par\uc2 2,499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, SMITH\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, FUNDING, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY IN WASHINGTON TO APPEAL FOR \uc2 U.S. AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN LEADER SET TO MEET WITH PRESIDENT BIDEN & HOUSE SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 ABULHIYA, MILLER, WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , YADLIN, HOSTAGES, CUNIO\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, DEFAMATION, MOSS, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUDY GIULIANI SUED FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY TO DETERMINE DAMAGES FOR FALSELY ACCUSED GEORGIA ELECTION WORKERS\par \par \uc2 TEXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 AIR FORCE MEMBERS DISCIPLINED IN PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTIGATORS: MEMBERS OF SUSPECT'S UNIT FAILED TO TAKE "PROPER ACTION"\par \par \uc2 OHTANI, CONTRACT, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON OHTANI'S HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: NEWLY-MINTED DODGERS STAR TO DEFER $68 MILLION OF ANNUAL SALARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "BARBIE"\par \par \uc2 MEDINA, ROMERO, STABBING, ARREST, DUBROC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH SCHOOL CHEERLEADER MURDER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SEARCH FOR MOTIVE AFTER ARREST OF SUSPECT IN 16-YEAR-OLD'S STABBING\par \par \uc2 CORCORAN, MORTGAGE, REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REAL ESTATE REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARBARA CORCORAN ON THE STATE OF THE HOUSING MARKET\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS, NOMINATIONS, "BARBENHEIMER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BARBENHEIMER" BLITZ!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOX OFFICE PHENOMENONS RULE THE GOLDEN GLOBE NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, \uc2 COX\uc2 , LAW, EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, COLD, WINTER, CHILLS, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS WAKING UP TO WINTER-LIKE WIND CHILLS\par \par \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2 , ANDROID, APP, LAWSUIT, ANTI-TRUST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2  LOSES APP STORE LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY SAYS TECH GIANT VIOLATED ANTITRUST LAWS\par \par \uc2 HEART ATTACK, WOMEN, SYMPTOMS, FLU, TANNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLY SYMPTOMS OR HEART ATTACK?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE MOM SHARES HER STORY & WARNING FOR OTHER WOMEN\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBES, ANISTON, WITHERSPOON, KEYS\par \par \uc2 "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL", GIVEAWAY, SEAHAWKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL" SUPERFAN SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFELONG SEAHAWKS FAN GETS FOOTBALL FAN GIFT\par \par \uc2 CHALAMET, LANE, "WONKA", MOVIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIMOTHEE CHALAMET & CALAH LANE LIVE\par \par \uc2 CANCER, HEALTH CARE, \uc2 MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CARING FOR CANCER THRIVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEET SOME HEROES OF MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here on "GMA." We call this controlled chaos going on here in the studio right now. We welcome you because we are joined by some of the healthcare heroes of \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2  (inaudible). Yes, and this morning we are excited to celebrate the care that they provide, all the hard work that they do to help their patients thrive, I said thrive, every step of the way. We're going to talk to a few of them in just a moment, but first, I want you to take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The team at \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2  work every day to care for cancer thrivers, the role even more critical as new research shows early onset cancer rates rising nearly 80 percent globally in three decades.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRACY GOSSELIN (MSK)\par \par \uc2 It's urgent in the sense that it's not what we would typically know to see.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Tracy Gosselin leads the over 5,000 nurses and support staff who make up MSK, one of the top cancer hospitals in the country.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRACY GOSSELIN (MSK)\par \par \uc2 Healthcare is hard work. The advent of technologies, new treatments, new ways of doing things make some of it easier yet some of it also harder.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As a dedicated nurse on the front line for 30 years, Tracy understands the gravity of the job. During her daily rounds, Tracy's known for her famed selfies and prioritizing personalized patient care.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRACY GOSSELIN (MSK)\par \par \uc2 Can they afford their medication? Can they afford co-pays? It's a lot about how does that funding look to do it better.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Jillian Allegretti, it's her role as a care coordinator that gives the patient chance to take control.\par \par \uc2 JILLIAN ALLEGRETTI (MSK)\par \par \uc2 I work alongside all the nurses, ensuring patients are scheduled for the right treatment, what's your day-to-day like so that I can work your appointments around your life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FROM THRIVERS TO CARETAKERS AT MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) What you don't know about Jillian is that she's a breast cancer thriver, treated at \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2  six years ago.\par \par \uc2 JILLIAN ALLEGRETTI (MSK)\par \par \uc2 I was 24. Cancer can go to anybody, at any time and at all times. I work in the exact infusion unit that I was given chemo.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's the staff, her now coworkers who stood by Jillian's side during her procedures and surprised proposal on the final day of chemo.\par \par \uc2 JILLIAN ALLEGRETTI (MSK)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Once, these were people that saved my life, and now, I get to work with these super humans that go out of their way to do everything for patients.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also on both sides, 27-year-old Joshua Morales.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA MORALES (MSK)\par \par \uc2 Hello. How are you doing, honey?\par \par \uc2 PATIENT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now a door attendant at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA MORALES (MSK)\par \par \uc2 I'm taking your mind off you being nervous about waiting in the waiting room, getting your blood drawn and the results of it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Joshua, only three years old when he was admitted to MSK as a high-risk leukemia patient, enduring multiple life-saving rounds of chemo and a bone marrow transplant.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA MORALES (MSK)\par \par \uc2 A lot of people see me, they say, "Hey, listen, this guy is, like, in good shape," but when I tell them I'm a patient, they never believe it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both Joshua and Jillian grateful for the compassionate care they received and that they're now\u160\'00\'A0able to give, helping others thrive.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA MORALES (MSK)\par \par \uc2 It helped me get through my situation, so I want to be the same way to support somebody else.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRACY GOSSELIN (MSK)\par \par \uc2 The why we do this every day is deeply personal. I think it's a higher-order calling of caring for humanity during a pretty tough time in someone's life.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wasn't that incredible?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amazing.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Here now Joshua, Jillian are joining us. It's so wonderful to hear about both of their journeys in that piece. And also joining us is Dr. Vickers. He is president and CEO of \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2 . You can clap for him, too. And \uc2 Kenneth Griffin\uc2 , the founder and CEO of \uc2 Citadel\uc2  and also head of the philanthropic group, Griffin Catalyst. I'm going to talk a little more about you in just a moment, Kenneth, but I just want to talk to these two right now, don't we both, Lara?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Joshua, we saw you as a three-year-old, there as a patient at MSK. Now you're there, you're greeting patients as they get out of their cars with a big smile. Why did you think this was the right role for you?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA MORALES (MSK)\par \par \uc2 It felt like the perfect full-circle moment, you know, being on both sides of the ball, patient and an employee now. It's cool to give care now and receive care when I was a child. So, the same feeling that they gave me when my, me and my parents walked through the door, and the warm welcomes and everything, is the same way I can give back, the same warm welcomes and ease people's mind when they come through the door, so...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Like you said, full circle.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Full circle. And Jillian, one of the nurses that treated you when you were going through your own fight, like the warrior that you are, is with us today. Tell us a little about how she inspired you to go full circle and now do that same work that they all did with you on your journey.\par \par \uc2 JILLIAN ALLEGRETTI (MSK)\par \par \uc2 After my diagnosis, it was always my dream career to work at \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2 . And seeing how all these nurses every day, all the coordinators, everybody that welcomes you, I just really wanted to be part of that team. And now that I'm on that team, I sometimes like to stand back and feel just really honored to work with these amazing people that saved my life.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you know firsthand, yeah, you know firsthand how pivotal their roles were in saving your life. And you get to do that now.\par \par \uc2 JILLIAN ALLEGRETTI (MSK)\par \par \uc2 Yes, and I'm very grateful, and it's very surreal sometimes that I, full circle, like he said, and I kind of get to give back.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's awesome.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LARGEST GIFT EVER FOR MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nurses in the position you're in, as well, so many unsung heroes, so many unsung heroes, and we're singing your praises right now. And somebody who's going to help us do that, I want you to know more about this gentleman right here. Kenneth is head of \uc2 Citadel\uc2  investment firm and Griffin Catalyst and has devoted his philanthropic efforts to going after solutions to largescale problems with the potential, the potential for life-changing impact. And he has said he wants to see cancer eradicated...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...believes in the importance of patient care. So guess what, everyone? Kenneth, bless you for that. Guess what, everyone? You're not just here to be recognized. You are here for so much more. We have a big surprise in store. Are you ready to hear the largest gift ever going to \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2 , the largest gift ever? Are you ready for that? Are you ready? Well, we, we're going to need your help. We're going to need your help. So, on the count, we'll show the amount, in three, two, one, hit it. I wish you could have seen some of the expressions. That's right, $400 million due to Kenneth's immense generosity. \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2  is receiving the ultimate gift of $400 million. What do you think about that, everybody?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Don't you cry, you're going to get me crying.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's unbelievable the difference that it will make in so many lives. Kenneth, why was this, I'm sorry, why was this so important for you to donate in such a large number that will make such a huge difference?\par \par \uc2 KENNETH GRIFFIN\uc2  (CITADEL)\par \par \uc2 So, so all of us here dream the day that we end cancer. And this gift is coming from both David Geffen and from me to MSK. And Dr. Vickers, your leadership at MSK and your team, just extraordinary. And I'm, I am certain that MSK will play an important role in ending cancer in our lifetime. But until that day, the team at MSK, the team that all of you are a part of, give the patients that are enduring cancer hope, dignity and compassion in their care. And we know cancer is more than just a disease. It's a moment of incredible emotional challenge. And all of the work that you do to help our, the patients at MSK beat cancer and find their lives again is God's work. Thank you so much. David and I are so honored to be in a position to support the great work of MSK.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I hope you feel the emotion from everybody that is here. There's nobody that has not been impacted both personally, with families and friends and things like that. It's impacted everybody. Dr. Vickers, just talk about $400 million. How is that going to help your team achieve what it is that you all want to achieve?\par \par \uc2 DR. SELWYN VICKERS (MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 As I was talking to Ken earlier, this gift is transformative. Not only is it the largest, but it means so much at this time. And so, I personally want to thank Ken for being here, and David, if he's watching, for allowing these resources to move toward what I think is a treasure in American healthcare. \uc2 Memorial Sloan Kettering\uc2  is a place that lives daily knowing that each person who comes through those doors, their life is threatened by a diagnosis, not just a chronic illness, but it's threatened very much, and we tell them each day, we have this, and we are going to allow you to thrive, as you said.\par \par \uc2 DR. SELWYN VICKERS (MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 So, this transformative gift will continually allow us to, first of all, take care of our people, which is first our patients. Secondly, our 20,000 Memorial individuals, our faculty and doctors, our nurses, our staff, our researchers, who all play this role in discovery. It will continually allow us to take the programs in this golden age of cancer care to transform outcomes in patients' lives at a place like Memorial because of resources like this.\par \par \uc2 DR. SELWYN VICKERS (MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 And thirdly, it will allow us to create facilities that are state of the art, to really create the patient experience that we know patients have along this journey, not only the outcomes that are really special at Memorial, but the experience of going through, knowing that we care about you as much as we do care about the outcomes. And so, Ken, David, this is a phenomenal gift. I couldn't have dreamed of something greater. And you hear from our staff, you hear from the lives that we've touched and that we will touch that this will immensely allow us to do it even more so. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, gentlemen, so much. Oh my goodness. Wow. And our crew doesn't have Kleenex for us? How can we do a segment like this, when they don't have any tissues for us?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The one time.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The one time (inaudible), Dr. Vickers, you all...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jillian, Joshua, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, thank you each and every one, and I, you both were speaking, just the pride, the pride in all of them right here means so much. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sam, I don't know if I can top this. I don't know...\par \par \uc2 SAM CHAMPION (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is nothing better than that, Robin, because if we make inroads here, it changes everybody's life.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcc0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL" SUPERFAN SURPRISE LIFELONG SEAHAWKS FAN GETS FOOTBALL FAN GIFT\b0\par\par\uc2 775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, COX\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, SMITH\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, FUNDING, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY IN WASHINGTON TO APPEAL FOR \uc2 U.S. AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN LEADER SET TO MEET WITH PRESIDENT BIDEN & HOUSE SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 ABULHIYA, MILLER, WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , YADLIN, HOSTAGES, CUNIO\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, DEFAMATION, MOSS, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUDY GIULIANI SUED FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY TO DETERMINE DAMAGES FOR FALSELY ACCUSED GEORGIA ELECTION WORKERS\par \par \uc2 TEXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 AIR FORCE MEMBERS DISCIPLINED IN PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTIGATORS: MEMBERS OF SUSPECT'S UNIT FAILED TO TAKE "PROPER ACTION"\par \par \uc2 OHTANI, CONTRACT, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON OHTANI'S HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: NEWLY-MINTED DODGERS STAR TO DEFER $68 MILLION OF ANNUAL SALARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "BARBIE"\par \par \uc2 MEDINA, ROMERO, STABBING, ARREST, DUBROC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH SCHOOL CHEERLEADER MURDER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SEARCH FOR MOTIVE AFTER ARREST OF SUSPECT IN 16-YEAR-OLD'S STABBING\par \par \uc2 CORCORAN, MORTGAGE, REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REAL ESTATE REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARBARA CORCORAN ON THE STATE OF THE HOUSING MARKET\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS, NOMINATIONS, "BARBENHEIMER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BARBENHEIMER" BLITZ!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOX OFFICE PHENOMENONS RULE THE GOLDEN GLOBE NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, COX, LAW, EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, COLD, WINTER, CHILLS, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS WAKING UP TO WINTER-LIKE WIND CHILLS\par \par \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2 , ANDROID, APP, LAWSUIT, ANTI-TRUST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2  LOSES APP STORE LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY SAYS TECH GIANT VIOLATED ANTITRUST LAWS\par \par \uc2 HEART ATTACK, WOMEN, SYMPTOMS, FLU, TANNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLY SYMPTOMS OR HEART ATTACK?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE MOM SHARES HER STORY & WARNING FOR OTHER WOMEN\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBES, ANISTON, WITHERSPOON, KEYS\par \par \uc2 "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL", GIVEAWAY, SEAHAWKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL" SUPERFAN SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFELONG SEAHAWKS FAN GETS FOOTBALL FAN GIFT\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to our "Monday Night Football" ticket giveaway. This morning, a lifelong \uc2 Seattle Seahawks\uc2  fan is getting surprised with tickets to the big game next week. Jess Sims was there for the special moment.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Touchdown.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You won't find a bigger fan of the \uc2 Seattle Seahawks\uc2  than Eamon Sylvester. Born and raised in the Emerald City, Eamon has been repping his team since childhood.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 We would go to games all the time, my family. Somebody always had tickets, and I've been blessed to be part of it to see their growth and their success as from an underdog franchise, you know, to now I feel like we're on the map, and people can never count us out.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 Hands up, hands up, hands up.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While living in Southern California, this superfan can be found deejaying during \uc2 Seahawks\uc2 ' games at his favorite bar.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 I'm in there, getting everybody hyped with the \uc2 Seahawks\uc2  chant, just going crazy, cheering, throwing Skittles, you know, playing music every time they score a touchdown.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we heard Blitz, the \uc2 Seahawks\uc2  mascot, made a special appearance.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 Let's go, let's go, let's go, Hawks, baby, Hawks. Blitz, Blitz, the actual Blitz came in. He deejayed with me. He got the crowd hyped up. It was incredible.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, have you been to a game yet this season?\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 No, it's tough. You know, tickets are hard to get.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But guess what? Because of your unwavering loyalty to the \uc2 Seahawks\uc2 , we are giving you four tickets to the Monday night game.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 What? Are you serious? This is crazy. Oh my God. I can't believe it. "Monday Night football"?\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But that's not all. You're also getting pregame sideline access to go on the field and see the players during the warm-up.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 Are you kidding me? What? What?\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm not kidding you at all.\par \par \uc2 EAMON SYLVESTER (\uc2 SEATTLE SEAHAWKS\uc2  SUPERFAN)\par \par \uc2 Oh my gosh, yes. Oh my, I'm shaking. This is crazy.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Enjoy the game. Have so much fun. And again, thank you so much for being such a great fan.\par \par \uc2 JESS SIMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," I'm Jess Sims, ABC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Love these surprises. \uc2 Seattle Seahawks\uc2  take on the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  on "Monday Night Football," 8:15 Eastern on \uc2 ESPN\uc2  and ABC.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcc0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\b0\par\par\uc2 510 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, SMITH\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, FUNDING, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY IN WASHINGTON TO APPEAL FOR \uc2 U.S. AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN LEADER SET TO MEET WITH PRESIDENT BIDEN & HOUSE SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 ABULHIYA, MILLER, WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , YADLIN, HOSTAGES, CUNIO\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, DEFAMATION, MOSS, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUDY GIULIANI SUED FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY TO DETERMINE DAMAGES FOR FALSELY ACCUSED GEORGIA ELECTION WORKERS\par \par \uc2 TEXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 AIR FORCE MEMBERS DISCIPLINED IN PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTIGATORS: MEMBERS OF SUSPECT'S UNIT FAILED TO TAKE "PROPER ACTION"\par \par \uc2 OHTANI, CONTRACT, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON OHTANI'S HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: NEWLY-MINTED DODGERS STAR TO DEFER $68 MILLION OF ANNUAL SALARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "BARBIE"\par \par \uc2 MEDINA, ROMERO, STABBING, ARREST, DUBROC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH SCHOOL CHEERLEADER MURDER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SEARCH FOR MOTIVE AFTER ARREST OF SUSPECT IN 16-YEAR-OLD'S STABBING\par \par \uc2 CORCORAN, MORTGAGE, REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REAL ESTATE REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARBARA CORCORAN ON THE STATE OF THE HOUSING MARKET\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS, NOMINATIONS, "BARBENHEIMER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BARBENHEIMER" BLITZ!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOX OFFICE PHENOMENONS RULE THE GOLDEN GLOBE NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, \uc2 COX\uc2 , LAW, EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First a look at the top stories breaking at 8:00, starting with the latest on the abortion battle in Texas. The state supreme court last night ruled against the pregnant woman who had sought a medical exception to the state's abortion ban. But her lawyer said she already left the state to get the procedure. Want to bring back Rachel Scott. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George, good morning. This has been legal whiplash. Attorneys for Kate Cox said it became clear if she wanted that emergency abortion, she had no other choice but to leave the state of Texas. And hours later, the Texas Supreme Court seemed to prove her right. \uc2 Cox\uc2  is more than 20 weeks pregnant, but doctors told her that her fetus has a severe condition and little chance of survival. Her doctors also warn that the longer that she continued to stay pregnant the more that her health and future fertility would be at risk. Texas, of course, bans abortions outright, with an exception to save the life of the mother, but the state supreme court ruled that some difficulties in pregnancy, even the serious ones, do not qualify.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate Cox' lawyers calling that decision unacceptable, saying all of this attorney saying all of this poses a very serious question for them. Who is making healthcare decisions on behalf of pregnant women in this country, judges and politicians or doctors? But we could see more legal challenges like this one emerge. Just days ago, a woman in Kentucky is now suing her state over the abortion ban there, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Many are watching these cases, as you know, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcc0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, COX\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, SMITH\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, FUNDING, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY IN WASHINGTON TO APPEAL FOR \uc2 U.S. AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN LEADER SET TO MEET WITH PRESIDENT BIDEN & HOUSE SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 ABULHIYA, MILLER, WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , YADLIN, HOSTAGES, CUNIO\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, DEFAMATION, MOSS, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUDY GIULIANI SUED FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY TO DETERMINE DAMAGES FOR FALSELY ACCUSED GEORGIA ELECTION WORKERS\par \par \uc2 TEXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 AIR FORCE MEMBERS DISCIPLINED IN PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTIGATORS: MEMBERS OF SUSPECT'S UNIT FAILED TO TAKE "PROPER ACTION"\par \par \uc2 OHTANI, CONTRACT, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON OHTANI'S HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: NEWLY-MINTED DODGERS STAR TO DEFER $68 MILLION OF ANNUAL SALARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "BARBIE"\par \par \uc2 MEDINA, ROMERO, STABBING, ARREST, DUBROC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH SCHOOL CHEERLEADER MURDER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SEARCH FOR MOTIVE AFTER ARREST OF SUSPECT IN 16-YEAR-OLD'S STABBING\par \par \uc2 CORCORAN, MORTGAGE, REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REAL ESTATE REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARBARA CORCORAN ON THE STATE OF THE HOUSING MARKET\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS, NOMINATIONS, "BARBENHEIMER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BARBENHEIMER" BLITZ!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOX OFFICE PHENOMENONS RULE THE GOLDEN GLOBE NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, COX, LAW, EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, COLD, WINTER, CHILLS, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS WAKING UP TO WINTER-LIKE WIND CHILLS\par \par \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2 , ANDROID, APP, LAWSUIT, ANTI-TRUST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2  LOSES APP STORE LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY SAYS TECH GIANT VIOLATED ANTITRUST LAWS\par \par \uc2 HEART ATTACK, WOMEN, SYMPTOMS, FLU, TANNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLY SYMPTOMS OR HEART ATTACK?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE MOM SHARES HER STORY & WARNING FOR OTHER WOMEN\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBES, ANISTON, WITHERSPOON, KEYS\par \par \uc2 "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL", GIVEAWAY, SEAHAWKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL" SUPERFAN SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFELONG SEAHAWKS FAN GETS FOOTBALL FAN GIFT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "GMA," live from Times Square.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcc0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE AS STATE SUPREME COURT DENIES EMERGENCY EXCEPTION\b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, COX\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But right now, we're going to turn to the abortion battle in Texas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE AS STATE SUPREME COURT DENIES EMERGENCY EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The state Supreme Court last night ruled against the pregnant woman who had sought an emergency medical exception to the state's abortion ban. But her lawyers said she already left the state to get the procedure. Rachel Scott has the latest for us. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Robin, good morning. Kate Cox is the Texas woman at the center of that unprecedented case, suing the state for an emergency abortion. But her attorney said it became clear that, if she wanted the procedure, she had no other choice but to leave the state of Texas to get it. And hours later, that state Supreme Court seem to prove her right.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Cox is more than 20 weeks pregnant, but doctors told her that her fetus has a severe condition and little chance of survival. Her doctors also warn the longer that she continued her pregnancy, the more her health and future fertility would be at risk. Texas, of course, bans abortions outright except to save the life of the mother. But the State Supreme Court ruled some difficulties in pregnancy, even serious ones, do not qualify.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate Cox's lawyer calling the decision unacceptable, saying that all of this poses a very serious question. Who is making health care decisions for pregnant women in this country? Judges and politicians or doctors? We could see more legal challenges just like this one emerged. Just days ago, a woman in Kentucky is suing her state over the abortion ban there, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Rachel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020240103ejcc0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET & CALAH LANE LIVE STARS TALK "WONKA"\b0\par\par\uc2 1,587 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, TEXAS, RULING, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, ELECTION INTERFERENCE, SMITH\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, FUNDING, UKRAINE, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY IN WASHINGTON TO APPEAL FOR \uc2 U.S. AID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN LEADER SET TO MEET WITH PRESIDENT BIDEN & HOUSE SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 ABULHIYA, MILLER, WAR, ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , YADLIN, HOSTAGES, CUNIO\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI, DEFAMATION, MOSS, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUDY GIULIANI SUED FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY TO DETERMINE DAMAGES FOR FALSELY ACCUSED GEORGIA ELECTION WORKERS\par \par \uc2 TEXEIRA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 15 AIR FORCE MEMBERS DISCIPLINED IN PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INVESTIGATORS: MEMBERS OF SUSPECT'S UNIT FAILED TO TAKE "PROPER ACTION"\par \par \uc2 OHTANI, CONTRACT, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DETAILS ON OHTANI'S HISTORIC DEAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: NEWLY-MINTED DODGERS STAR TO DEFER $68 MILLION OF ANNUAL SALARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "BARBIE"\par \par \uc2 MEDINA, ROMERO, STABBING, ARREST, DUBROC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH SCHOOL CHEERLEADER MURDER INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE SEARCH FOR MOTIVE AFTER ARREST OF SUSPECT IN 16-YEAR-OLD'S STABBING\par \par \uc2 CORCORAN, MORTGAGE, REAL ESTATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REAL ESTATE REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARBARA CORCORAN ON THE STATE OF THE HOUSING MARKET\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBE AWARDS, NOMINATIONS, "BARBENHEIMER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BARBENHEIMER" BLITZ!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOX OFFICE PHENOMENONS RULE THE GOLDEN GLOBE NOMINATIONS\par \par \uc2 NFL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, \uc2 COX\uc2 , LAW, EXCEPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN LEAVES STATE FOR PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, COLD, WINTER, CHILLS, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS WAKING UP TO WINTER-LIKE WIND CHILLS\par \par \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2 , ANDROID, APP, LAWSUIT, ANTI-TRUST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2  LOSES APP STORE LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JURY SAYS TECH GIANT VIOLATED ANTITRUST LAWS\par \par \uc2 HEART ATTACK, WOMEN, SYMPTOMS, FLU, TANNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLY SYMPTOMS OR HEART ATTACK?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE MOM SHARES HER STORY & WARNING FOR OTHER WOMEN\par \par \uc2 GOLDEN GLOBES, ANISTON, WITHERSPOON, KEYS\par \par \uc2 "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL", GIVEAWAY, SEAHAWKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MONDAY NIGHT FOOTBALL" SUPERFAN SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFELONG SEAHAWKS FAN GETS FOOTBALL FAN GIFT\par \par \uc2 CHALAMET, LANE, "WONKA", MOVIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIMOTHEE CHALAMET & CALAH LANE LIVE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're here for "Wonka" week on "GMA." Willy Wonka's origin story\u160\'00\'A0hits theaters on Friday. This morning, we have two of its stars with us in the studio, Timothy Chalamet, Calah Lane. Welcome, welcome back and welcome for the first time. And Timothy, congratulations. You've been nominated for a Golden Globe for your performance here.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, this is a prequel to the earlier movie. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Yes, this is different than the Gene Wilder Willy Wonka. We all grew up on that. I'm a huge fan of the original. But this is more of, like, an ambitious, joyous, optimistic, sensitive, young Willy Wonka, not the sort of crazed chocolatier (inaudible) in those versions.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I'm looking at all the chocolate here. I'm hungry just being here on set. And Calah, this is your first big role in a feature film. And you play Noodle, who is Willy's friend. What was it like working with this guy to your right?\par \par \uc2 CALAH LANE ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 So fun. I mean, I feel like we had so much fun on set, definitely. I feel like we did a lot. We had, we did a lot of playing around, in a very professional way.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CALAH LANE ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Yes. We did the "Thriller" dance, and he came over for Thanksgiving. That was super fun. Yeah, and I also loved watching him become Willy Wonka. It was super fun watching him, like, dancing and singing on set.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And did you, gid you give any advice?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Calah didn't need any of my advice. She's more of a natural than I am. She's more of a natural than I am at this. And she's got an amazing mother. Actually, Tracy's (PH) right there. And...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, mom.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 When you've got, when you've got an amazing mom on set, my mom's standing there, too.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love it.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Oh, we got a shot of the moms. When you have great moms in show business. That's, that's the secret ingredient.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, speaking of your mom, you've said this is her favorite movie that you've ever done.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 I doubt this was her favorite movie. You know, she called me yesterday. She corrected me. She said this was, she said "Beautiful Boy" was her favorite. Anyway, but this is, she, you did say when you saw this, she goes, "It's nice to see you smile."\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you get points for honesty on that answer.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Thank you, yeah, yeah, that's the truth.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's take a look at a clip.\par \par \uc2 MOVIE CLIP FROM "WONKA"\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Where do we begin, that's the question. I know, silver linings made of condensed thunderclouds and liquid sunlight.\par \par \uc2 CALAH LANE ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Did you always want to make chocolate?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 No. Back when I was your age, I wanted to be a magician. Here, try one.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You do seem so honestly excited about this movie.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's the easiest movie I've ever been in to talk about because it just makes you feel great. It's joyous. It's the perfect holiday film. It leaves you with a smile on your face, it really does.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's awesome.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How about the candy? What's your favorite?\par \par \uc2 CALAH LANE ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Oh, on set? Ooh, I like, I like the teacups, the teacup on the (inaudible) scene. That was super delicious. It was a cute little teacup, and it had, like, I think it was, like, blue, kind of like a turquoise color. It was super cute.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you have many takes where you got to eat it over and over again/\par \par \uc2 CALAH LANE ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Oh yes, yes, we did. That was fun.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Calah eats more chocolate than anyone in this movie, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But there was a lot of singing and dancing in this movie, and I hear that the director watched your old films from high school before they cast you.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, really cringe, awful high school videos.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I'm glad you don't like it because we have it right here. I'm joking.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We would do that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We would do that.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Was it hard for you to go back and watch stuff you did in high school?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Oh, you don't have it. Okay, even better. Yeah, to go back and watch that stuff?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, is it, do you look at it, and do you really cringe at the...?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 No, I mean, I see, you know, I was just having fun. I had no idea I would be able to act in big movies and the theater. I had no idea those video would haunt me, you know, and, you know, before the first movie I was in came out, I remember seeing my agent and saying, "Should I try to get this down?" And he was like, "It's all right," so, you know, yeah.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're so glad it wasn't available when we were coming up in this business, a different time.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 What would there be for your guys?\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh God.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of bad wrestling matches.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's talk to Calah. Let's move on Calah.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE CHALAMET ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Wrestling, wrestling.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to know from you, what was, you had a lot of dancing, right? You guys, we were just talking about that. So, what was your favorite scene? Can you describe it to us, for those, you know, none of us have seen it yet.\par \par \uc2 CALAH LANE ("WONKA")\par \par \uc2 Probably the dancing scene on top of the rooftop. That one was my favorite. We did, we did wires. And that's in the, the song "For a Moment," and we're on top of this, on top of the gallery (inaudible), I'm dancing with Timmy. That was, that was a really fun moment because we were on wires, and we were in front of blue screens, but there was another time when we actually flew down 30 from, like, feet high in the air.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amazing.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHEE C